window.COURSE_SEARCH = [{"number": 1, "title": "Data, prediction, and correlation", "url": "chapter-01.html", "text": "1.1 An observation is part of the model A table of customer records can support several different questions. We might predict the next purchase amount, identify groups of similar purchasing profiles, or estimate how a change in an offer affects spending. These tasks can use the same columns while requiring different models and different evidence. Before calculating anything, specify the observational unit, the information available at prediction time, and the quantity the analysis is intended to recover. An observational unit is the entity to which a row refers. A row might represent a customer, a customer-day, or a transaction. Those choices change which rows can reasonably be treated as independent. A feature is a recorded attribute of that unit. A response , also called a target, is the attribute a supervised prediction task attempts to predict. A database identifier is useful for joining records, but its numerical representation does not automatically make it a scientifically meaningful predictor. The variable taxonomy has two distinct dimensions. A quantitative variable has numerical values for which arithmetic has a subject-specific meaning. A count is quantitative and discrete: the number of transactions takes values in the nonnegative integers. A continuous measurement is modeled on an interval of the real line. That idealization includes irrational real numbers even though a computer stores a finite-precision approximation. A categorical variable records membership, such as store region or product type. Categories can be unordered, or ordered without having meaningful equal numerical spacings. Variable Mathematical object Permitted interpretation Number of purchases A nonnegative integer A difference of one means one additional purchase. Purchase amount A real-valued measurement in a currency Differences and ratios are meaningful under the stated currency convention. Store region encoded by an integer A label from a finite set Equality identifies a region; the distance between integer labels has no intrinsic meaning. Recoding three region names from $0,1,2$ to $0,2,1$ preserves the categories but can change their Pearson correlation with spending. The Python laboratory computes both correlations for the same observations. A method that treats the codes as measurements has introduced an ordering assumption. Later, indicator columns will represent categories while making that assumption visible. Data structure also matters. Cross-sectional data compare units at a designated time or sampling occasion. A time series records a quantity across an ordered sequence of times. Panel data , or longitudinal data, follow multiple units repeatedly. A child's measurements through time form one series; one year's measurements across children form a cross-section; the complete child-by-time collection forms a panel. A cross-section can still contain dependence between units. The name describes the sampling layout, not a proof of independence. A row-level random train/test split can therefore be misleading when the same customer appears repeatedly. A model may recognize a customer from the training rows rather than generalize to a new customer. Conversely, forecasting a future purchase requires respecting which information existed before that purchase. The task determines the appropriate split. This distinction will return when regression assumptions and model evaluation are developed. Source discussion: types of variables, 1:17 and cross-sectional and longitudinal data, 8:39 . The definitions here distinguish quantitative counts from qualitative categories and distinguish real-valued models from finite-precision storage. 1.2 Prediction requires an objective Suppose a feature vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$ is available before a numerical response $y\\in\\mathbb{R}$ is observed. Here $d$ is the number of numerical feature coordinates after any required encoding. A prediction rule is a function $f$ that maps the available features to a predicted response $f(\\mathbf{x})$. A learning procedure constructs that function from a training sample of paired features and responses. Supervised learning uses responses in the training examples to construct or assess a prediction rule. Regression predicts a numerical response; classification predicts a category or its conditional probabilities. These definitions do not require a causal connection. A response need not be a deterministic function of the predictors, and predictors need not be mutually independent. In particular, the traditional expression “independent variable” is not an assertion of probabilistic independence. A prediction is evaluated through a loss function , a numerical penalty for a discrepancy between prediction and observation. Under squared loss, $L(y,a)=(y-a)^2$, where $a$ is a proposed numerical prediction. If $Y$ is a random response with finite second moment and $\\mu(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid\\mathbf{x}]$ is its conditional mean, then conditioning on the features gives Proposition 1.1 — the squared-loss target. For every fixed prediction $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$, the conditional expected squared loss equals\n$$\\mathbb{E}[(Y-a)^2\\mid\\mathbf{x}]=\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid\\mathbf{x})+[\\mu(\\mathbf{x})-a]^2. \\tag{1.1}$$\nIt is minimized at $a=\\mu(\\mathbf{x})$. Write $Y-a=(Y-\\mu)+(\\mu-a)$, square, and take the conditional expectation. The cross term vanishes because $\\mathbb{E}[Y-\\mu\\mid\\mathbf{x}]=0$. The first remaining term is the conditional variance. The second is a nonnegative square whose unique minimum is zero. ∎ The first term is uncertainty that no function of these particular features removes under squared loss. The second term measures disagreement with the conditional mean. A finite training procedure does not know that mean: it must estimate a rule from limited observations and assess its behavior on new observations. The training loss is a calculation on data already used to choose the function. It is not automatically an estimate of its future predictive performance. Unsupervised learning studies structure without a designated response used as a prediction target. Principal component analysis chooses a low-dimensional representation according to a variance or reconstruction objective. Clustering chooses a partition according to a dissimilarity and a clustering objective. Neither task becomes assumption-free because a target is absent. Scaling, the distance function, the requested number of groups, and the population to which the results are applied remain modeling choices. These objectives explain why a single “best model” cannot be selected from a list of methods without stating a purpose. A direction that explains much feature variance need not predict a response. A cluster that has a small within-group distance need not correspond to a scientifically meaningful population. An association that predicts an outcome need not identify what an intervention would change. The course first studies representations and groups, then conditional response models, and finally a setting where causal interpretation needs additional assumptions. Source discussion: targets and supervised learning, 24:55 . The conditional-risk derivation is an authored mathematical extension. 1.3 Covariance measures paired variation A useful first comparison asks whether two numerical measurements move together around their respective means. Let $(x_i,y_i)$, for $i=1,\\ldots,n$, be $n$ paired observations with $n\\geq2$. The pairing is part of the information: $x_i$ and $y_i$ must describe the same unit or the same designated occasion. Define the means $\\bar{x}=n^{-1}\\sum_i x_i$ and $\\bar{y}=n^{-1}\\sum_i y_i$. The empirical covariance with divisor $n$ is $c_{xy}=n^{-1}\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar{x})(y_i-\\bar{y})$. Pairs above both means or below both means contribute positive products. Pairs lying on opposite sides contribute negative products. The result combines those contributions; a positive total does not require every pair to follow an increasing pattern. Expanding and using $\\sum_i x_i=n\\bar{x}$ and $\\sum_i y_i=n\\bar{y}$ gives $$c_{xy}=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i x_i y_i-\\bar{x}\\bar{y}. \\tag{1.2}$$ This identity is convenient for hand calculations. For floating-point computation, directly centering the observations usually avoids the subtraction of two unnecessarily large quantities when the variation is small relative to the means. With three or more features the same calculation produces a covariance matrix, which will govern principal component analysis. The source example uses $\\mathbf{x}=(1,3,9,11)$ and $\\mathbf{y}=(5,9,21,25)$. The paired product sum is $496$, and the means are $6$ and $15$. Equation (1.2) gives $496/4-6\\cdot15=34$. The corresponding empirical variances are $17$ and $68$. These are reproduced in the executable laboratory . The relation is exactly $y_i=2x_i+3$, so each centered response is twice its corresponding centered predictor. The response standard deviation doubles, and its variance is four times the predictor variance. The sample covariance used by many statistical functions instead divides the centered product sum by $n-1$. Denote it by $s_{xy}$. Then $s_{xy}=n c_{xy}/(n-1)$, which gives $45.333333$ in this example. Under identically distributed independent observations with finite second moments, this correction makes the estimator unbiased for the population covariance. It does not improve every possible estimation criterion, and it does not justify treating dependent rows as independent. A program must choose its convention explicitly before comparing outputs. To see the unbiasedness calculation, let the population means be $\\mu_x,\\mu_y$ and the population covariance be $\\sigma_{xy}$. Independence across rows implies $\\mathbb{E}[\\bar{x}\\bar{y}]=\\mu_x\\mu_y+\\sigma_{xy}/n$, while $\\mathbb{E}[n^{-1}\\sum_i x_i y_i]=\\mu_x\\mu_y+\\sigma_{xy}$. Subtracting gives $\\mathbb{E}[c_{xy}]=(n-1)\\sigma_{xy}/n$; multiplying by $n/(n-1)$ gives the stated result. Covariance has the product of the variables' units. Replacing purchase amounts measured in dollars by amounts measured in cents multiplies their covariance with a fixed second variable by the currency conversion factor. Consequently, its magnitude cannot be interpreted without scale. Dividing by the two standard deviations removes that unit dependence. Source discussion and exact numerical pairs: covariance, 7:28 and paired products, 9:58 . The source uses divisor $n$; Python sample-covariance defaults are not silently substituted. 1.4 Correlation is an angle between centered observations Assume neither observed variable is constant. Let $\\mathbf{x}_c=(x_i-\\bar{x})_{i=1}^n$ and $\\mathbf{y}_c=(y_i-\\bar{y})_{i=1}^n$ be the centered vectors. The Pearson sample correlation is $$r_{xy}=\\frac{\\mathbf{x}_c^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{y}_c}{\\|\\mathbf{x}_c\\|\\,\\|\\mathbf{y}_c\\|}=\\frac{s_{xy}}{s_x s_y}. \\tag{1.3}$$ Here $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is the Euclidean norm, and $s_x,s_y$ are the square roots of the sample variances computed with the same divisor as $s_{xy}$. Using divisor $n$ consistently in all three quantities gives exactly the same correlation because the factors cancel. A constant column makes the denominator zero. Its correlation is undefined, rather than a numerical zero. Proposition 1.2 — range and equality. For nonconstant real observations, $-1\\leq r_{xy}\\leq1$. Equality in absolute value holds exactly when there is a nonzero real $a$ such that $\\mathbf{y}_c=a\\mathbf{x}_c$, equivalently $y_i=a x_i+b$ for every observation and a fixed intercept $b$. Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\\mathbf{x}_c^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{y}_c|\\leq\\|\\mathbf{x}_c\\|\\|\\mathbf{y}_c\\|$. Dividing by the positive denominator proves the bound. Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz holds precisely when the nonzero centered vectors are linearly dependent. If $\\mathbf{y}_c=a\\mathbf{x}_c$, restoring means gives $b=\\bar{y}-a\\bar{x}$. The sign of the correlation is the sign of $a$. ∎ Equation (1.3) is the cosine of the angle between the two centered vectors. Correlation near zero means they are nearly orthogonal in observation space. It does not say that their scatterplot has no structure. The exact source relation $y_i=2x_i+3$ has $r_{xy}=34/\\sqrt{17\\cdot68}=1$. A negative slope of the same magnitude would give $-1$. More generally, for nonzero constants $a$ and $b$, replacing the two measurements by $a x_i+c$ and $b y_i+d$ changes the correlation to $\\operatorname{sign}(ab)r_{xy}$. Centering removes $c,d$; the numerator gains $ab$; the norm product gains $|ab|$. Thus changing positive units does not change correlation, but reversing an axis changes its sign. The laboratory checks this identity using the source data. The population definition follows the same geometry in a space of random variables. For real random variables $X,Y$ with finite, strictly positive variances, $\\rho=\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)/\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\operatorname{Var}(Y)}$. Cauchy–Schwarz for expectations gives the same bound. A finite-sample $r$ estimates a property of a sampling distribution only under an explicit model for how the pairs were collected. Computing the number does not establish that model. There is no universal threshold separating scientifically weak and strong correlations. Their practical consequences depend on measurement noise, sampling uncertainty, the task, and alternative predictors. A correlation can also be dominated by an influential point. Before fitting a line or interpreting a scalar summary, inspect the paired observations and how they were measured. Source discussion: Pearson correlation, 14:28 . The vector proof and affine-transformation result are derived here. 1.5 Zero correlation leaves nonlinear structure untouched The second source example has predictor values $-3,-2,-1,1,2,3$ and response $y=x^2$. The predictor mean is zero; the response mean is $28/6$. Each positive contribution $x^3$ to the paired product sum is canceled by the corresponding negative contribution. Hence $\\sum_i x_i y_i=0$, the covariance is zero, and the correlation is zero. Nevertheless the response is determined exactly by the predictor. The laboratory verifies zero squared reconstruction error for the quadratic rule and a positive error for a constant rule. Independence is the stronger distributional requirement that knowing one variable does not change the distribution of the other. With finite second moments, independence implies zero covariance because $\\mathbb{E}[XY]=\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[Y]$. The converse fails. For example, let $X$ be uniformly distributed on the six displayed predictor values and let $Y=X^2$. Symmetry gives zero covariance. But $\\Pr(Y=9\\mid X=3)=1$, whereas $\\Pr(Y=9)=1/3$. The conditional and marginal probabilities differ, proving dependence directly. A zero Pearson correlation therefore records the absence of a centered linear association as measured by that covariance. It neither excludes useful nonlinear prediction nor proves that the variables are causally unrelated. Even a perfect sample correlation does not establish causation: common causes, selection, and the observational design can generate associations. The regression-discontinuity chapter will identify a specific design and the additional assumptions that make one local causal interpretation possible. Correlation also discards information. Different data clouds can have the same means, variances, and correlation. It does not describe tail behavior, the direction of prediction, nonlinear conditional variation, or whether a relationship survives outside the observed range. These limitations motivate explicit response models and diagnostics. They also explain the next step in unsupervised learning: rather than summarize one pair of variables, principal component analysis studies the covariance geometry of all measured features together. Source counterexample: quadratic dependence with zero correlation, 18:44 . All displayed numerical values are reproduced by the laboratory. 1.6 Exercises with worked solutions Categories and arithmetic. A region is stored as an integer. Explain why replacing each region label by a different integer preserves its information but can change its correlation with purchase amount. State a representation that does not impose equally spaced region effects. Worked solution A one-to-one relabeling preserves which rows belong to the same category. Pearson correlation also uses distances from the numerical mean, which a general relabeling changes. The laboratory's two recodings preserve all group memberships but give correlations $0.979796$ and $0.489898$. An indicator column for each nonreference region represents unrestricted group offsets in a regression with an intercept. It does not require the offsets to follow the arbitrary integer codes. A sampling decision. A table contains monthly measurements for each customer. The deployment target is a previously unseen customer. Explain why a random split of rows can answer a different question, and propose a more relevant split. Worked solution Rows from the same customer can appear in both subsets. The fitted procedure then receives indirect information about a test customer's individual characteristics. Holding out complete customers instead estimates generalization to units not represented in training. If deployment concerns a future month for existing customers, a time-respecting split answers that different question. Neither split automatically eliminates dependence or population drift; it must match the specified use. Covariance convention. Recalculate the two covariance conventions for the source pairs $(1,5),(3,9),(9,21),(11,25)$. Explain why Pearson correlation is unchanged if the same convention is used consistently. Worked solution The centered product sum is $496-4\\cdot6\\cdot15=136$. Dividing by $4$ gives $34$; dividing by $3$ gives $136/3$. Changing the divisor multiplies the covariance by $4/3$ and each variance by $4/3$. Their standard-deviation product is therefore also multiplied by $4/3$, so the ratio remains one. Mixing conventions would fail to cancel and can even produce an apparent correlation outside its valid range. Affine invariance. Prove the transformation rule for the correlation between $aX+c$ and $bY+d$, assuming the original variances are finite and positive and $a,b\\ne0$. Explain the special case in which a measurement axis is reversed. Worked solution Covariance is multiplied by $ab$, while the two standard deviations are multiplied by $|a|$ and $|b|$. The new correlation is $ab/(|a||b|)$ times the original, or $\\operatorname{sign}(ab)\\rho$. If just one axis is reversed, the sign changes. Offsets cancel under centering. If either multiplier is zero, the transformed variable is constant and the correlation is undefined, so the formula's hypotheses are necessary. Nonlinear prediction. Use the six-point quadratic example to compare a constant predictor with the rule $f(x)=x^2$ under empirical mean squared error. Is the zero correlation evidence that prediction is impossible? Worked solution The best constant is the response mean, $14/3$. The empirical second moment of the response is $(1+16+81)/3=98/3$, so its variance is $98/3-(14/3)^2=98/9$, or $10.888889$. The quadratic predictor has zero error on all six points. Zero correlation does not exclude nonlinear prediction. The zero training error alone also does not establish performance on a new population with a different response mechanism. An objective determines a prediction. Given fixed features, show that the conditional mean minimizes expected squared loss. What part of that proof fails if the response has no finite second moment? Explain why a clustering method cannot be judged by this loss until a response prediction rule has been specified. Worked solution Expand $Y-a=(Y-\\mu)+(\\mu-a)$ as in Proposition 1.1. The cross term has zero conditional expectation, leaving the conditional variance plus a nonnegative square. If the second moment is not finite, the expected squared loss may be infinite for every finite $a$, so this decomposition is not a finite optimization argument. A clustering algorithm returns group memberships rather than numerical response predictions. An additional rule, such as a fitted training-group response mean, is needed before squared prediction loss can be evaluated. 1.7 Python laboratory and source boundary Run python labs/ch01.py from the downloaded course directory. The program reproduces the two source arithmetic examples, checks covariance conventions and correlation transformations, and demonstrates the effect of arbitrary category coding. The category example and risk derivation are original teaching constructions. No R code is demonstrated in the two assigned caption tracks; this statement is confined to those recordings and does not describe the rest of the course. The notes use corrected mathematical definitions rather than treating caption wording as an authority on the real-number system. The introductory historical retail anecdotes are not needed for the statistical argument and are omitted. Timestamped captions support the topic coverage and source arithmetic; they do not constitute a comprehensive review of every on-screen object."}, {"number": 2, "title": "Principal components: variance, geometry, and reconstruction", "url": "chapter-02.html", "text": "2.1 Compressing variation without selecting columns Suppose several measurements of the same observational units move together. Storing every coordinate may be inexpensive, yet estimating a model or interpreting a plot in all coordinates can be difficult. Principal component analysis (PCA) replaces the original coordinates by linear combinations ordered by their observed variance. Keeping only a subset produces a low-dimensional representation. The objective is precise: preserve as much squared variation as possible in an orthogonal linear subspace. PCA is feature extraction : the retained coordinates are usually combinations of many measured features. Feature selection instead retains a subset of the measured columns. PCA does not delete observations, and merely changing to all principal coordinates does not reduce dimension. Compression occurs when some of those coordinates are discarded. High dimension also does not imply that variables are highly correlated. When variation is spread across many directions, an accurate low-dimensional linear compression may not exist. Let $n>1$ observations have $d$ real-valued feature coordinates. After subtracting each feature's sample mean, put the centered rows in $\\mathbf{Z}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$. Its sample covariance matrix is $$\\mathbf{S}=\\frac{\\mathbf{Z}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{Z}}{n-1}. \\tag{2.1}$$ The diagonal entry $S_{jj}$ is feature $j$'s sample variance; the off-diagonal entry $S_{j\\ell}$ is the sample covariance between features $j$ and $\\ell$. The matrix is symmetric because transposing $\\mathbf{Z}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{Z}$ leaves it unchanged. It is positive semidefinite : for every vector $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$, $\\mathbf{a}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{a}=\\|\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{a}\\|^2/(n-1)\\geq0$. This property concerns quadratic forms and eigenvalues; individual covariance entries can be negative. The real symmetric spectral theorem supplies an orthonormal eigenbasis $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf{v}_d$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1\\geq\\cdots\\geq\\lambda_d\\geq0$. Each vector satisfies $\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{v}_j=\\lambda_j\\mathbf{v}_j$ and $\\mathbf{v}_j^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{v}_\\ell$ equals one for equal indices and zero otherwise. A direction $\\mathbf{v}_j$ lives in feature space. The corresponding vector $\\mathbf{t}_j=\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{v}_j$ contains one score for each observation and lives in observation space. Directions and scores have different dimensions and should not be described interchangeably. Source discussion: the PCA objective, 4:47 and covariance-matrix properties, 2:25 . The notation uses $n$ for observations and $d$ for features throughout these notes. 2.2 Why an eigenvector gives the largest variance For a unit vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$, the linear score vector $\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{v}$ has sample variance $\\mathbf{v}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{v}$. The unit-length constraint is essential. Without it, multiplying a direction by an arbitrarily large constant would make its score variance arbitrarily large without discovering any new structure. Proposition 2.1 — maximum projected variance. Among unit vectors, the largest sample variance is $\\lambda_1$, attained by a leading eigenvector. Restricting to unit vectors orthogonal to the first $j-1$ chosen eigenvectors gives maximum $\\lambda_j$. Expand a unit vector in the eigenbasis as $\\mathbf{v}=\\sum_{j=1}^d a_j\\mathbf{v}_j$, where $\\sum_j a_j^2=1$. Then $\\mathbf{v}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{v}=\\sum_j\\lambda_j a_j^2\\leq\\lambda_1\\sum_j a_j^2=\\lambda_1$. Choosing $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{v}_1$ attains the bound. Orthogonality to the first $j-1$ directions sets their coefficients to zero; the same argument on the remaining basis gives $\\lambda_j$. ∎ The hinge is that a unit vector's squared eigenbasis coefficients form nonnegative weights summing to one. The variance is a weighted average of the eigenvalues; its maximum is the largest available eigenvalue. The result requires only the observed covariance geometry. No normal distribution or probabilistic independence assumption is needed for this sample calculation. Let $\\mathbf{V}$ contain all eigenvectors as columns and let $\\mathbf{T}=\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{V}$ contain all score vectors. The covariance of these scores is $$\\frac{\\mathbf{T}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{T}}{n-1}=\\mathbf{V}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{V}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d). \\tag{2.2}$$ Thus distinct score columns are uncorrelated and their variances are ordered. Uncorrelated scores need not be independent: the distinction from Chapter 1 remains. Orthogonal directions are deterministic geometric objects, while zero sample covariance is a relation between their projected observation columns. The trace, defined as the sum of diagonal entries, gives $\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf{S})=\\sum_j\\lambda_j$. It is both the sum of original feature variances and the sum of all principal-score variances. An orthogonal change of coordinates preserves this total. Keeping the first $k$ coordinates preserves only $\\sum_{j=1}^k\\lambda_j$; discarded variance has not vanished from the original data. A direction and its negative are equally valid. Reversing $\\mathbf{v}_j$ reverses the associated scores without changing variance or reconstruction. If an eigenvalue is repeated, any orthonormal basis within its eigenspace is valid. Numerical comparisons should therefore compare projection matrices or reconstructed data when a basis is not uniquely identified. An apparent sign disagreement between two implementations is not automatically a computational error. Source derivation: orthogonal transformation and variance preservation, 11:04 . Proposition 2.1 supplies the maximum-variance argument explicitly. 2.3 The same solution minimizes reconstruction error Let $k\\leq d$ be a retained dimension, and let $\\mathbf{V}_k$ contain the first $k$ eigenvectors. The retained scores are $\\mathbf{T}_k=\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{V}_k$. Multiplying them back by $\\mathbf{V}_k^{\\mathsf T}$ reconstructs a centered data matrix $\\widehat{\\mathbf{Z}}=\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{V}_k\\mathbf{V}_k^{\\mathsf T}$. The matrix $\\mathbf{P}_k=\\mathbf{V}_k\\mathbf{V}_k^{\\mathsf T}$ projects each observation orthogonally onto the retained subspace. Proposition 2.2 — discarded variance is squared reconstruction error. Among orthogonal projections onto $k$-dimensional subspaces, the leading-eigenvector projection minimizes the squared Frobenius error, the sum of squared entrywise differences. Its error is\n$$\\|\\mathbf{Z}-\\widehat{\\mathbf{Z}}\\|_F^2=(n-1)\\sum_{j=k+1}^d\\lambda_j. \\tag{2.3}$$ For any matrix $\\mathbf{W}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times k}$ with orthonormal columns, orthogonal decomposition gives error $\\|\\mathbf{Z}\\|_F^2-\\|\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{W}\\|_F^2$. Dividing the retained term by $n-1$ gives $\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf{W}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{W})=\\sum_j\\lambda_j a_j$, where $a_j=\\|\\mathbf{W}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{v}_j\\|^2$. Projection geometry gives $0\\leq a_j\\leq1$ and $\\sum_j a_j=k$. Moving weight from a smaller eigenvalue to a larger available one cannot decrease this sum, so its maximum is $\\sum_{j=1}^k\\lambda_j$. The eigenvector projection attains that value. Subtracting from total squared variation $(n-1)\\sum_j\\lambda_j$ proves (2.3). ∎ The laboratory checks this identity on the source judge ratings. It obtains reconstruction error $36.39646810$ both by reconstructing the standardized observations and by summing the discarded eigenvalues and multiplying by $n-1$. Agreement checks the complete sequence of centering, scaling, eigenvectors, score normalization, and reconstruction. The explained-variance fraction of the first $k$ components is $\\sum_{j=1}^k\\lambda_j/\\sum_{j=1}^d\\lambda_j$, provided total variance is positive. A cumulative-variance threshold chooses the smallest $k$ reaching a designated fraction. A scree plot instead displays the ordered eigenvalues to look for a change in slope. Neither procedure proves that the discarded coordinates are irrelevant to a downstream response. For a counterexample, consider centered independent features with covariance $\\operatorname{diag}(100,1)$ and let the response be exactly the second feature. The leading PCA direction retains the first feature and most feature variance, but discards the entire response signal. PCA optimizes reconstruction of the features; supervised feature reduction needs an objective involving the response. If PCA becomes part of a prediction pipeline, its means, scales, and directions must be fitted within each training fold. Source discussion: choosing the retained dimension, 3:22 . The reconstruction proof and prediction counterexample are authored extensions that delimit the source motivation. 2.4 Scaling, loadings, and scores are separate choices Centering subtracts each column's mean. Standardizing additionally divides each centered column by its sample standard deviation. If $\\mathbf{D}$ is the diagonal matrix of positive sample standard deviations, the standardized matrix is $\\mathbf{Z}_s=\\mathbf{Z}\\mathbf{D}^{-1}$ and its covariance is the correlation matrix $\\mathbf{R}=\\mathbf{D}^{-1}\\mathbf{S}\\mathbf{D}^{-1}$. Zero-variance columns must be removed or handled separately because their scaling is undefined. Covariance PCA measures squared reconstruction error in the original numerical units. Correlation PCA measures it after each variable has unit sample variance. Standardization is appropriate when relative variation should receive comparable weight, but it is a modeling decision. It can amplify a low-variance noisy feature. A covariance eigenvalue threshold of one has no unit-invariant meaning. For a correlation matrix the mean eigenvalue is one because the trace is $d$; retaining eigenvalues exceeding one is then a heuristic comparison with the average standardized-variable variance, not a theorem identifying a true dimension. Software also uses the word loading for different quantities. For correlation PCA, let $\\mathbf{v}_j$ be a unit eigenvector and let $\\lambda_j>0$. The ordinary score column $\\mathbf{t}_j=\\mathbf{Z}_s\\mathbf{v}_j$ has variance $\\lambda_j$. Its correlation with feature $\\ell$ is $\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}v_{\\ell j}$. Define the loading matrix $\\mathbf{L}=\\mathbf{V}_k\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{\\lambda_1},\\ldots,\\sqrt{\\lambda_k})$. Its entries are these feature–component correlations. The standardized score columns are $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{T}_k\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1^{-1/2},\\ldots,\\lambda_k^{-1/2})$. Their sample covariance is the identity. When $\\mathbf{R}$ is invertible, the coefficient matrix producing these standardized scores is $\\mathbf{B}=\\mathbf{R}^{-1}\\mathbf{L}$, which equals $\\mathbf{V}_k\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_j^{-1/2})$. Thus eigenvectors, loading correlations, and standardized-score weights are three different matrices. Multiplying standardized observations directly by the loading matrix does not give the standardized scores. For an orthogonal rotation $\\mathbf{Q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times k}$, rotated loadings $\\mathbf{L}\\mathbf{Q}$ preserve $\\mathbf{L}\\mathbf{L}^{\\mathsf T}$. The standardized scores rotate correspondingly to $\\mathbf{F}\\mathbf{Q}$, and retain identity covariance. Rotating ordinary scores whose variances differ generally introduces off-diagonal covariance instead. A rotated component representation need not retain the ordered maximum-variance characterization of principal axes. Rotation is useful for interpretation, but should not be presented as another set of unrotated principal axes. The source's psych::principal output uses loading correlations and standardized regression-score weights. Its h2 value is the sum of squared retained loadings for a variable; with one component it is the squared loading, rather than the correlation itself. By contrast, stats::prcomp returns unit direction coefficients and ordinary component scores. Its default centers without standardizing. The Python conversion makes each convention explicit instead of comparing different output columns as if they were the same object. Conventions verified in the primary software documentation: psych principal and R prcomp . Source demonstration: loadings and communalities, 23:44 . 2.5 Reproducing the source demonstrations in Python The canonical USJudgeRatings dataset contains observations for 43 judges and 12 numerical columns. The first numerical column, CONT , records lawyer–judge contacts. Judge names are row labels. Consequently, the source expression USJudgeRatings[,-1] removes CONT and leaves 11 rating columns; it does not remove a names column. The Python conversion reproduces that actual expression with a named column exclusion and retains all 12 columns in the bundled canonical data. The source covariance, correlation, and scatterplot compare DECI and DILG . Python reproduces their correlation as $0.95616608$. The 11-rating correlation matrix has leading eigenvalue $10.133417$, explaining fraction $0.92121977$ of its total standardized variance. The first integrity loading is $0.918422$, its communality is $0.843499$, and its standardized-score weight is $0.090633$. Their numerical differences illustrate why a printed loading is not a coefficient to substitute directly into the standardized-score calculation. Figure 2.1 connects the paired observations to the two multivariate eigenvalue comparisons. Figure 2.1. Python-rendered source scatter and scree examples. The judge ratings have one dominant direction; the Harman matrix has two eigenvalues above the displayed null comparison. The null curves use explicitly seeded Python simulations, not the recording's unknown random stream. psych::fa.parallel(...,fa=\"pc\",n.iter=100) compares observed PCA eigenvalues with random-data eigenvalues. The converted implementation runs 100 Gaussian-null matrices with the same numbers of rows and columns. When raw data exist, it also resamples each column independently with replacement, preserving marginal empirical distributions while removing systematic cross-column pairing. It reports the componentwise 95th percentiles. The random-number stream is explicitly seeded in Python and is not R's stream, so a finite simulated curve need not match the recording point for point. The observed eigenvalues and the underlying null procedures can still be compared directly. The Harman23.cor dataset contains an eight-variable correlation matrix rather than individual measurements. Its leading eigenvalues are $4.672880$ and $1.770983$. Dividing by the full trace of eight gives variance fractions $0.584110$ and $0.221373$, totaling $0.80548281$. Dividing those two eigenvalues by their retained sum instead gives approximately $0.725167$ and $0.274833$. Those latter fractions sum to one because their denominator excludes discarded components. They do not mean that two components recover all original variance. Selected source-code frames show n.obs=302 in the Harman parallel-analysis call, while the canonical dataset documents 305 observations. The laboratory executes the source setting and the documented setting separately. This affects the random-null comparison, not the eigendecomposition of the supplied matrix. Its two-component extraction and Kaiser-normalized varimax rotation reproduce the source methods, while recording explicit normalization and convergence tolerance. The reconstructed matrix and variable communalities remain unchanged under the orthogonal rotation. Scores require additional care. The source's judge-score extraction can be converted because the 43 rows are available; the first six Python standardized scores agree with the displayed source values to their printed precision. The source's Harman score-weight matrix can also be computed from its correlation matrix. Individual Harman observation scores cannot be recovered: many different individual-data matrices have the same covariance. The missing object is the original observation matrix, not an unavailable Python algorithm. The laboratory supplies weights and states that boundary rather than inventing 305 rows. The final prcomp example uses the unstandardized DECI,DILG pair in that order. NumPy's centered singular value decomposition reproduces its eigenvalues $1.424834$ and $0.031489$, whose variance fractions are $0.978377$ and $0.021623$. Scores may differ by an entire-column sign convention, which does not change the fit. All displayed numeric results are generated by the Python laboratory ; its data and provenance are included for offline use. Source examples: judge covariance, correlation, and plot, 15:07 ; judge parallel analysis and principal extraction, 17:44 ; Harman example, 25:34 ; scores, 31:33 ; prcomp, 32:40 . The conversion follows the primary parallel-analysis implementation for the null constructions, without claiming identical Monte Carlo draws. 2.6 Exercises with worked solutions Rotation or compression? Explain why keeping every principal score does not reduce dimension or lose variation. What changes when only the first $k<d$ scores are retained? Worked solution The full eigenvector matrix is orthogonal and invertible, so $\\mathbf{Z}=\\mathbf{T}\\mathbf{V}^{\\mathsf T}$. It is a coordinate change. Truncation uses a rank-$k$ projection, so scores in omitted directions cannot generally be reconstructed. Equation (2.3) measures the lost squared variation. If all omitted eigenvalues vanish, the original observations already lie in the retained subspace and the reconstruction remains exact. Variance and prediction. Explain why keeping most input variance is insufficient to guarantee good prediction of a response. Give an explicit two-feature construction. Worked solution Take independent centered features of variances $100$ and $1$, and let the response equal the second feature. The leading PCA direction retains the first coordinate because it maximizes feature variance. Independence means that coordinate provides no information about the response; the omitted coordinate would predict it exactly. A predictor needs a response-sensitive criterion or validation of the entire PCA-plus-model procedure. No variance fraction alone supplies that guarantee. Loading versus score coefficient. For an eigenpair $(\\lambda,\\mathbf{v})$ of a correlation matrix with $\\lambda>0$, derive the feature correlations of the ordinary score and the coefficients for a unit-variance score. Worked solution The ordinary score is $\\mathbf{t}=\\mathbf{Z}_s\\mathbf{v}$, whose variance is $\\lambda$. Feature $\\ell$ has covariance $(\\mathbf{R}\\mathbf{v})_\\ell=\\lambda v_\\ell$ with it, so their correlation is $\\sqrt{\\lambda}v_\\ell$. Dividing the score by $\\sqrt{\\lambda}$ gives coefficients $\\mathbf{v}/\\sqrt{\\lambda}$. These differ from loading correlations $\\sqrt{\\lambda}\\mathbf{v}$ by a factor of $\\lambda$. The covariance identity follows directly from $\\mathbf{R}\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda\\mathbf{v}$. A misleading percentage. The first two Harman eigenvalues sum to $6.443862$ to displayed precision. Explain why percentages normalized within that pair add to one, while the fraction of all standardized variance is smaller. Reproduce both calculations in Python. Worked solution The within-pair denominator is the same retained sum, so dividing each member by it necessarily gives fractions summing to one. The full covariance trace is eight; dividing the retained sum by eight gives approximately $0.80548281$. The laboratory uses unrounded eigenvalues. It prints within-pair fractions approximately $0.725167$ and $0.274833$, and original-variance fractions approximately $0.584110$ and $0.221373$. The remaining eigenvalues account for the omitted variation. Verify a complete reconstruction. For the standardized 11-rating data, compute the rank-one reconstruction in two ways: ordinary scores times unit directions, and standardized scores times loading correlations. Compare its squared error with the discarded-eigenvalue formula. Worked solution The ordinary representation is $(\\mathbf{Z}_s\\mathbf{v}_1)\\mathbf{v}_1^{\\mathsf T}$. The standardized representation is $(\\mathbf{Z}_s\\mathbf{v}_1/\\sqrt{\\lambda_1})(\\sqrt{\\lambda_1}\\mathbf{v}_1)^{\\mathsf T}$, which is identical because the scale factors cancel. The laboratory forms the latter through the correlation-system weights, checks its identity with the former, and obtains error $36.39646810$. The formula $(43-1)\\sum_{j>1}\\lambda_j$ gives the same result. Comparing score columns without accounting for normalization would fail this check. What covariance cannot recover. Explain why the Harman correlation matrix is enough to calculate loading and score-weight matrices, but insufficient to list the score of each participant. Does replacing the sample size by 302 rather than 305 fix this? Worked solution The loading matrix depends on the correlation eigensystem, and regression-score weights solve a linear system involving that matrix. Individual scores multiply the unknown standardized observation rows by those weights. Covariance records pairwise second moments, not those rows. Different centered observation matrices can share the same Gram matrix and therefore the same correlation while giving different row-level scores. Changing the observation count only changes an assumed size; it does not recover the missing observations. It also changes the parallel-analysis null distribution and must not be used to conceal the documented sample-size discrepancy."}, {"number": 3, "title": "Factor analysis and the limits of latent explanations", "url": "chapter-03.html", "text": "3.1 Common variation is a model, not a discovered cause A group of bank-stock returns may move together while another group of energy-stock returns follows a different pattern. A compact representation can describe those shared movements. Calling two extracted coordinates “bank” and “energy” may help interpretation, but the names do not prove that two independently existing economic causes have been identified. Factor analysis makes a particular covariance model explicit; its fit and its interpretation are separate questions. For one observational unit, let $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$ be a real feature vector and let $\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{x}]$ be its mean. An orthogonal common-factor model writes $$\\mathbf{x}=\\boldsymbol\\mu+\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbf{f}+\\mathbf{u}. \\tag{3.1}$$ The vector $\\mathbf{f}\\in\\mathbb{R}^k$ contains $k<d$ latent common factors. The loading matrix $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times k}$ relates them to observed features. The vector $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$ contains feature-specific contributions. The model assumes $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{f}]=\\mathbf{0}$, $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{u}]=\\mathbf{0}$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{f})=\\mathbf{I}_k$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{u})=\\boldsymbol\\Psi$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{f},\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}$. The uniqueness matrix $\\boldsymbol\\Psi$ is diagonal with nonnegative entries $\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_d$. “Orthogonal” here means uncorrelated unit-variance factors. It does not require their statistical independence. A diagonal uniqueness matrix also means uncorrelated specific contributions, rather than independence without an additional distributional assumption. Centering observed columns does not prove any of these latent covariance restrictions. They are modeling assumptions that must be assessed through their observable implications and the scientific context. The model is not a partition of variables into disjoint factor groups. A measured feature can load on several common factors. Nor does the loading matrix guarantee that each resulting direction has a unique verbal interpretation. Exploratory factor analysis estimates a covariance representation with relatively few common coordinates. Confirmatory factor analysis adds specified restrictions from a proposed structure; that broader model-testing topic is outside the present course. Source discussion: observed variables as functions of latent factors, 12:44 and factor-model assumptions, 0:16 . The illustrative source claims about academic and athletic performance are not needed to establish the mathematics and are not treated as verified population facts. 3.2 The covariance decomposition and communalities Proposition 3.1 — observable covariance. Under the assumptions of (3.1), the feature covariance matrix is\n$$\\boldsymbol\\Sigma=\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{x})=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}+\\boldsymbol\\Psi. \\tag{3.2}$$\nThe feature–factor covariance is $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{f})=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda$. Substitute $\\mathbf{x}-\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbf{f}+\\mathbf{u}$ into $\\mathbb{E}[(\\mathbf{x}-\\boldsymbol\\mu)(\\mathbf{x}-\\boldsymbol\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}]$. Expanding gives $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{f}\\mathbf{f}^{\\mathsf T}]\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}$, two cross terms, and $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{u}\\mathbf{u}^{\\mathsf T}]$. Unit factor covariance gives the first term $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}$. Zero factor–specific covariance eliminates both cross terms, and the final term is $\\boldsymbol\\Psi$. Similarly, $\\mathbb{E}[(\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbf{f}+\\mathbf{u})\\mathbf{f}^{\\mathsf T}]=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda$. ∎ For feature $j$, the communality is $h_j^2=\\sum_{a=1}^k\\Lambda_{ja}^2$, its variance contributed by common factors. Its total variance is $\\Sigma_{jj}=h_j^2+\\psi_j$. If features have unit variance, the decomposition becomes $1=h_j^2+\\psi_j$. A loading equals a feature–factor covariance under this model. It equals a correlation only when that feature also has unit variance, since the factor already does. For different features $j$ and $\\ell$, the diagonal uniqueness assumption gives $\\Sigma_{j\\ell}=\\sum_a\\Lambda_{ja}\\Lambda_{\\ell a}$. Common factors explain the off-diagonal relationships. A positive uniqueness allows a variable to have substantial variation that is not shared with the others. If a fitted unit-variance model produces $h_j^2>1$, its implied uniqueness is negative. Such a Heywood case is not a permissible variance decomposition and requires investigation rather than literal interpretation. This distinguishes common-factor analysis from PCA. PCA forms linear combinations of observed variables and optimizes total projected variance or reconstruction error. A low-rank PCA loading product approximates the whole observed covariance, including its diagonal. A common-factor model additionally fits a diagonal uniqueness term, so the common component need not explain every marginal variance. Taking a full spectral square root of a covariance matrix with $d$ directions gives an exact product with zero uniqueness, but that construction alone does not establish a low-dimensional common-factor explanation. A factor count must also respect identifiability. An unrestricted $d\\times k$ loading matrix contains $dk$ entries and the diagonal uniqueness adds $d$, but orthogonal rotations contribute $k(k-1)/2$ equivalent coordinates. A necessary parameter-count condition is $dk+d-k(k-1)/2\\leq d(d+1)/2$. This count is not a sufficient identifiability theorem. It does expose why attempting many factors from a small covariance matrix can leave insufficient independent information. Source derivation: the covariance expansion, 3:34 and communalities and specific variances, 9:39 . The parameter count is an explicit dimensional check, not a claim of unique factor identification. 3.3 Rotation changes interpretation without changing fitted common covariance Proposition 3.2 — rotational nonidentification. Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be any orthogonal $k\\times k$ matrix. Set $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_* =\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbf{Q}$ and $\\mathbf{f}_*=\\mathbf{Q}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{f}$. These replacements preserve the common term in (3.1), the identity factor covariance, and the covariance decomposition (3.2). Orthogonality gives $\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{Q}^{\\mathsf T}=\\mathbf{I}$. Therefore $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_*\\mathbf{f}_*=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\mathbf{f}$ and $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_*\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_*^{\\mathsf T}=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}$. The factor covariance becomes $\\mathbf{Q}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{I}\\mathbf{Q}=\\mathbf{I}$. The remaining zero means and cross covariances are preserved by the same linear transformations. ∎ Each row of the loading matrix retains its squared length under this rotation, so each communality stays fixed. Column sums of squared loadings may change. Thus the share assigned to a named factor can change even when the fitted common covariance is identical. A variable can move from a mixture of two factors toward a larger loading on one rotated factor, making a compact verbal description easier. Varimax is an orthogonal-rotation criterion that favors uneven squared loadings within columns: many relatively small loadings and a smaller set of relatively large ones. For a loading matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, its criterion is proportional to $\\sum_a[\\sum_j A_{ja}^4-d^{-1}(\\sum_j A_{ja}^2)^2]$. When Kaiser normalization is enabled, rows are first divided by their common-factor lengths for the rotation search, and restored afterward. The laboratory states that normalization explicitly and implements the corresponding singular-value-based orthogonal updates. An oblique representation permits correlated factors with covariance $\\boldsymbol\\Phi$ instead of the identity. Its common covariance is $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda\\boldsymbol\\Phi\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}$. Dropping $\\boldsymbol\\Phi$ while retaining oblique pattern loadings would change the model. Orthogonal and oblique conventions must therefore remain visible when translating software output. The source stock code explicitly requests rotate=\"none\" before rotate=\"varimax\" ; that first result is not a default oblimin fit. Factor scores add another layer of inference. Even if the population parameters were known, the factors are unobserved because specific variation remains. Among linear predictors $\\widehat{\\mathbf{f}}=\\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{x}-\\boldsymbol\\mu)$ with positive definite $\\boldsymbol\\Sigma$, expected squared prediction error is minimized at $\\mathbf{A}_*=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}\\boldsymbol\\Sigma^{-1}$. To see this, use $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{f})=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda$ and expand the quadratic expectation. Completing the square gives $$E\\|\\mathbf{f}-\\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{x}-\\boldsymbol\\mu)\\|^2=\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf{I}_k-\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}\\boldsymbol\\Sigma^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\Lambda)+\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left[(\\mathbf{A}-\\mathbf{A}_*)\\boldsymbol\\Sigma(\\mathbf{A}-\\mathbf{A}_*)^{\\mathsf T}\\right].$$ Positive definiteness makes the second term nonnegative, uniquely zero at $\\mathbf{A}=\\mathbf{A}_*$. The prediction-error covariance is $\\mathbf{I}_k-\\boldsymbol\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}\\boldsymbol\\Sigma^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\Lambda$. The matrix need not vanish. A calculated score is therefore an estimate of a latent coordinate, not a direct measurement of that coordinate. Source rotation argument and example: orthogonal invariance, 0:49 and stock-factor rotation, 12:20 . Software normalization is checked against the R varimax documentation . 3.4 Principal-axis extraction and the meaning of convergence The displayed R examples request fm=\"pa\" , meaning principal-axis extraction. This is not the default minimum-residual method, and it is not an instruction to compute ordinary principal components. For a correlation matrix $\\mathbf{R}$, principal-axis extraction replaces the diagonal ones by provisional communalities while retaining the observed off-diagonal correlations. An eigendecomposition of that reduced matrix then estimates its common component. One initialization uses squared multiple correlations. For invertible $\\mathbf{R}$, the initial communality for variable $j$ is $1-1/(\\mathbf{R}^{-1})_{jj}$. To interpret the expression, regress standardized variable $j$ linearly on the other standardized variables. The block-inverse identity gives residual variance $1/(\\mathbf{R}^{-1})_{jj}$; subtracting from its unit total variance gives the variance fraction explained by that auxiliary regression. At iteration $m$, let $\\mathbf{R}^{(m)}$ have the observed off-diagonal entries and current communality estimates on its diagonal. Retain its largest $k$ positive eigenvalues $\\eta_a$ and corresponding unit eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_a$. Form provisional loadings $\\Lambda_{ja}^{(m+1)}=v_{ja}\\sqrt{\\eta_a}$ and replace the diagonal with $\\sum_a(\\Lambda_{ja}^{(m+1)})^2$. Repeating the update seeks a fixed point of this diagonal-replacement construction. It does not supply a general guarantee that a statistically acceptable model will result. The source package stops this principal-axis iteration when the absolute change in total communality falls below $0.001$, or when its default limit of 50 iterations is reached. The Python conversion implements those same choices. It also records iteration history so the two stopping reasons remain distinguishable. A program returning a loading matrix after the limit is reached is not evidence of convergence. Subsequent rotation cannot repair a failed variance decomposition because it preserves communalities. Minimum-residual extraction instead optimizes a residual criterion, and Gaussian maximum-likelihood extraction uses a distributional likelihood. Their finite estimates, constraints, and numerical behavior differ. It is therefore inappropriate to replace a source principal-axis call with a library's default factor-analysis method solely because both functions return a loading matrix. The parallel-analysis calls in this recording do use a separate default one-factor minimum-residual calculation for their factor reference spectrum. The laboratory preserves that distinction. Normality is not needed for the covariance identity or for running the algebraic principal-axis update. Normal-theory likelihood inference does require its distributional assumptions; interpreting a non-Gaussian quasi-likelihood fit would need a separate justification. There is no universal ordering under which one of these methods must always have smaller residuals or lower computational cost. Those comparisons depend on the criterion, the matrix, constraints, and convergence settings. Displayed source commands and matrix inspected at 7:20 . Extraction and stopping conventions checked in the primary psych implementation ; the Python minimum-residual interface is documented by factor_analyzer . 3.5 Two converted examples, with a failed-fit warning preserved The first displayed program uses ability.cov , a six-test covariance summary from 112 individuals. Dividing each covariance by the product of its two marginal standard deviations converts it to a correlation matrix. Python then reproduces the source's two-factor principal-axis extraction. It reaches the stated tolerance after 22 iterations. Its communalities range from approximately $0.202182$ to $0.910786$, and all six uniqueness estimates are positive. That numerical admissibility is necessary for interpreting a variance decomposition; it does not establish that the factors are unique psychological entities. The stock example loads a CSV whose numerical part is already a five-by-five correlation matrix. Selected on-screen code and output supply all 25 entries, retained with the notebook data. The three bank variables are JPMorgan, Citibank, and Wells Fargo; the two energy variables are Shell and Exxon. The correlation between Shell and Exxon is $0.683$, while correlations between the two groups are smaller in this supplied matrix. Matrix recognition matters: treating these five rows as five independent original return observations and computing another correlation matrix would analyze a different object. The source stock parallel-analysis call omits its original observation count. The R package recognizes a correlation matrix and falls back to an assumed count of 100. Python reproduces that assumption explicitly. It cannot determine the number of underlying trading observations from this matrix. Consequently, this simulated-null illustration is not a validated sampling analysis of the original returns. The ability example, in contrast, supplies its documented count of 112. The displayed stock extraction reaches its default limit of 50 principal-axis iterations before satisfying the tolerance. The final total-communality change is approximately $0.00273909$, above $0.001$. Its Shell uniqueness is already near zero, approximately $0.004479$. Continuing the same update for 100 diagnostic iterations gives a negative Shell uniqueness, approximately $-0.199958$. Those additional iterations are an authored investigation of the failure, not a recommended factor fit. This warning is reproduced in a fresh R run as well as in Python. The source method is convertible; its numerical model has a convergence problem. Both the unrotated and varimax loadings at the source's finite stopping point agree between the two implementations, up to sign and column permutation. Interpretable bank and energy loading patterns do not erase that failure. Rotation preserves the common covariance and therefore cannot convert the inadmissible continuation into a valid variance model. Figure 3.1. Python conversion of the source factor examples. The left panel rotates the source's finite 50-iteration stock result; it must be read with its nonconvergence warning. The null spectra use 112 observations for ability and an explicitly assumed 100 for stocks. Negative tail eigenvalues belong to the reduced factor matrices, not to the original correlation matrices. The laboratory includes the covariance conversion, both PCA and one-factor minimum-residual spectra in the parallel analysis, principal-axis extraction, and Kaiser-normalized varimax. Fresh R 4.5.1 with psych 2.6.9 provides a separate implementation check. Loading and regression-weight arrays agree within $10^{-8}$ absolute tolerance after sign and permutation alignment; the one-factor minimum-residual spectra agree within $10^{-3}$, allowing for the different optimizers. These checks are recorded separately from the Monte Carlo null streams, which are not identical across languages. Neither summary matrix contains the individual observations needed to calculate each person's or each time point's factor score. Score-weight matrices are computable, but inventing individual rows would create a new example. All displayed source operations are converted in the Python laboratory ; this information limit applies to possible further score calculations, not to a failure to translate the shown factor calls. Source example: stock matrix and program, 5:24 . Fresh-reference evidence: R-to-Python array comparison . The full stock return series and its original sample count were not supplied in the recording. 3.6 What the model captures and what it misses The common-factor model captures a low-rank explanation of shared second moments plus feature-specific variance. It does not automatically capture nonlinear dependence, identify causal mechanisms, or validate verbal factor names. A fit should be examined through residual correlations, nonnegative uniquenesses, sensitivity to factor count and extraction method, convergence, and consistency with the measurement design. A plausible rotation cannot substitute for those checks. The source also discusses converting categorical features to indicator columns and weighting them by a category's frequency. Such a transformation is calculable, but its interpretation must be stated. For a binary indicator $B$ with probability $p$ of value one, the variance is $p(1-p)$ and its standard deviation is $\\sqrt{p(1-p)}$. Dividing a centered indicator by $\\sqrt{p}$, a weighting used in some categorical-data representations, is therefore not the same as ordinary unit-variance standardization. The two procedures optimize different geometries. All indicators for one categorical variable sum to one, so their centered columns sum to zero. The resulting covariance is singular if all categories are retained. Although numerical covariance calculations are still possible, a continuous Gaussian-factor likelihood is not justified merely by writing category membership as zeros and ones. A latent-variable model for categorical observations or a dedicated mixed-data representation requires its own assumptions. These notes preserve the source's computational idea while separating it from a claim that ordinary continuous-data factor analysis has become automatically appropriate. Factor analysis compresses relationships among variables. Clustering, developed next, assigns observations to groups under a dissimilarity. A group of variables sharing a factor is not the same object as a group of observations assigned a cluster. The distinction determines which rows or columns are being compared and which scientific question the method can answer. Source scope and mixed-variable discussion: 16:48 . The Bernoulli variance calculation and the singular-indicator warning specify the limits of that proposed preprocessing. 3.7 Exercises with worked solutions Centering is not a latent assumption. Does subtracting each observed feature's mean prove that the factors have identity covariance or that the specific contributions are uncorrelated with them? Worked solution No. Centering fixes observed first moments. Identity covariance and zero cross covariance concern second moments of unobserved components. For example, replacing a latent variable by twice itself changes its variance without changing its mean; the associated loading can be halved without changing the observed model. Factor scaling therefore needs an explicit convention. The zero cross-covariance restriction is another independent part of the model. A small covariance model. Let the rows of a two-factor loading matrix be $(0.8,0)$, $(0.6,0)$, and $(0,0.7)$, and let the diagonal uniquenesses be $0.36,0.64,0.51$. Calculate the implied covariance and identify which pair shares common variation. Worked solution The common covariance is the loading product. Its diagonal is $0.64,0.36,0.49$, and its only nonzero off-diagonal pair is $0.8\\cdot0.6=0.48$. Adding the specified uniquenesses makes every total variance one. The first and second observed variables share the first common factor. The third loads only on the second. This example does not imply that real data must have disjoint factor groups; nonzero cross-loadings would mix them. Rotation audit. Given an orthogonal matrix $\\mathbf{Q}$, prove that rotating a loading matrix preserves each communality. Explain why this also preserves a negative fitted uniqueness rather than fixing it. Worked solution For row $j$, its rotated squared length is $\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_j\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{Q}^{\\mathsf T}\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_j^{\\mathsf T}=\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_j\\boldsymbol\\Lambda_j^{\\mathsf T}$. The uniqueness is the observed variance minus that common variance, so it is unchanged as well. Orthogonal rotation changes coordinates within an existing common subspace. It does not change the model's diagonal residual or repair an inadmissible variance estimate. Scores from a summary. A covariance matrix and factor count are available, but the original participant rows are absent. Which operations can still be converted from R to Python, and which further request cannot be fulfilled exactly? Worked solution Covariance standardization, extraction, rotation, residual-correlation calculation, and score-weight estimation depend on summary moments and can be computed. Null simulations also need an explicit observation count. Listing the actual participant scores requires each original observation vector, so that further request cannot be satisfied by the summary alone. Simulating observations with the same covariance would create a different dataset and must not be labeled as recovered participants. Reproduce and diagnose. Run the source stock principal-axis update with its default 50-iteration limit, and then inspect 100 iterations at a tighter tolerance. Does agreement with a fresh R implementation establish a valid fitted factor model? Worked solution The default result has total-communality change approximately $0.00273909$, so it has not reached $0.001$. After 100 diagnostic iterations, the Shell uniqueness is approximately $-0.199958$. The Python and fresh R finite-iteration results agree, showing that the translation preserves the source algorithm. The negative continuation demonstrates why implementation agreement is a different claim from model admissibility or convergence. Increasing an iteration limit without checking the variance decomposition is insufficient. Indicator scaling. Derive the variance of a binary indicator with success probability $p$. Explain why dividing its centered values by $\\sqrt{p}$ does not generally give unit variance, and why keeping every centered category indicator creates linear dependence. Worked solution Because $B^2=B$, $\\operatorname{Var}(B)=\\mathbb{E}[B^2]-\\mathbb{E}[B]^2=p-p^2=p(1-p)$. After division by $\\sqrt{p}$, the variance is $1-p$, not one. Ordinary standardization divides by $\\sqrt{p(1-p)}$ when $0<p<1$. For mutually exclusive exhaustive categories, their indicators sum to one on every row. Their column means also sum to one, so subtracting those means makes the centered columns sum to zero. That is an exact linear dependency, independent of software."}, {"number": 4, "title": "Clustering and hierarchical linkage", "url": "chapter-04.html", "text": "4.1 Similar observations depend on a chosen comparison A collection of customer profiles may contain observations that can be served by a common strategy. A clustering procedure attempts to express those similarities through groups. Its output depends on what is measured, which differences matter, and how a group is defined. An algorithm can divide an undifferentiated cloud into groups when asked; the existence of a returned partition is not proof that the population contains distinct natural classes. Let $\\mathbf{x}_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_n$ be $n$ observations with $d$ feature coordinates. A partition into $k$ clusters is a collection $C_1,\\ldots,C_k$ of nonempty sets of observation indices whose union is $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ and whose pairwise intersections are empty. Every observation belongs to exactly one cluster in this hard-partition setting. A label such as cluster one is an arbitrary identifier. Two programs can return identical partitions while numbering them differently. PCA and factor analysis study relationships among feature coordinates; clustering usually groups observation rows. Reducing a table to representative cluster summaries may compress its description, but assigning labels does not by itself reduce the number of feature dimensions. Nor does clustering guarantee lower total computational cost. In particular, constructing all pairwise dissimilarities already requires a quadratic number of stored values in the number of observations. A dissimilarity $\\delta(i,j)$ is a numerical comparison in which a smaller value means the two observations are more alike for the chosen task. It should at least have a stated interpretation. A metric is a more restrictive function on feature vectors: it is nonnegative, symmetric, zero exactly for identical vectors, and satisfies the triangle inequality $\\delta(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{z})\\leq\\delta(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})+\\delta(\\mathbf{y},\\mathbf{z})$. Distinct observations can nevertheless have zero metric distance if their recorded feature vectors are identical. The triangle inequality is a property of a comparison, not a claim that every real route is a straight line in a geographical drawing. Some clustering procedures can operate on dissimilarities that are not metrics. That flexibility does not entitle us to use metric geometry to interpret their results. A definition such as one minus an absolute correlation needs its own audit rather than acquiring metric properties by being called a distance. Source discussion: row grouping versus feature reduction, 9:14 and metric properties, 24:47 . Specific commercial and historical anecdotes are not required for the mathematical claims and are not asserted as verified deployment descriptions. 4.2 Numerical distances and the effect of units For real vectors $\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$, the Manhattan distance is $\\delta_1(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})=\\sum_{j=1}^d|x_j-y_j|$, while the Euclidean distance is $\\delta_2(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})=[\\sum_{j=1}^d(x_j-y_j)^2]^{1/2}$. Both are metrics on numerical feature vectors. Manhattan geometry adds coordinatewise travel, whereas Euclidean geometry combines orthogonal coordinate differences through a squared norm. They are not interchangeable simply because both preserve a notion of proximity. Proposition 4.1 — two metric triangle inequalities. For finite-dimensional real vectors, both $\\delta_1$ and $\\delta_2$ satisfy the triangle inequality. Write $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{z}=(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y})+(\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{z})=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$. The scalar absolute-value inequality gives $\\sum_j|a_j+b_j|\\leq\\sum_j|a_j|+\\sum_j|b_j|$, proving the Manhattan case. For the Euclidean case, expand $\\|\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\|^2=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|^2+2\\mathbf{a}^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{b}+\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|^2$. Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the middle term by $2\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|$, giving the square of $\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|+\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|$. Taking nonnegative square roots proves the result. ∎ The Minkowski distance of order $r\\geq1$ is $\\delta_r(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})=[\\sum_j|x_j-y_j|^r]^{1/r}$. Orders one and two give the two distances above. The condition $r\\geq1$ matters. At $r=1/2$, the expression between $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ is four, while the route through $(1,0)$ has total two. Thus the triangle inequality fails. The laboratory executes this counterexample; a formula that resembles a norm is not automatically a metric for every exponent. More generally, for $r>1$, Hölder's inequality with conjugate exponent $r/(r-1)$ gives $\\sum_j|a_j+b_j|^r\\leq(\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|_r+\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|_r)\\|\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\|_r^{r-1}$. Dividing by the last factor when it is positive gives Minkowski's triangle inequality; if it is zero, the inequality is immediate. This explicitly identifies the inequality used to extend the two elementary proofs. Changing a feature's units changes these distances. If numerical feature $j$ is multiplied by $c_j$, its contribution to squared Euclidean distance is multiplied by $c_j^2$. Standardizing by a positive sample standard deviation $s_j$ therefore chooses coordinate weight $1/s_j^2$. That is a substantive decision that gives a unit sample-standard-deviation difference the same distance contribution across features. It can be useful when original units are incomparable, but it can also increase the influence of a noisy small-variance measurement. Centering alone does not alter pairwise differences. A numerical scale change does. On the other hand, standardization fitted on a training population may produce different distances if recomputed on a different population. A reusable clustering model should retain its preprocessing parameters and describe how new observations are assigned. An agglomerative tree of a fixed dataset does not itself supply a unique out-of-sample assignment rule. Source definitions: Manhattan distance, 27:06 and Euclidean and Minkowski distances, 29:59 . All numerical comparisons in this chapter are reproduced by the laboratory . 4.3 Covariance geometry, profiles, and mixed features A covariance-aware comparison can account for correlated coordinate variation. Given a symmetric positive-definite matrix $\\mathbf{S}$, the Mahalanobis distance is $\\delta_M(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})=[(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y})^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf{S}^{-1}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y})]^{1/2}$. Let $\\mathbf{S}=\\mathbf{V}\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_j)\\mathbf{V}^{\\mathsf T}$ with every $\\lambda_j>0$, and define $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{V}\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_j^{-1/2})\\mathbf{V}^{\\mathsf T}$. Then $\\delta_M=\\|\\mathbf{W}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y})\\|$. It is Euclidean distance after an invertible whitening transformation, and therefore is a metric. Large-variance covariance directions receive smaller weights, and small-variance directions receive larger ones. The laboratory independently computes the quadratic form and the whitened-vector norm, obtaining $2.563480$ for its specified example. If $\\mathbf{S}$ is singular, the inverse in this definition does not exist. Replacing it by a pseudoinverse is a different convention and can assign zero distance to nonzero differences in the nullspace. Estimated covariance also introduces sensitivity to limited samples and outliers. For profiles whose shape across coordinates matters more than their levels, one can compare centered unit-length profile vectors by their correlation. The choice $1-|\\rho|$ treats opposite patterns as identical, assigning zero dissimilarity to a profile and its negative. The choice $1-\\rho$ preserves that sign distinction but need not satisfy the triangle inequality. The laboratory constructs three centered unit profiles for which the direct value is one and the sum through the third profile is $0.585786$. By contrast, $\\sqrt{2(1-\\rho)}$ is the Euclidean distance between these normalized profiles. Its interpretation still concerns standardized shape, not original levels. For binary presence information, let $A$ and $B$ be the sets of features present in two observations. Their Jaccard dissimilarity is $1-|A\\cap B|/|A\\cup B|$ when the union is nonempty. Joint absences do not contribute to the numerator or denominator. A convention must be specified for two empty sets; setting their distance to zero is a common implementation choice. Applying this definition to arbitrary integer category codes without a presence-set interpretation would change its meaning. A simple complete-data Gower dissimilarity combines numerical and nominal variables. For numerical feature $j$, divide $|x_j-y_j|$ by a declared positive range $R_j$. For a nominal feature, use zero for equal categories and one for different categories. Average the contributions using declared nonnegative feature weights. The laboratory's numerical contribution is one half and nominal contribution is one, so equal weighting gives $0.75$. This implementation deliberately states its complete-data and nonzero-range assumptions. Extensions for missing values, ordered categories, and asymmetric binary features must specify their conventions separately. Every comparison discards something: Euclidean distance depends on units, profile correlation discards levels, Jaccard ignores joint absences, and range-normalized mixed comparisons depend on how ranges and weights are chosen. These are properties to match to a task. An automatic scalar distance cannot decide which differences are scientifically meaningful. Source discussion: Mahalanobis distance, 6:14 and categorical and mixed-data distances, 7:12 . The profile counterexample and whitening verification are authored checks. 4.4 A point distance does not determine a cluster distance Once a cluster contains more than one observation, comparing two clusters needs another definition. For two nonempty disjoint index sets $A$ and $B$, single linkage is $\\delta_{\\mathrm{single}}(A,B)=\\min_{i\\in A,j\\in B}\\delta(i,j)$. Complete linkage is the corresponding maximum, and average linkage is $\\delta_{\\mathrm{average}}(A,B)=(|A||B|)^{-1}\\sum_{i\\in A,j\\in B}\\delta(i,j)$. Here $|A|$ denotes the number of observations in the cluster. These definitions answer different questions. Single linkage asks whether at least one close cross-cluster pair connects the groups. Complete linkage asks how far apart their most separated cross-cluster members are. Average linkage considers every cross-cluster pair. For $A=\\{(0,0),(0,2)\\}$ and $B=\\{(3,0),(3,2)\\}$, the laboratory obtains single linkage $3$, complete linkage $3.605551$, and average linkage $3.302776$. The centroids are $(0,1)$ and $(3,1)$, whose distance is $3$. Centroid distance is therefore another rule, not a synonym for average pairwise distance. Agglomerative hierarchical clustering starts with $n$ singleton clusters. At each step it merges a pair having the smallest current linkage dissimilarity, updates comparisons with the new cluster, and continues. Each step reduces the number of clusters by one, so a complete hierarchy has $n-1$ merges. Its partitions are nested: a cluster once formed is never split later. A divisive hierarchy starts with all observations together and splits groups, using a separately specified splitting rule. The linkage updates follow directly from their definitions. For single linkage, the distance from $A\\cup B$ to a third cluster $C$ is the smaller of the previous distances from $A$ and $B$ to $C$. Complete linkage takes the larger. For average linkage, counting all cross-pairs gives $$\\delta_{\\mathrm{average}}(A\\cup B,C)=\\frac{|A|\\delta_{\\mathrm{average}}(A,C)+|B|\\delta_{\\mathrm{average}}(B,C)}{|A|+|B|}. \\tag{4.1}$$ The factors of $|C|$ cancel because they occur in both numerator and denominator. Replacing the two weights by one half would instead weight previously formed clusters equally regardless of their sizes. That is a distinct clustering rule. Explicit recurrence formulas make these otherwise easy-to-miss software conventions visible. Single linkage has a useful threshold interpretation. Join pairs of observations whose dissimilarity is at most a threshold. Its connected components are the single-linkage groups at that threshold, with the chosen tie convention. A chain of short links can therefore join observations whose endpoints are far apart. This explains its sensitivity to bridges. Complete linkage constrains a newly formed cluster's largest internal pairwise dissimilarity through the merge height. Average linkage lies between the minimum and maximum cross-pair values but supplies neither the single-link connectivity interpretation nor the same diameter bound. The greedy merge sequence is not a proof that a cut with $k$ groups globally optimizes every possible $k$-cluster objective. Ties can yield more than one valid hierarchy. Reversing left and right children in a plotted tree also changes its leaf order without changing any cluster. Reproducibility therefore requires comparisons of memberships and heights under stated tie rules, rather than a demand that every dendrogram drawing look identical. Source definitions: single, complete, and average linkage, 9:13 ; centroid distance, 14:40 ; agglomerative and divisive hierarchies, 19:37 . 4.5 Exercises with worked solutions What is reduced? A clustering program replaces each row by a group label. Explain how this differs from retaining principal-component scores, and why a returned set of five groups is not evidence for five natural populations. Worked solution The group label records membership in a partition of observations. PCA scores are numerical coordinates obtained by projecting feature vectors into a linear subspace. Neither object can be interpreted as the other without an additional construction. A clustering procedure can return five nonempty groups because five was requested, even for a homogeneous distribution. Population claims require a specified model or external evidence and sensitivity analysis, not simply a successful algorithm call. Numerical units. Show that multiplying one numerical feature by $c$ multiplies its contribution to squared Euclidean distance by $c^2$. What does standardization choose as that feature's weight? Worked solution The coordinate difference becomes $c(x_j-y_j)$, whose square is $c^2(x_j-y_j)^2$. Dividing by sample standard deviation $s_j>0$ chooses weight $1/s_j^2$. Centering changes neither difference. Equal standardized weights therefore encode a relative-variation convention, not an empirically established equality of feature importance. A zero-variance column cannot be standardized by this formula. Cluster comparison. Reproduce the single, complete, average, and centroid comparisons for the two vertical point pairs in the text. Why can average linkage exceed centroid distance even though the clusters have equal sizes? Worked solution The four cross-distances are $3,\\sqrt{13},\\sqrt{13},3$. Their minimum is $3$, maximum $\\sqrt{13}$, and average $(3+\\sqrt{13})/2$, approximately $3.302776$. The centroid difference is $(3,0)$, so its norm is $3$. Averaging distances is not the same operation as taking the distance between averaged coordinates. The nonlinearity of the norm is responsible; equal sample counts do not make the operations commute. Average-linkage update. Derive (4.1) by dividing the pairwise sum between $A\\cup B$ and $C$ into two parts. Explain the defect in updating with an unweighted average when $|A|\\ne|B|$. Worked solution The two sums contain $|A||C|$ and $|B||C|$ pairs. Replace each sum by its average times its pair count, then divide their sum by $(|A|+|B|)|C|$. The factor $|C|$ cancels and leaves size weights. Giving both previous averages weight one half overweights the smaller cluster's members relative to the larger cluster's members. That is a different linkage definition, even though it is also sometimes called an average in informal descriptions. Audit a proposed metric. Use the laboratory's three centered unit profiles to test the triangle inequality for one minus correlation. Explain why one minus absolute correlation also fails metric separation on distinct signed profiles. Worked solution The first and third profiles are orthogonal; the middle profile lies midway in angle. One minus their direct correlation is one, while the two intermediate values sum to $0.585786$. The direct value exceeds that sum, violating the triangle inequality. A profile and its negative have absolute correlation one, so one minus absolute correlation is zero despite their different signed vectors. Both definitions can still be meaningful dissimilarities under particular goals; neither deserves all metric conclusions automatically. Missing information and mixed scales. For the two-feature Gower example, use a numerical range of 40, values 10 and 30, and unequal nominal categories. Calculate the equal-weight dissimilarity. What additional rule is needed if one nominal value is missing? Worked solution The numerical contribution is $|30-10|/40=1/2$. The nominal contribution is one, so their mean is $3/4$. With a missing nominal value, the complete-data formula is inapplicable. A common extension excludes unavailable comparisons and renormalizes the weights over available features, which would give one half here. That convention must be stated because pair-specific availability can change the geometry; filling a missing category with an ordinary label would answer a different comparison. The laboratory verifies its direct linkage calculations against SciPy's implementation. These are original teaching constructions for the theory recordings; no R program is demonstrated in the two assigned caption tracks. The next chapter follows the source's manual hierarchy examples and converts its actual R demonstrations."}, {"number": 5, "title": "Ward clustering, dendrograms, and reproducible implementations", "url": "chapter-05.html", "text": "5.1 Reading the source's five-object hierarchies A dendrogram records a nested sequence of groups and the comparison value at each merge or division. Its vertical coordinate is not a universal measure of scientific similarity. It must be interpreted through the linkage or splitting criterion that generated the tree. The horizontal leaf order can change when a branch is reversed without changing a single membership. The source's first numerical example supplies the symmetric dissimilarity matrix $$\\mathbf{D}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&9&3&6&11\\\\9&0&7&5&10\\\\3&7&0&9&2\\\\6&5&9&0&8\\\\11&10&2&8&0\\end{pmatrix}. \\tag{5.1}$$ Selected handwritten frames confirm its entries, including $D_{34}=9$. The matrix is a valid nonnegative symmetric dissimilarity with zero diagonal, but it is not a metric: $D_{15}=11$ exceeds $D_{13}+D_{35}=5$. Single and complete linkage can still analyze it as a dissimilarity matrix. A Euclidean geometric interpretation would require additional evidence. Single linkage first joins objects three and five at height two. The distance from that group to object one is $\\min(3,11)=3$, so object one joins next. Objects two and four merge at height five, and the final two groups merge at height six. The successive heights are therefore $2,3,5,6$. A two-group cut yields $\\{1,3,5\\}$ and $\\{2,4\\}$. Complete linkage also begins with three and five at height two. It then joins two and four at height five. Object one is at complete-link distance nine from that group and eleven from the other, so it joins $\\{2,4\\}$ next. The final height is eleven. A two-group cut therefore gives $\\{1,2,4\\}$ and $\\{3,5\\}$. The source's intermediate verbal calculation of the distance from object four to $\\{3,5\\}$ is inconsistent with the displayed matrix: the maximum of nine and eight is nine. Correcting that slip leaves the stated merge sequence unchanged. Figure 5.1. The same source dissimilarities produce different two-group cuts under single and complete linkage. Merge heights describe their respective linkage rules; leaf order is a drawing convention. For a monotone hierarchy, cutting at a height merges all branches whose relevant joins are at or below that threshold. Cutting for a specified number of groups instead follows the merge sequence until that number remains. Tied heights can make these operations differ: a horizontal threshold may cross several simultaneous joins. The laboratory uses an explicit fixed-group cut and compares partitions independently of their arbitrary label numbers. Source calculation: single linkage, 0:53 and complete linkage, 15:17 . The matrix and the separate divisive example were checked against selected source frames. 5.2 Ward's criterion measures the cost of a merge A useful extension compares two groups through the increase in squared-error distortion caused by representing their union by one centroid. For a nonempty set $A$ of numerical observations, let $\\boldsymbol\\mu_A$ be its arithmetic centroid and define $W(A)=\\sum_{i\\in A}\\|\\mathbf{x}_i-\\boldsymbol\\mu_A\\|^2$. Ward's criterion chooses a merge with the smallest increase $W(A\\cup B)-W(A)-W(B)$. Proposition 5.1 — Ward merge increment. For Euclidean observations and disjoint nonempty groups,\n$$\\Delta W(A,B)=\\frac{|A||B|}{|A|+|B|}\\|\\boldsymbol\\mu_A-\\boldsymbol\\mu_B\\|^2. \\tag{5.2}$$ The union centroid is $(|A|\\boldsymbol\\mu_A+|B|\\boldsymbol\\mu_B)/(|A|+|B|)$. For the points of $A$, expand each difference from that union centroid as $(\\mathbf{x}_i-\\boldsymbol\\mu_A)+(\\boldsymbol\\mu_A-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{A\\cup B})$. The cross term sums to zero because deviations from an arithmetic mean sum to zero. The squared-error contribution becomes $W(A)+|A|\\|\\boldsymbol\\mu_A-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{A\\cup B}\\|^2$. Repeat for $B$. Substitute the union centroid into the two remaining terms and collect their common squared centroid difference; the coefficient reduces to $|A||B|/(|A|+|B|)$. ∎ This increment includes both centroid separation and group sizes. Simply choosing the closest centroids is therefore a different rule. The conventional Ward dendrogram height used by SciPy and R's ward.D2 corresponds to $\\sqrt{2\\Delta W}$ for Euclidean inputs. For singleton groups, it reduces to their original Euclidean distance. R's older ward.D convention is different and must not be silently substituted. The laboratory checks the squared-error identity on the source Utilities numerical features and separately compares the complete Ward cophenetic matrix with a fresh R ward.D2 calculation. This extension is intentionally applied to Euclidean observations, not to the nonmetric matrix (5.1). Squared-error geometry cannot be asserted for an arbitrary supplied dissimilarity merely by selecting a method named Ward. This section is an authored extension. Conventions verified in R hclust and SciPy linkage . 5.3 DIANA divides a group through a splinter The source's divisive example begins with observations $A,B,C,D,E$ together. DIANA, short for divisive analysis, chooses a current cluster with the largest diameter , the largest dissimilarity between any two of its members. It starts a new splinter group with the observation having the largest average dissimilarity to the other members. It then considers transferring more observations from the remaining party to that splinter. For an observation $i$ still in a party $P$, let $a_i$ be its average dissimilarity to the other members of $P$, excluding itself. Let $b_i$ be its average dissimilarity to the current splinter $S$. The largest positive contrast $a_i-b_i$ identifies the next transfer. Recompute the averages after each transfer, and stop when no positive contrast remains or only one party member remains. This produces two nonempty groups. Repeating the largest-diameter division creates a complete nested hierarchy. The source's five-observation divisive matrix has row totals $84,73,65,89,91$, so $E$ starts the first splinter. Observation $D$ then has average dissimilarity $(32+25+23)/3$ to the remaining party, but dissimilarity nine to $E$. It moves to the splinter. The remaining party is $\\{A,B,C\\}$; its within-party averages are $10.5,10.5,9$, while its averages to $\\{D,E\\}$ are $31.5,26,23.5$. Every transfer contrast is now negative. The first stable division is therefore $\\{A,B,C\\}$ and $\\{D,E\\}$, as the laboratory verifies from the inspected source matrix. Software preprocessing is particularly consequential here. In cluster::diana(...,stand=TRUE) , standardization subtracts a feature's mean and divides by its mean absolute deviation from that mean. It does not divide by its sample standard deviation. The source uses this option for both the arrest and voting examples. Python implements that exact convention. Missing feature values in the voting table are handled through pairwise available comparisons with the same full-dimension rescaling used by the source dissimilarity calculation. The divisive coefficient summarizes the tree through the diameter of the last cluster containing each observation before it becomes a singleton. If these diameters are $d_i$ and the full-data diameter is $D>0$, the coefficient is $1-n^{-1}\\sum_i d_i/D$. The Python implementation agrees with the R coefficient and the full cophenetic matrix, whose $(i,j)$ entry is the height of their lowest common ancestor, equivalently the last cluster containing both observations before a divisive split separates them. This coefficient describes a hierarchy; it is not a probability that its clusters are genuine population classes. Source algorithm and numerical split: 25:14 . The literal Python helper is ch05_diana.py ; standardization and splitting conventions were checked against the primary DIANA documentation . 5.4 Nutrient data: a cut is a decision The first R demonstration uses the 27-by-five flexclust::nutrient dataset. Its columns are energy, protein, fat, calcium, and iron for different foods. The canonical package data are bundled in CSV form. The source first computes raw Euclidean distances, then subtracts column means and divides by sample standard deviations before recomputing distances and fitting average linkage. Python reproduces both distance matrices and the complete average-linkage cophenetic matrix against a fresh R reference. The manual five-group cut gives group sizes $7,16,1,2,1$ under first-occurrence label numbering. Raw and standardized within-group medians are computed separately, preserving the source's aggregate(...,median) demonstrations. A raw median has the original measurement units; its standardized counterpart expresses a position relative to the full-data mean and sample standard deviation. The two summaries serve different interpretations even though they describe the same membership. Figure 5.2. Converted agglomerative examples. Nutrient distances use all five standardized variables. Utilities distances use only standardized Sales and Fuel_Cost. The highlighted cuts are decisions on the trees, rather than evidence that an automatic group count has been established. The displayed NbClust call requests Euclidean average linkage for candidate counts two through fifteen and leaves its index argument at the default multi-index setting. That requests 26 criteria. It is not a request for one elbow curve or one silhouette calculation. In the fresh R reference, four criteria each recommend two, three, five, and fifteen groups: the largest vote count is tied. The package's reported selection of two resolves that tie by its convention; it is not a unique majority. The separate manual choice of five groups should remain distinguishable from this reported choice. The exact multi-index calculation is converted in ch05_nbclust.py . Its complete index tables, critical values, recommendations, and chosen partition match fresh R references for the nutrient data and a second independent fixture after the source's rounding. The comparison record states that scope. Its vote and Dindex plots reproduce the requested diagnostic quantities rather than substituting an elbow or silhouette curve. The recording also produces plotting warnings and an error in one barplot annotation; correcting a display call does not resolve the tied recommendations. Source examples: nutrient data, 1:16 , scaling and average linkage, 6:55 , and the multi-index calculation, 12:58 . 5.5 Utilities: separate the clustering space from the display space The source's Utilities table contains 22 companies and eight numerical features. Its named company column becomes row labels. The code standardizes all eight measurements, but then computes Euclidean clustering distances from columns six and eight only: Sales and Fuel_Cost . That column selection is part of the example. Clustering on all eight standardized variables would answer a different question even if a plotting call happened to produce a similar-looking figure. Python reproduces single- and average-linkage trees, requested fixed-count cuts, colored membership displays, and group listings. A fresh R reference verifies the full pairwise cophenetic matrix for each hierarchy and partition agreement for a four-group cut. A tree's drawing can reverse individual branches; exact horizontal positions and color names are not statistical invariants. The source's four-group scatter display passes all eight variables to a PCA visualization while using membership determined from two variables. Python follows the same separation. The first two display components explain approximately $27.16\\%$ and $23.75\\%$ of all-eight-variable standardized variation. Separation or overlap in this plot is therefore not a complete depiction of the two-variable clustering geometry. Its convex hulls summarize projected group points and do not establish a probabilistic boundary. Figure 5.3. Two source display conventions translated separately. Left: four-group labels come from Sales/Fuel_Cost clustering, while the projection uses all eight variables. Right: colors encode within-row standardized feature values, rather than pairwise company distances. The heatmap has another independent operation. The source supplies all eight standardized numerical columns to heatmap , keeps the column order with Colv=NA , and uses a complete-linkage row hierarchy. The default heatmap color scaling then centers and standardizes each row for display. These rowwise colors are not the original Euclidean distance matrix and do not define the earlier two-feature average-linkage groups. The converted full heatmap with its row tree preserves that distinction. Its row labels include the source's six-group average-linkage memberships as an annotation. Source code and outputs: Utilities input and column selection, 14:47 ; PCA cluster display, 18:24 ; heatmap, 19:15 . Default color scaling and independent tree construction are documented in R heatmap . 5.6 Divisive examples and distinct splitting algorithms The source next fits standardized DIANA to USArrests , then to votes.repub with Manhattan distance and mean-absolute-deviation standardization. Python retains the voting table's missing historical entries and reproduces the source distance convention. It obtains divisive coefficient $0.88691817$ for the Manhattan voting tree. Its two groups contain eight and 42 states, matching the source group listing. The standardized Euclidean version produces the same two-group partition for this table, although its complete hierarchy and coefficient differ. That agreement is an observed result for this dataset, not a metric-invariance theorem. The agriculture take-home example is also included. Fresh R checks of all four DIANA variants—arrests, Manhattan votes, Euclidean votes, and agriculture—compare their complete distance matrices, cophenetic matrices, two-group partitions, and divisive coefficients. All numerical comparisons pass the recorded tolerances. The executable main laboratory and DIANA helper require no R runtime. Figure 5.4. Converted DIANA examples with criterion-specific division heights. Equal two-group membership in the two voting analyses does not make their full trees equal. A separate banner display uses neighboring leaf split heights from the Manhattan voting tree. The final source script uses a different package, clustlearn , on its supplied two-dimensional demonstration datasets. Its divisive_clustering(...,max_iterations=5) helper repeatedly bisects groups by a bounded-iteration two-means procedure. It is not DIANA. Its height convention also differs: it records a square-root parent squared-error quantity rather than DIANA's diameter. The dedicated Python conversion is ch05_clusterlearn.py , with all six original demonstration datasets bundled. Its separate comparison record states how reference initialization is replayed to distinguish algorithm agreement from unrelated random starts. These examples illustrate three distinct decisions: the dissimilarity or feature geometry, the hierarchy construction, and the cut or display. None can be inferred reliably from a function's general label “hierarchical.” Source-code arguments and their defaults are part of the statistical specification. Source demonstrations: arrest DIANA, 20:33 ; voting example, 23:06 ; agriculture and the separate toy helper, 25:07 . Independent numeric evidence: DIANA comparisons and agglomerative comparisons . 5.7 Exercises with worked solutions A source-matrix audit. Recalculate the distance between object four and cluster $\\{3,5\\}$ in (5.1) for both single and complete linkage. Does the resulting difference change the reported two-group complete-linkage partition? Worked solution The relevant dissimilarities are nine and eight. Single linkage gives eight; complete linkage gives nine. The smallest remaining complete-linkage merge after joining three and five is still the two–four pair at five. Object one then joins that group at nine, so the final two groups remain $\\{1,2,4\\}$ and $\\{3,5\\}$. A corrected intermediate calculation can therefore leave the final partition unchanged while remaining necessary for an accurate derivation. Ward's domain. Why is it invalid to claim squared-error Ward geometry for (5.1) solely because the matrix is symmetric? State a condition that makes the centroid derivation applicable. Worked solution Symmetry and nonnegative entries do not show that distances arise from Euclidean coordinate differences. Here the triangle inequality already fails because eleven exceeds three plus two. The derivation requires actual observations in a Euclidean inner-product space, with arithmetic centroids and squared norms. On such coordinates (5.2) follows by centering and expanding sums of squares. A routine accepting arbitrary numbers does not establish those prerequisites. Divisive transfer. After $E$ starts the source splinter, explain why $D$ transfers. Recheck the decision after the split becomes $\\{A,B,C\\}$ and $\\{D,E\\}$. Worked solution Observation $D$ is on average $(32+25+23)/3$ from its party but only nine from the splinter, so moving it has a positive contrast. Afterward the three party members have within-party averages $10.5,10.5,9$ and cross-splinter averages $31.5,26,23.5$. Their contrasts are all negative, so none transfers. The averages exclude the observation itself; including its zero self-distance would change the criterion. One option, two scaling formulas. Explain why replacing diana(...,stand=TRUE) by column sample-standard-deviation scaling is not a literal conversion. How can a programmer verify that a translation uses the intended convention? Worked solution The DIANA option uses the mean absolute deviation about the column mean. The scale denominator differs from a sample standard deviation and need not differ by the same ratio across columns. Therefore the relative contributions to distance can change. Inspect the primary function documentation and compare complete dissimilarity matrices on the same original data. This project's fresh R comparisons test the full matrices, rather than relying on a similar final picture. Multiple answers on one table. The source uses two Utilities variables for clustering, all eight for PCA display, and row-standardized colors in a heatmap. State which evidence is needed before calling visual overlap a clustering failure. Worked solution First recover the actual feature subset and distance used for the labels. Then inspect that geometry or the full fitted objective, not only the two-dimensional projection of another feature space. The heatmap's rowwise colors encode relative feature levels and supply no direct pairwise-distance test. A projected overlap can result from information lost by display or from differences between the display and clustering spaces. It does not by itself refute the two-feature hierarchy. Algorithm and group-count claims. Explain why neither the multi-index selection of two groups nor agreement of two voting cuts establishes a unique population clustering. Why must the final clustlearn examples be checked independently of DIANA? Worked solution The fresh multi-index reference has a four-way tie in its largest recommendation count, so the selected two groups follow a tie convention. The voting distance choices happen to agree at a two-group cut but differ elsewhere in their trees. Neither finding is a population-identification theorem. The toy helper uses repeated two-means bisections and a squared-error height convention; DIANA uses a splinter-distance rule and diameter heights. Correctly translating one algorithm gives no automatic evidence that the other has been translated."}, {"number": 6, "title": "K-means as alternating optimization", "url": "chapter-06.html", "text": "6.1 A partition defined by squared error A hierarchy answers a nested sequence of grouping questions. K-means instead asks for one partition into a specified number of groups. Its computational appeal comes from replacing repeated comparisons between whole clusters by comparisons with representative centers. That convenience has a precise geometric cost: arithmetic means and squared Euclidean distances define the problem. Changing feature units can change the answer, and replacing a distance formula does not automatically produce a valid version of the same algorithm. The lecture introduces this distinction before its numerical example. Source: partitioning versus hierarchical clustering . Let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$ be the observed feature vectors, where $d$ is the number of features. A partition $C_1,\\ldots,C_k$ consists of nonempty, disjoint index sets whose union is $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$. With representative $\\mu_j\\in\\mathbb R^d$ for group $j$, the objective is $$W(C,\\mu)=\\sum_{j=1}^k\\sum_{i\\in C_j}\\|x_i-\\mu_j\\|_2^2.\\tag{6.1}$$ The integer $k$ is an input, not an estimate supplied by this objective alone. In particular, allowing one group per observation gives zero error. A useful grouping therefore balances compression, separation, stability, interpretability, and the intended decision. None of those additional purposes is captured merely by minimizing (6.1) over every possible $k$. The representative minimizing a fixed group's error is its arithmetic mean $\\bar x_C=|C|^{-1}\\sum_{i\\in C}x_i$. To see this without differentiability assumptions, write $x_i-a=(x_i-\\bar x_C)+(\\bar x_C-a)$, expand the squared norm, and use $\\sum_{i\\in C}(x_i-\\bar x_C)=0$. The cross terms disappear, leaving $$\\sum_{i\\in C}\\|x_i-a\\|^2=\\sum_{i\\in C}\\|x_i-\\bar x_C\\|^2+|C|\\|a-\\bar x_C\\|^2.\\tag{6.2}$$ The second term is nonnegative and vanishes exactly at the mean. This identity also explains why an outlying observation can move a center strongly: its contribution grows quadratically with distance. A medoid, a coordinatewise median, and a trimmed mean solve different representative problems and should be named separately. If each feature is standardized as $z_{i\\ell}=(x_{i\\ell}-\\bar x_\\ell)/s_\\ell$, Euclidean k-means on $z$ minimizes a weighted error on the original coordinates, with weight $s_\\ell^{-2}$ on feature $\\ell$. Standardization is thus part of the model. It does not merely make the computer numerically comfortable. The course uses sample standard deviations in its scale examples; the lab makes that divisor explicit. This differs from the mean absolute deviation used by the preceding chapter's DIANA standardization. 6.2 Descent, a hand calculation, and optimizer conventions The familiar Lloyd iteration alternates two operations. Holding the centers fixed, assign every observation to a nearest center. Holding the resulting assignments fixed, replace each center by its group mean. The first operation minimizes each observation's contribution independently. Equation (6.2) proves that the second operation minimizes each group's contribution. Consequently neither operation increases (6.1). These are conditional minimizations; their composition need not find the best partition among all possible assignments. For a finite dataset there are finitely many assignments. With a deterministic treatment of ties, nonempty groups, and strict improvement whenever the assignment changes, descent must reach a stable assignment after finitely many changes. Practical software adds iteration limits, numerical tolerances, and rules for empty groups. Without specifying these conventions, a claim that “k-means converged” is incomplete. A stable solution is a local solution under the algorithm's moves; it is not a certificate of a global minimum. The source example uses four observations $A=(5,3)$, $B=(-1,1)$, $C=(1,-2)$, and $D=(-3,-2)$. Start from groups $\\{A,B\\}$ and $\\{C,D\\}$, whose means are $(2,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$. The squared distances to these two centers are respectively $(10,61)$ for $A$, $(10,9)$ for $B$, $(17,4)$ for $C$, and $(41,4)$ for $D$. Thus only $B$ changes group. The old assigned error is $10+10+4+4=28$; reassignment with the old centers reduces it to $27$. Source: four-observation calculation . The updated centers are now $A$ itself, $(5,3)$, and the mean of $B,C,D$, namely $(-1,-1)$. The first group contributes zero. The remaining squared distances to their own center are $4,5,5$, so the updated error is $14$. Rechecking the assignments leaves all four observations in these groups. The lab executes both conditional steps and verifies the full sequence $28\\to27\\to14$. Separating the assignment error from the updated-center error makes the descent argument visible instead of hiding it inside one library call. Initialization matters because different basins can lead to different stable partitions. Multiple starts repeat an optimizer from several initial center sets and retain the result with the smallest observed error. Increasing the number of starts improves the search opportunity; it gives no general proof that the global optimum has been found. A seed fixes a particular generator's state. The same integer seed in two programming languages generally does not select the same rows or imply identical fitted groups. A useful extension explains why a transfer optimizer can differ from nearest-center reassignment. Moving $x_i$ from group $A$ of size $a>1$ to group $B$ of size $b$, while refitting both means, changes the error by $$\\Delta W=\\frac{b}{b+1}\\|x_i-\\mu_B\\|^2-\\frac{a}{a-1}\\|x_i-\\mu_A\\|^2.\\tag{6.6}$$ Apply (6.2) to the enlarged group and to the group with the point removed: addition costs the first term, while removal saves the second. Thus the exact gain includes group-size factors, whereas Lloyd's fixed-center assignment compares only distances. In the source initialization, transferring $B$ and refitting saves $2(10)-(2/3)(9)=14$, agreeing with the executed change from error 28 to 14. This transfer identity describes a move, not the complete scheduling and acceleration rules of a software optimizer. The R calls in this lecture use the default Hartigan–Wong optimizer. Its transfer operations differ from the batch reassignment just used to prove Lloyd descent. Scikit-learn's explicitly selected Lloyd implementation is therefore an instructive alternative, but is not by itself a literal translation of those default R calls. Our conversion records distinguish the objective, the optimizer, the initial centers, and the final partition. Agreement of the last item does not establish agreement of every intermediate update. The primary R kmeans documentation states the optimizer and default random-row initialization; the scikit-learn documentation describes its separate initialization and algorithm controls. 6.3 Choosing the number of groups Let $W_k^*$ denote the smallest attainable value of (6.1) among partitions into exactly $k$ nonempty groups. For $1\\le k<n$, a group containing at least two observations can be split. Using its original center for both children preserves the old error, and subsequently fitting the two means can only reduce it. Therefore $W_{k+1}^*\\le W_k^*$. This argument is about optimal objective values. A collection of finite, randomly initialized fits need not satisfy the same ordering. The source's USArrests error plot rises between two neighboring values of $k$. That rise signals an inferior local fit, a change of computational settings, or a plotting/data error; it is not a property of the optimal k-means objective. Nor does such an increase prove that the extra group is intrinsically unnecessary. The lab reports both independently fitted errors and their running minimum so this distinction can be checked. The running minimum is a diagnostic display, not a new claim that all fits have become globally optimal. Source: error-curve interpretation requiring this qualification . An elbow seeks a value beyond which further groups make comparatively small error reductions. It is a heuristic and may have no visually unique answer. A more formal source construction transforms a distortion $d_k$, proportional to mean squared error per feature, before differencing: $$d_k=\\frac{W_k}{nd},\\qquad J_k=d_k^{-Y}-d_{k-1}^{-Y},\\qquad Y=d/2.\\tag{6.3}$$ The minus sign in the power is essential. The source board shows it, although the automatic captions describe only an unspecified power. For the initial comparison one may set the transformed zero-group distortion to zero, corresponding to infinite distortion. Exact zero distortion makes the negative power singular; this method is not defined by simply raising zero to a negative exponent. A largest transformed jump is a selection rule whose meaning depends on feature dimension, scaling, and how distortions were estimated. It is different from a raw decrease $W_{k-1}-W_k$. Source: inverse-power jump formula . The source also describes the Hartigan statistic $$H(k)=(n-k-1)\\left(\\frac{W_k}{W_{k+1}}-1\\right),\\tag{6.4}$$ and the heuristic of choosing the smallest $k$ for which $H(k)\\le10$. This compares the reduction from adding a group with the remaining error. The threshold is a convention, not a universal guarantee of the true number of populations. Hartigan's selection statistic is also a different object from the Hartigan–Wong optimization algorithm. Sharing an author's name does not make their roles interchangeable. Silhouette width evaluates a fitted partition in pairwise-distance geometry. For observation $i$, let $a_i$ be the average distance to the other members of its own group. For each other group compute its average distance from $i$, and let $b_i$ be the smallest such average. For a nonsingleton group and a positive denominator, $$s_i=\\frac{b_i-a_i}{\\max(a_i,b_i)}\\in[-1,1].\\tag{6.5}$$ Positive values indicate that the observation is closer on average to its own group than to its nearest competing group. Negative values flag the opposite. The R convention assigns width zero to a singleton. The mean width compares candidate partitions for $2\\le k\\le n-1$; it is not the same objective as squared-error minimization. The lab uses Euclidean distances for this evaluation, matching the source's silhouette(...,dist(df)) . Squared distances would define a different evaluation. See the primary silhouette documentation for these conventions. Finally, a multi-index vote such as NbClust combines many criteria, each with its own response to dispersion, separation, and dimensionality. Replacing its full call by one silhouette or elbow calculation would omit most of the requested computation. The converted workflow retains the actual index tables and recommendations, rather than treating a visually similar plot as equivalent evidence. 6.4 Utilities: the feature set changes the problem The first R application reads the same 22-company Utilities table used in the hierarchical lecture. It removes the company-name column, standardizes the eight numeric features, and calls kmeans(utilities.df.norm,6) . This time all eight features define the fit. The earlier hierarchy used only standardized Sales and Fuel Cost. A difference in the resulting groups can therefore arise from a changed feature space even before considering the changed optimization criterion. Source: six-group Utilities fit and center profiles . The lab retains the recovered book-author table and translates every displayed output: group memberships, the six center vectors, the within-group errors, the group sizes, the line profiles of the centers, and the Euclidean distance matrix between centers. The source call supplies neither nstart nor a visible seed, so the relevant R defaults are one random start and Hartigan–Wong optimization. Our runnable version supplies the explicitly recorded seed 2026 to make a new execution reproducible. That choice does not reconstruct an unseen historical random-generator state. A profile coordinate is a mean in standardized units. A center with Fuel Cost coordinate one is approximately one sample standard deviation above the overall Fuel Cost mean. A negative coordinate is not a negative physical fuel price. Comparing entire profiles is more informative than calling the group number a ranking: cluster labels are arbitrary identifiers, and their numerical order has no ordinal meaning. The center-distance display summarizes separation among representative means; it omits each group's dispersion and is not a silhouette analysis. Figure 6.1. The source's all-feature k-means calculation, with a recorded execution seed. Center profiles and center-to-center distances answer different descriptive questions. 6.5 Wine: distinguish raw and standardized analyses The lecture next downloads the original UCI wine table. There are 178 observations, a cultivar label, and 13 chemical measurements. The lab retrieves that primary table and checks all 2,314 feature values against the canonical scikit-learn copy. Cultivar is retained only for evaluation and display; it is excluded from the fitted feature matrix. Including a known class label among the clustering inputs would change the question and contaminate the proposed unsupervised comparison. Source: UCI input and explicit cultivar exclusion . The first wine analysis uses raw measurement units, seed 278613, three groups, and the default single start. It then repeats the three-group analysis with 25 starts. Fresh R references give errors 2,629,315.194018 and 2,370,689.686783 respectively. These values quantify a substantial improvement in the observed search result; the second value still carries no global-optimality certificate. Raw chemical units also strongly privilege high-variance coordinates. The first raw PCA display axis alone contains approximately 99.8091% of total raw variance. A visually compelling projection in that geometry should not be mistaken for equal weighting of all 13 measurements. The source displays clusters as colors and cultivars as point shapes, then tabulates cultivar against cluster. The translated cross-tabulation preserves this distinction. Cluster labels can be permuted without changing the partition, so numerical label equality is not a classification accuracy. To compute an accuracy after label matching, the matching rule would itself need to be specified. More fundamentally, agreement with cultivars is an external assessment; it is not the squared-error quantity the clustering procedure optimized. The useful::FitKMeans call evaluates one through twenty groups with 25 starts, resetting seed 278613 before each fit. The lab reproduces that reset convention and computes (6.4). The package's returned column Clusters denotes the group count being added : its row labeled 17 contains the comparison $H(16)$ between sixteen and seventeen groups. A reader must account for this offset before interpreting the plot. The primary wrapper source establishes both the indexing and seed reset. This table does not justify three groups by the stated threshold rule. In the fresh reference the first statistic at most ten is $H(16)\\approx1.987005$. The source's visually emphasized early reduction and its threshold rule are different decision procedures. Their disagreement is an informative consequence of raw-scale geometry and heuristic selection, not a reason to edit the table until it supports a preferred answer. Figure 6.2. Raw-scale wine analysis. The Hartigan horizontal axis follows the source wrapper's next-group indexing, while the PCA display retains the original raw feature scales. A separate source block loads rattle::wine , removes its first label column, and standardizes every chemical feature. It evaluates a one-start error curve for $k=1,\\ldots,15$, resetting seed 1234 before each fitted value with $k\\ge2$. It then calls NbClust(df,min.nc=2,max.nc=15,method=\"kmeans\") , with default Euclidean distances and the default collection of 26 indices. These are new, standardized fits; they must not be merged with the raw-scale Hartigan calculation. Source: standardized wine and the complete multi-index call . An additional source-code convention matters here. NbClust 3.0.1 explicitly resets the seed to one before its internal k-means partitions. The outer set.seed(1234) is therefore not the effective initialization seed for those partitions. The literal Python wrapper retains this behavior and records its package-version scope. Its output includes the full index and critical-value tables, group-count recommendations, and selected partition. The lecture's displayed recommendation favors three groups; that result belongs to this standardized multi-index procedure. It does not turn the raw-scale Hartigan table into a three-group threshold result. Figure 6.3. Distinct diagnostics for the standardized wine analysis. The multi-index calculation uses the source package's internal initialization convention. 6.6 USArrests: inspect the partition and the diagnostics separately The final application uses the 50-state USArrests table. The four columns are Murder, Assault, Urban Population percentage, and Rape. The source removes missing rows and standardizes the retained features by sample standard deviations. This table has no missing entries, so the removal changes no rows. The translated distance heatmap contains Euclidean distances between these standardized state profiles, rather than feature values or center distances. Source: preprocessing and distance display . The source fits two through five groups with 25 starts and displays their projections into two principal coordinates. The first two standardized PCA directions contain approximately 62.0% and 24.7% of the total variance, leaving about 13.3% outside the picture. The lab records a controlled stream for these comparisons because the initial seed is not shown in that source block. Convex hulls in the projection help a reader see the displayed groups; overlap of projected hulls does not prove overlap in the complete four-dimensional feature space. Figure 6.4. Candidate partitions in one common PCA display. The projection is a diagnostic view of fitted labels, not a replacement for their original four-feature geometry. The separate fviz_nbclust calls forward no number of starts to kmeans , so their default is one start, with a maximum of ten groups. The lab retains these additional wrapper curves alongside the custom ten-start error and 25-start silhouette curves. Separate recorded seeds make these demonstrations repeatable; an unseen historical session stream is not inferred. The full state-label displays also preserve the source's selected-feature two-group scatter and final four-group PCA view. The source's error-curve function uses ten starts and seed 123 over one through fifteen groups. Its silhouette function uses 25 starts and Euclidean pairwise distances. Those are distinct fitting/evaluation settings. In a separately recorded seed-123 silhouette run, the maximum mean width is approximately 0.408489 at two groups; four groups give approximately 0.339689. Consequently the chosen four-group solution should be explained as a particular descriptive resolution, rather than presented as the unanimous optimum of every diagnostic. The final, explicitly seeded source call is set.seed(123);kmeans(df,4,nstart=25) . The fresh reference has group sizes $8,13,16,13$ and total within-group error $56.4031734583$. Total standardized error about the overall mean is $4(50-1)=196$. Its between-group component is therefore $139.5968265417$, or approximately 71.22% of total variation. This percentage is a descriptive variance decomposition, not a predictive accuracy or a causal explanation of differences among states. The source also reports means on the original feature scales. The first group, for example, has means 13.9375 for Murder, 243.625 for Assault, 53.75 for Urban Population, and 21.4125 for Rape. Original-scale summaries are often easier to interpret than standardized center coordinates. The lab prints all groups, retains each state assignment, and permits a reader to audit the summary against the exact input rows. None of these aggregate observations licenses a claim about a particular individual's behavior. Figure 6.5. Distance structure, squared-error improvement, and average silhouette address separate questions. A local-fit error increase does not overturn the monotonicity of the optimal objective. The downloadable lab separates the source-data workflow from an independent mathematical construction. Its four-point example exhaustively checks every nonempty two-group partition, proving the reported error fourteen is globally best for that small dataset. No analogous exhaustive claim is made for the larger examples. The conversion ledger records optimizer, random-stream, scaling, wrapper, and data conventions separately so a numerical agreement has a clear scope. 6.7 Exercises with worked solutions Mean optimality. For the four source points, compare the one-group squared error at the mean with its value at $a=(2,-3)$. Show why the difference cannot be negative. Worked solution The overall mean is $(0.5,0)$. The lab verifies that both sides of (6.2), evaluated at $a$, equal 98. The additional error is $4\\|(2,-3)-(0.5,0)\\|^2=4(2.25+9)=45$, so the mean-centered error is 53. The difference is a nonnegative squared norm multiplied by group size; no choice of $a$ can reduce it below zero. Two descent steps. In the source initialization, why is the intermediate error 27 rather than 14? Which operation is responsible for each reduction? Worked solution Initially the assigned squared distances sum to 28. Keeping the centers fixed but moving $B$ changes its contribution from ten to nine, producing 27. Updating the first center to $A$ makes its contribution zero; updating the second center from $(-1,-2)$ to $(-1,-1)$ yields contributions $4,5,5$ for $B,C,D$, giving total fourteen. Combining assignment and centroid updates in one report conceals which conditional minimization established each inequality. Optimal errors and failed searches. A fitted curve reports $W_4=60$ and $W_5=68$. Has the monotonicity theorem been disproved? Give a feasible five-group construction from the four-group fit. Worked solution No. The theorem concerns globally best errors, whereas these are errors returned by particular searches. Split any nonsingleton group of the four-group fit into two nonempty parts. Retaining the old center for both gives error sixty, and refitting the two means cannot increase it. Thus a feasible five-group solution has error at most sixty. The reported value sixty-eight is inferior to an explicitly available candidate; it does not establish that five groups are unnecessary. A silhouette calculation. Partition the one-dimensional observations $0,2,10,12$ into $\\{0,2\\}$ and $\\{10,12\\}$. Compute every silhouette width and its mean using ordinary absolute distance. Worked solution Each within-group average is two. For zero and twelve the competing-group average is eleven, giving width $9/11$. For two and ten it is nine, giving width $7/9$. The mean is $(9/11+7/9)/2=79/99\\approx0.79797980$, matching the executed library calculation. Squaring the pairwise distances would change the competing averages and answer. One seed, several meanings. Explain the difference between the wine WSS loop, FitKMeans , and NbClust initialization. Why is replacing all three by one scikit-learn call with the same integer seed insufficient? Worked solution The source WSS loop resets seed 1234 for each fitted group count and uses one start. FitKMeans resets seed 278613 for each group count and uses 25 starts. The audited NbClust version overwrites the outer seed with one before its internal fits. Moreover R's default optimizer is Hartigan–Wong, and its random-row initialization and generator stream differ from scikit-learn defaults. Reproducing the objective alone omits optimizer and initialization behavior. The literal helper and reference comparisons must test these conventions explicitly. Interpreting four groups. Recover the final USArrests between-group fraction from the reported total and within-group errors. Explain why a silhouette preference for two groups does not make the four-group descriptive table arithmetically incorrect. Worked solution The between-group fraction is $(196-56.4031734583)/196\\approx0.71222871$. It measures the error reduction from the overall mean to four fitted means. Silhouette instead compares within-group pairwise distances with nearest competing-group distances. The objectives differ, and different resolutions can serve different descriptive purposes. A four-group table can be computed correctly while its group count remains a modeling choice requiring explanation."}, {"number": 7, "title": "Simple linear regression: fitting and uncertainty", "url": "chapter-07.html", "text": "7.1 A fitted line is a prediction rule An insurer wants to predict a premium from an applicant's age. A table contains paired observations $(x_i,y_i)$, where $x_i$ is the age recorded for applicant or age bracket $i$, and $y_i$ is the corresponding premium. The subscript $i$ identifies a row, and $n$ is the number of rows. A useful prediction rule must preserve these pairs: permuting complete rows changes no least-squares calculation, whereas sorting the age and premium columns separately creates a different dataset. Simple linear regression uses one numerical predictor and an intercept. Its population model is\n$$Y_i=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_i+\\varepsilon_i. \\tag{7.1}$$\nHere $Y_i$ is the random response, $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ are unknown real coefficients, and $\\varepsilon_i$ is the discrepancy from the proposed line. When $E(\\varepsilon_i\\mid X)=0$, the conditional mean is $E(Y_i\\mid X)=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_i$, where $X$ denotes all observed predictor values. This conditional-mean assumption is substantive: omitted risk factors associated with age can violate it. Merely fitting a line cannot establish it. The fitted predictor is $\\widehat y(x)=\\widehat\\beta_0+\\widehat\\beta_1x$. The hat identifies a quantity calculated from the sample. Its residual for observation $i$ is $e_i=y_i-\\widehat y(x_i)$. The error $\\varepsilon_i$ belongs to the population model with unknown coefficients; the residual belongs to the fitted sample model. Calling them both an error without this distinction hides the statistical assumptions behind the calculation. Plot the paired data before interpreting a slope. A scatterplot can reveal curvature, isolated observations, unequal spread, and separate groups. A small sample correlation is not a prohibition against regression, and a significant correlation is not a certificate that the line is adequate. A curved relation can have zero linear correlation. An approximately linear relation can have a negative slope. The relevant questions are whether the proposed mean is useful for the task and whether the uncertainty calculation matches the observations. The coefficient is also not automatically a causal effect. Age can be associated with premium because of pricing rules, health composition, or other covariates. An observational fitted difference describes that association. A claim about an intervention requires an identification argument beyond least squares. Regression can be used with temporal or panel data as well, but the dependence structure must then enter the model and uncertainty calculation. The present chapter develops the independent-observation setting first. Source discussions: paired data and scatterplots ; the insurance example . The distinctions between errors and residuals, dependence, and causal interpretation are explicit clarifications in this edition. 7.2 Deriving the least-squares coefficients The ordinary least-squares criterion is the sum of squared vertical discrepancies,\n$$Q(b_0,b_1)=\\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i-b_0-b_1x_i)^2. \\tag{7.2}$$\nThe trial coefficients $b_0,b_1$ are real numbers. Squaring prevents positive and negative discrepancies from cancelling, but it also gives a large discrepancy more weight than an absolute-deviation criterion would. Least squares is a choice of loss function, not an inevitable definition of a best line. Differentiate the criterion with respect to each coefficient. At a minimizer,\n$$\\sum_i(y_i-\\widehat\\beta_0-\\widehat\\beta_1x_i)=0,\\qquad\n\\sum_i x_i(y_i-\\widehat\\beta_0-\\widehat\\beta_1x_i)=0. \\tag{7.3}$$\nThese are the normal equations. The first gives $\\widehat\\beta_0=\\overline y-\\widehat\\beta_1\\overline x$, where $\\overline x=n^{-1}\\sum_i x_i$ and $\\overline y=n^{-1}\\sum_i y_i$. Substitute this expression into the second equation and define the centered sums\n$$S_{xx}=\\sum_i(x_i-\\overline x)^2,\\quad S_{xy}=\\sum_i(x_i-\\overline x)(y_i-\\overline y),\\quad S_{yy}=\\sum_i(y_i-\\overline y)^2.$$\nIf $S_{xx}>0$, the result is\n$$\\widehat\\beta_1=\\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}},\\qquad\n\\widehat\\beta_0=\\overline y-\\widehat\\beta_1\\overline x. \\tag{7.4}$$ Proposition 7.1 — unique minimizer, proved here. If the observed predictor is not constant, equivalently $S_{xx}>0$, equation (7.4) gives the unique minimizer of (7.2). For any changes $a_0,a_1$ from the coefficients in (7.4), expand the new residual squares:\n$$Q(\\widehat\\beta_0+a_0,\\widehat\\beta_1+a_1)\n=Q(\\widehat\\beta_0,\\widehat\\beta_1)+\\sum_i(a_0+a_1x_i)^2\n-2a_0\\sum_i e_i-2a_1\\sum_i x_ie_i.$$\nThe last two sums vanish by (7.3). The remaining added sum is nonnegative. It is zero only if $a_0+a_1x_i=0$ for every observation. Two distinct predictor values imply $a_1=0$ and then $a_0=0$. Thus every other line has strictly larger criterion. ∎ If all ages are identical, the data identify the fitted value at that age but cannot distinguish an intercept change from an offsetting slope change. More repeated rows at the same age cannot repair this identifiability failure. Predictor variation, rather than sample size alone, identifies the slope. The normal equations imply $\\sum_i e_i=0$ and $\\sum_i(x_i-\\overline x)e_i=0$. These are algebraic consequences of fitting an intercept by least squares. They are not evidence that the unobserved population errors have mean zero or are uncorrelated with the predictor. In particular, omitted-variable bias can coexist with residuals whose sample mean and sample predictor covariance vanish exactly. Source derivation: least-squares differentiation and normal equations . This edition separates sample fitting identities from probabilistic assumptions. 7.3 Units, correlation, and explained variation The intercept has the units of the response. The slope has response units per predictor unit. If age is stored in decades rather than years, the slope describes a decade change. Multiplying ages by a constant rescales the coefficient, even though it can leave every fitted premium unchanged. A slope without the predictor's coding and units is not yet an interpretation. When both centered sums $S_{xx}$ and $S_{yy}$ are positive, the sample correlation is $r=S_{xy}/\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}$. Define sample standard deviations $s_x=\\sqrt{S_{xx}/(n-1)}$ and $s_y=\\sqrt{S_{yy}/(n-1)}$. Substitution into (7.4) yields\n$$\\widehat\\beta_1=r\\frac{s_y}{s_x}. \\tag{7.5}$$\nThus correlation carries sign and standardized strength, whereas the slope also carries the scale of measurement. Perfect negative linear association gives $r=-1$ and is as exactly linear as perfect positive association. With an intercept, $\\widehat y_i-\\overline y=\\widehat\\beta_1(x_i-\\overline x)$. Since centered fitted values are orthogonal to residuals,\n$$S_{yy}=\\sum_i(\\widehat y_i-\\overline y)^2+\\sum_i e_i^2\n=\\widehat\\beta_1^2 S_{xx}+\\mathrm{SSE}, \\tag{7.6}$$\nwhere $\\mathrm{SSE}=\\sum_i e_i^2$ is the residual sum of squares. For nonconstant response, the centered coefficient of determination is\n$$R^2=1-\\frac{\\mathrm{SSE}}{S_{yy}}\n=\\frac{S_{xy}^2}{S_{xx}S_{yy}}=r^2. \\tag{7.7}$$\nThis identity is proved by the substitution just shown. It requires this simple, intercept-containing least-squares model. It does not describe general nonlinear models, held-out prediction, or every software definition of an uncentered $R^2$. A high in-sample $R^2$ establishes neither accurate extrapolation nor small errors in business units. A line fitted across a broad age range may explain much variation and still make unacceptable premium errors. Conversely, noisy individual outcomes can yield a modest $R^2$ despite a useful estimated average association. Inspect the error scale and the intended prediction domain as well as a dimensionless summary. 7.4 From a fitted coefficient to uncertainty The least-squares formulas above are deterministic. To describe their behavior across repeated samples, now assume that the predictor values are fixed and nonconstant, the errors have conditional mean zero, and their conditional covariance is $\\sigma^2 I_n$. Here $\\sigma^2>0$ is the common error variance and $I_n$ is the identity matrix. This covariance condition rules out cross-observation correlation as well as unequal variances. Substitute the population model into the centered slope formula. Because the centered predictors sum to zero,\n$$\\widehat\\beta_1-\\beta_1\n=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\overline x)\\varepsilon_i}{S_{xx}}.$$\nThe conditional mean of this weighted error sum is zero. Its variance is\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\beta_1\\mid X)\n=\\frac{\\sigma^2\\sum_i(x_i-\\overline x)^2}{S_{xx}^2}\n=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}. \\tag{7.8}$$\nThis derivation identifies what improves precision: less unexplained variation, more observations with useful predictor spread, or both. Collecting many observations near the same predictor value can leave slope uncertainty large. For $n>2$, estimate the unknown variance by $s^2=\\mathrm{SSE}/(n-2)$. The two lost degrees of freedom correspond to fitting an intercept and a slope. Under the stated covariance model this estimate is unbiased: the residual vector is the projection of the error onto an $(n-2)$-dimensional orthogonal complement, so its expected squared length is $(n-2)\\sigma^2$. The projection argument is developed fully in Chapter 8 . For exact small-sample distributional inference, add the assumption that the errors are jointly Gaussian. The slope error is then normal; the residual sum of squares divided by $\\sigma^2$ is chi-square with $n-2$ degrees of freedom; and the orthogonal normal projections make them independent. These normal-distribution facts give the pivot\n$$T=\\frac{\\widehat\\beta_1-\\beta_1}{s/\\sqrt{S_{xx}}}\\sim t_{n-2}. \\tag{7.9}$$\nHere $t_\\nu$ denotes Student's distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom. Consequently a two-sided $1-\\alpha$ confidence interval is $\\widehat\\beta_1\\pm t_{1-\\alpha/2,n-2}s/\\sqrt{S_{xx}}$, where $t_{a,\\nu}$ is its $a$ quantile. Without Gaussian errors this is not an exact finite-sample result; suitable asymptotics or another uncertainty method are needed. At predictor value $x_0$, the estimated conditional mean is $\\widehat m_0=\\widehat\\beta_0+\\widehat\\beta_1x_0$. Its standard error and the prediction standard error for an independent new observation are respectively\n$$s\\sqrt{\\frac1n+\\frac{(x_0-\\overline x)^2}{S_{xx}}},\\qquad\ns\\sqrt{1+\\frac1n+\\frac{(x_0-\\overline x)^2}{S_{xx}}}. \\tag{7.10}$$\nTo see the added unit, write the prediction error as the estimation error of the mean plus the new observation's independent error. Their variances add. The irreducible new-observation variance remains even if the mean is estimated precisely. Both intervals widen away from the predictor mean; extrapolation also risks violating the proposed mean relation, a risk these formulas do not quantify. This uncertainty treatment is an authored mathematical extension of the two source lectures. The fitted model and $\\texttt{get_prediction}$ interface are documented by statsmodels . 7.5 Converting the insurance demonstration to Python The course imports an insurance CSV, uses age as the predictor, and fits male premiums with R's lm . The Python equivalent reads the same rows with pandas.read_csv , explicitly adds an intercept, and fits statsmodels.OLS . Matrix-based statsmodels does not automatically add a constant. The laboratory uses missing=\"raise\" so that missing values cannot silently change the fitted sample. A reproduction of a source run that omits missing rows must explicitly choose those same complete rows instead. The supplied Python program accepts --csv FILE with numeric age and male columns. Rename the authentic columns once if their capitalization differs; preserve the actual age coding rather than assuming years. The statistical method is convertible. Exact numerical reproduction of the course insurance output remains limited by the original data: the caption track does not enumerate the rows, and the inspected spreadsheet frame cuts off the relevant columns. The bundled default is therefore labeled an original teaching construction. It is not an inferred or invented copy of the course CSV. For the construction, take ages $x=(1,2,3,4,5,6)$ and define premiums by $y_i=2+3x_i+u_i$, with $u=(1,-1,0,0,-1,1)$. The vector $u$ has zero sum and is orthogonal to centered $x$. Equations (7.3)–(7.4) therefore recover the intercept and slope exactly. The executed laboratory verifies them both with an independent centered-sum calculation and with the library fit. Numerical checks from the executed chapter laboratory; constructed data. Quantity Value Interpretation Intercept; slope $2;\\ 3$ Recovered because the constructed discrepancy is orthogonal to the design. $S_{xx}$; SSE; $s^2$ $17.5;\\ 4;\\ 1$ Residual variance divides by $n-2$. $R^2$ $0.975232198$ Equals the squared sample correlation. 95% slope interval $[2.336302676,3.663697324]$ Normal-theory calculation; a constructed sample is not evidence of sampling coverage. Mean interval at $\\overline x$ $[11.366521032,13.633478968]$ Uncertainty about a conditional mean. New-observation interval at $\\overline x$ $[9.501096535,15.498903465]$ Includes the new error variance. The program also permutes paired rows and checks that coefficients are unchanged. Reversing the sign of the predictor changes the slope to $-3$ while preserving fitted values. Removing the intercept instead gives a slope $3.461538462$ and residual sum $2.307692308$. The residual-zero-sum identity belongs to the intercept model, so that last result is an expected change, not numerical failure. Source R workflow: CSV import and lm . Conventions checked against R's lm manual and the statsmodels OLS documentation . The exact program and saved stdout appear in the Python laboratory . 7.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — error or residual? A fitted intercept model has zero residual sum. Does that prove the population error has conditional mean zero? Solution No. Differentiating the sample criterion with respect to the intercept imposes $\\sum e_i=0$ at the minimizer, regardless of the data-generating mechanism. Conditional mean-zero errors refer to the unknown population relation and require a substantive model. An omitted covariate associated with $x$ can bias a causal interpretation while the fitted residual sum is still zero. Conceptual — correlation as a gate. Explain why a negative sample correlation or an insignificant correlation test does not by itself rule out a regression analysis. Solution A negative correlation indicates a downward linear association; equation (7.5) gives a negative slope. An insignificant test means the sample does not reject a specified null at its chosen level, not that the slope or any nonlinear relation is absent. Model usefulness depends on effect size, uncertainty, the prediction task, sample size, and diagnostics. A nonlinear feature model may be useful even if raw linear correlation is zero. Derivation — changing age units. Set $z_i=(x_i-a)/b$, with $b\\ne0$. Derive coefficients representing the same fitted line in $z$. Solution Since $x_i=a+bz_i$, the fitted mean is $\\widehat\\beta_0+\\widehat\\beta_1a+(b\\widehat\\beta_1)z_i$. Thus the new intercept is $\\widehat\\gamma_0=\\widehat\\beta_0+a\\widehat\\beta_1$ and new slope $\\widehat\\gamma_1=b\\widehat\\beta_1$. Every fitted value, residual, and SSE is unchanged. The intercept changes its reference age; the slope changes its unit. Derivation — why prediction intervals are wider. Derive the extra term in the second standard error of (7.10). Solution For an independent new response $Y_0=m_0+\\varepsilon_0$, the prediction error is $Y_0-\\widehat m_0=\\varepsilon_0-(\\widehat m_0-m_0)$. The new error is independent of the training estimate and has variance $\\sigma^2$. The mean-estimation variance is $\\sigma^2[1/n+(x_0-\\overline x)^2/S_{xx}]$. Add them and replace $\\sigma^2$ by $s^2$. The extra one is irreducible observation variability. Computation — reproduce the construction. Run labs/ch07.py and verify the coefficient formula, residual degrees of freedom, and $R^2=r^2$ independently. Solution The centered predictor sum of squares is $17.5$. Since the constructed noise has zero design inner products, $S_{xy}=3S_{xx}$, giving slope $3$ and intercept $2$. The squared noise sums to $4$, and division by $6-2$ gives $s^2=1$. The program calculates $R^2=0.975232198$ and asserts equality to squared correlation at absolute tolerance $10^{-12}$. These are numerical checks of the finite construction. Computation and design — a misleadingly precise extrapolation. Use (7.10) to compare a conditional-mean interval at the predictor mean with one outside the observed range. What additional risk is absent from both intervals? Solution The squared distance term is zero at $x_0=\\overline x$ and positive elsewhere, so the model-based mean interval is narrowest at the predictor mean. For the laboratory data, the 95% conditional-mean interval at $x_0=\\overline x=3.5$ has half-width $1.133478968$. At $x_0=8$ its half-width is $3.194492272$, with fitted mean $26$ and interval $[22.805507728,29.194492272]$. Outside the observed range, the assumed straight mean can be wrong even when coefficient uncertainty is small. Equations (7.9)–(7.10) condition on the model; they do not include misspecification or an altered deployment population. A meaningful deployment check therefore needs data near its intended prediction domain. 7.7 What the single-predictor model captures and misses A straight line summarizes a conditional average using two coefficients. Its derivation exposes the role of predictor spread, measurement units, and residual variation. It cannot separate the roles of several correlated predictors. A second measured risk factor can change both a premium prediction and the interpretation of the age coefficient. Multiple regression therefore extends the mean from a line to a linear combination of several features while retaining the projection structure. Independent Gaussian, equal-variance errors are sufficient for the exact intervals developed here, not universal properties of regression data. Later chapters examine influential observations, dependent errors, distributional diagnostics, and unequal variance. These additions modify particular assumptions; they do not erase the difference between a fitted relationship and a causal claim."}, {"number": 8, "title": "Multiple regression and projection geometry", "url": "chapter-08.html", "text": "8.1 A coefficient is conditional on a specified model A used car's mileage and age both help predict its price. A simple mileage slope averages over the observed ages at different mileages. A multiple-regression mileage slope instead describes a comparison at the same modeled age and other included predictors. These need not be the same comparison, so different coefficient values do not by themselves indicate software disagreement. Let $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$ collect observed responses. Let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$ be the design matrix , with row $x_i^{\\mathsf T}$ describing observation $i$. An intercept is represented by a column of ones. With $p$ nonintercept columns, $q=p+1$ coefficients are collected in $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q$. The model is\n$$y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon, \\tag{8.1}$$\nwhere $\\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is the unobserved error vector. Each column must have a defined measurement scale or encoding. A categorical predictor can contribute several indicator columns, so counting named variables is not always counting coefficients. When $E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=0$, the model specifies the conditional response mean $X\\beta$. In an additive model, a slope compares a one-unit change in its design coordinate while the other coordinates are fixed. Whether such a comparison exists in the observed data is a separate question. For strongly dependent features, holding one fixed and moving another can describe combinations with little support. The coefficient then extrapolates within the model even if it is algebraically identified. The laboratory constructs two correlated predictors with response coefficients $-2$ and $3$. Its simple first-predictor slope is $-0.2$, whereas the adjusted slope is $-2$. Both calculations use the same response rows. The difference occurs because the simple model incorporates the second predictor's association with the first. It is an illustration of adjustment, not a causal estimate or a reconstruction of the source Toyota dataset. Source discussion: several factors affecting a price and simple and adjusted Toyota regressions . 8.2 Least squares is an orthogonal projection Minimize $Q(b)=\\|y-Xb\\|^2$, where $\\|v\\|^2=v^{\\mathsf T}v$ is the squared Euclidean norm. Differentiation gives the normal equations\n$$X^{\\mathsf T}X\\widehat\\beta=X^{\\mathsf T}y. \\tag{8.2}$$\nAssume $X$ has full column rank $q\\leq n$. Then $X^{\\mathsf T}X$ is positive definite: for any nonzero $a$, $a^{\\mathsf T}X^{\\mathsf T}Xa=\\|Xa\\|^2>0$. Consequently the unique solution is\n$$\\widehat\\beta=(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}y. \\tag{8.3}$$\nThe inverse is a mathematical expression, not a recommended computational recipe. A QR factorization or a singular-value decomposition solves the least-squares problem without explicitly forming the inverse of a potentially ill-conditioned cross-product matrix. Proposition 8.1 — projection decomposition, proved here. Define $H=X(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$ and $M=I_n-H$. The fitted response is $\\widehat y=Hy$, the residual is $e=My$, and $X^{\\mathsf T}e=0$. Both $H$ and $M$ are symmetric and idempotent, meaning $H^2=H$ and $M^2=M$. Transpose $H$ and use symmetry of $(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}$ to obtain $H^{\\mathsf T}=H$. Multiplication gives\n$$H^2=X(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}(X^{\\mathsf T}X)(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}=H.$$\nIt follows that $M^{\\mathsf T}=M$ and $M^2=I_n-2H+H^2=M$. Also $HX=X$ and $MX=0$, so $X^{\\mathsf T}My=0$. Equation (8.3) yields $X\\widehat\\beta=Hy$, and subtraction yields $e=My$. Finally $HM=0$ makes fitted and residual components orthogonal. ∎ For any other coefficients $b$, write $y-Xb=e+X(\\widehat\\beta-b)$. Orthogonality makes its squared norm equal to $\\|e\\|^2+\\|X(\\widehat\\beta-b)\\|^2$. Full rank makes the second term strictly positive unless $b=\\widehat\\beta$. This proves global minimality and uniqueness without appealing to a local derivative test alone. The column space $\\operatorname{col}(X)$ is the set of all response vectors representable by this design. A rank-deficient matrix still has a unique projection onto that space, but several coefficient vectors may represent the same projected vector. Software can select a pseudoinverse solution or omit redundant coefficients. Such a convention cannot create unique scientific identification where the design lacks it. Source derivation: matrix least squares and residual projection . This edition uses $q$ for the full coefficient count, including any intercept. 8.3 What unbiasedness actually assumes Keep $X$ fixed and full rank. Put $A=(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$. Substituting (8.1) into (8.3) gives the exact identity $\\widehat\\beta-\\beta=A\\varepsilon$. Hence\n$$E(\\widehat\\beta\\mid X)=\\beta+A E(\\varepsilon\\mid X). \\tag{8.4}$$\nConditional mean-zero errors therefore imply conditional unbiasedness. The sample normal equations do not imply this assumption: a least-squares residual is orthogonal to the design by construction, even if omitted features make $E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)$ nonzero. Let $\\Omega=\\operatorname{Cov}(\\varepsilon\\mid X)$ be the conditional error covariance matrix. Linear transformation of a random vector gives\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(\\widehat\\beta\\mid X)=A\\Omega A^{\\mathsf T}. \\tag{8.5}$$\nWhen $\\Omega=\\sigma^2I_n$, this reduces to $\\sigma^2(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}$. Gaussianity has not entered either derivation. Unequal variances or correlated errors change the covariance formula; they need not change the coefficient expectation if the conditional mean remains zero. The residual is $e=M(X\\beta+\\varepsilon)=M\\varepsilon$. It therefore has conditional mean zero and covariance\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(e\\mid X)=\\sigma^2M \\tag{8.6}$$\nin the equal-variance, uncorrelated-error model. This is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The scalar expected residual squared length is different:\n$$E(e^{\\mathsf T}e\\mid X)=E[\\operatorname{tr}(\\varepsilon\\varepsilon^{\\mathsf T}M)\\mid X]\n=\\operatorname{tr}(\\sigma^2I_n M)=\\sigma^2(n-q). \\tag{8.7}$$\nThe trace of $H$ is $\\operatorname{tr}[(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}X]=q$, so $\\operatorname{tr}(M)=n-q$. Symmetric idempotent matrices have only zero and unit eigenvalues, identifying these traces as the projection dimensions. For $n>q$, the residual variance estimator $s^2=e^{\\mathsf T}e/(n-q)$ is thus unbiased for $\\sigma^2$. Even with independent errors, residuals are generally dependent because (8.6) contains off-diagonal entries. Their individual variances are $\\sigma^2(1-h_{ii})$, where $h_{ii}$ is the corresponding diagonal element of $H$. This fact is central to the different residual scalings in influence diagnostics . Source discussion: coefficient moments and residual squared length . Covariance of a vector and expectation of its squared length are kept separate here. 8.4 Gaussian likelihood and exact inference Now impose $\\varepsilon\\mid X\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2I_n)$, with $\\sigma^2>0$. Apart from an additive constant, the log likelihood is\n$$\\ell(\\beta,\\sigma^2)=-\\frac n2\\log(\\sigma^2)-\\frac{(y-X\\beta)^{\\mathsf T}(y-X\\beta)}{2\\sigma^2}. \\tag{8.8}$$\nFor any fixed positive variance, maximizing this expression minimizes the same least-squares criterion. Thus the coefficient maximum-likelihood estimator is (8.3). For positive residual sum of squares, differentiating the likelihood in $v=\\sigma^2$ after fitting the coefficients gives $-n/(2v)+\\mathrm{SSE}/(2v^2)=0$, and therefore\n$$\\widehat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{ML}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{SSE}}n\n=\\frac{n-q}{n}s^2. \\tag{8.9}$$\nThe coefficient estimators coincide under this Gaussian model, but the unbiased and likelihood variance estimators differ. If the residual sum of squares is zero, the likelihood increases as the variance approaches zero; the stated positive-variance parameter space has no finite maximizer. Under the Gaussian model, $\\widehat\\beta\\mid X$ is multivariate normal. Orthogonal projection makes $e$ independent of $\\widehat\\beta$, and $\\mathrm{SSE}/\\sigma^2\\sim\\chi^2_{n-q}$. For coefficient index $j$, the exact null statistic for $H_0:\\beta_j=b_j$ is\n$$T_j=\\frac{\\widehat\\beta_j-b_j}{s\\sqrt{[(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}]_{jj}}}\\sim t_{n-q}. \\tag{8.10}$$\nThe degrees of freedom count all estimated coefficients, including the intercept. A multiple-regression prediction does not automatically use the simple-model value $n-2$. A two-sided p-value is the null-model tail probability $\\Pr(|T_{n-q}|\\geq|T_j^{\\mathrm{obs}}|)$. It is not the probability that $H_0$ is true. Failure to reject does not establish an absent association; rejection does not establish a causal effect or a useful predictive improvement. The confidence interval describes uncertainty for the coefficient of this specified model under its assumptions. For a reduced design nested inside a full design, let their ranks be $q_R<q_F<n$, with $r=q_F-q_R$. Under the Gaussian model and the null that the reduced mean is correct,\n$$F=\\frac{(\\mathrm{SSE}_R-\\mathrm{SSE}_F)/r}{\\mathrm{SSE}_F/(n-q_F)}\\sim F_{r,n-q_F}. \\tag{8.11}$$\nThe additional fitted subspace has dimension $r$ and is orthogonal to the full residual space. Their independent normal squared lengths produce the numerator and denominator chi-squares. This identifies both why the statistic is nonnegative for truly nested models and why the calibration fails if the candidate models were chosen adaptively without accounting for selection. For a new design row $x_0^{\\mathsf T}$, the fitted mean is $x_0^{\\mathsf T}\\widehat\\beta$. Its estimated variance is $s^2x_0^{\\mathsf T}(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}x_0$. An independent new observation adds $s^2$ to that quantity for a prediction interval. Rebuild the new design with exactly the training encoding: a changed category baseline or missing interaction column changes the meaning of $x_0$. Source discussions: Gaussian likelihood , coefficient tests , and new-data prediction . Nested-model geometry is an authored extension. 8.5 Python ports of the car and housing analyses The source Toyota demonstration fits price on mileage, adds age, and then uses R's Price ~ . formula to include the other columns. It samples a training subset and holds the remaining rows out. The Python port implements all three fits, expands the all-predictor formula explicitly, and predicts the held-out rows. It accepts exact zero-based training-row indices rather than pretending that an identical seed generates the same sample in R and NumPy. A small sample is still vulnerable to overfitting; its size is a reason for careful assessment, not an exemption from it. The original course car file is a small subset, and the caption text does not identify every row or the selected split. Therefore its exact numerical output cannot currently be reproduced. The conversion of the methods is supplied as --toyota FILE --training-rows ... , with the data and split requirement stated explicitly. A differently sampled public Toyota dataset would not authenticate the source run. The housing demonstration uses a borough category, building units, and floor area to model market value per square foot. A canonical dataset is available from the statistical author Jared Lander, together with new observations. The local Python run uses these downloaded files, renames the descriptive source columns to Units , SqFt , and ValuePerSqFt , and fits ValuePerSqFt ~ Units + SqFt + C(Boro) . The default reference borough is Bronx. The source caption's spelling or decimal renderings are not used as numerical ground truth. Executed Python housing analysis on Jared Lander's downloaded data; coefficients and intervals also checked against a fresh R fit on identical inputs. Quantity Executed value Observations; residual degrees of freedom $2626;\\ 2619$ Intercept $43.290862795$ Units coefficient $-0.188115072$ Floor-area coefficient $0.000210323$ Manhattan offset from Bronx $130.992362857$ In-sample $R^2$ $0.600903519$ First new-row fitted mean $75.417637834$ First new-row mean interval, 95% $[71.293693226,79.541582441]$ First new-row observation interval, 95% $[-9.692148739,160.527424407]$ The negative lower prediction bound is mathematically possible under the Gaussian linear model even though a market value cannot be negative. This reveals a limitation of that response-scale model. It is not a reason to delete the reported bound or to reinterpret it as a guaranteed economic range. A different response transformation or likelihood would need a new model and assessment. R's coefficient extraction becomes fit.params ; coefficient standard errors become fit.bse ; confidence limits become fit.conf_int() ; and new-observation summaries come from fit.get_prediction(new).summary_frame(alpha=0.05) . A coefficient plot must state whether its bars show one standard error or a confidence interval. These are different quantities. Histograms and faceted borough displays compare observed distributions; they do not establish that borough is causally responsible for price differences. Default laboratory execution uses the original finite construction. The two publicly obtained housing files are also bundled for offline use: housing observations and new observations . Run python labs/ch08.py --housing labs/data/ch08-housing.csv --new labs/data/ch08-housingNew.csv for the converted real-data analysis. Its saved execution output is recorded separately, preserving the distinction between the original teaching construction and the recovered course example. Source R examples: Toyota fitting and split , housing exploration and model , coefficient extraction and plots , and prediction intervals . Data: housing.csv and housingNew.csv . Implementation conventions: R lm and statsmodels OLS . 8.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — what is fixed? Why can a mileage coefficient change after adding age, and why is the adjusted coefficient not automatically causal? Solution The simple coefficient summarizes the response change over the ages associated with observed mileage. The adjusted coefficient describes a model comparison at fixed age. Correlation between age and mileage lets an omitted age contribution enter the simple coefficient. Adjustment only concerns included columns; unmeasured confounding, selection, and lack of supported predictor combinations can still defeat a causal interpretation. Conceptual — vector covariance or squared length? Are $\\operatorname{Cov}(e\\mid X)$ and $E(e^{\\mathsf T}e\\mid X)$ interchangeable? Solution No. The covariance is the matrix $\\sigma^2M$, encoding each residual's variance and every pair's covariance. The expected squared length is the scalar trace $\\sigma^2(n-q)$ because the residual mean is zero. A scalar cannot replace a matrix in a covariance propagation calculation. Derivation — unbiased residual variance. Starting from $e=M\\varepsilon$, derive $E(s^2\\mid X)=\\sigma^2$ without Gaussianity. Solution Under conditional mean-zero errors with covariance $\\sigma^2I_n$, $E(\\varepsilon\\varepsilon^{\\mathsf T}\\mid X)=\\sigma^2I_n$. Since $M$ is symmetric and idempotent, $e^{\\mathsf T}e=\\operatorname{tr}(M\\varepsilon\\varepsilon^{\\mathsf T})$. Conditional expectation gives $\\sigma^2\\operatorname{tr}(M)=\\sigma^2(n-q)$. Division by $n-q>0$ proves the claim. Gaussianity is needed later for the exact chi-square law, not this expectation. Derivation — coefficient basis change. If $B$ is an invertible $q\\times q$ matrix and $X_*=XB$, show the fitted values are unchanged and derive the transformed coefficient. Solution The two designs span the same column space, so their unique orthogonal projection of $y$ is identical. Set $\\widehat\\beta_*=B^{-1}\\widehat\\beta$; then $X_*\\widehat\\beta_*=XBB^{-1}\\widehat\\beta=X\\widehat\\beta$. Full rank ensures this is the unique transformed least-squares coefficient. Coefficients depend on coordinates, whereas fitted vectors depend on the represented space. Computation — verify the projections. Run the default laboratory and independently check the projection dimensions and the two variance estimates. Solution The run has $n=8$, $q=3$, trace $H=3$, and trace $M=5$. The constructed discrepancy lies in the residual space and has squared length $8$. Consequently the unbiased variance estimate is $8/5=1.6$ and its Gaussian likelihood counterpart is $8/8=1$. The program asserts idempotence, $MX=0$, and agreement between two least-squares solvers. Computation — reproduce and audit new-data intervals. Use the two bundled housing files to run the real-data analysis. Compare mean and observation intervals for the first row, and state what would invalidate a cross-language comparison. Solution The first fitted mean is $75.417637834$. The mean interval is $[71.293693226,79.541582441]$, and the wider observation interval is $[-9.692148739,160.527424407]$. An authentic comparison must match rows, missing-value handling, column names, factor levels and contrasts, intercept, confidence level, and interval type. Agreement on a different dataset or a different training split would not reproduce the source run. A fresh R fit on the downloaded housing files agrees with Python at an absolute tolerance of $10^{-8}$; this checks the conversion while preserving the separate uncertainty about the source file's exact version. 8.7 A fitted surface still needs diagnostic checks Multiple regression captures additive conditional associations in a chosen design space. Nonlinear features and interactions can enlarge that space while retaining linear coefficients. Neither full rank nor a small residual sum of squares guarantees an adequate error model, supported extrapolation, or stable coefficients. A single influential row can materially change the fitted surface, and a small residual does not by itself exclude influence. The next chapter separates residual size, predictor geometry, and deletion effects."}, {"number": 9, "title": "Outliers, leverage, and influence", "url": "chapter-09.html", "text": "9.1 Three different reasons to inspect an observation A delivery route contains a stop whose service time is much larger than the others. Before deleting it, distinguish three questions. Is its response unusual relative to the fitted mean? Is its predictor combination unusual relative to the other design rows? Does removing it materially change the fitted model? Residuals, leverage, and influence answer these different questions. Use the full-rank linear model $y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon$ with $n$ rows and $q$ design columns, including an intercept when specified. The fitted coefficient is $\\widehat\\beta=(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}y$, the fitted response is $\\widehat y=X\\widehat\\beta$, and $e=y-\\widehat y$ is the residual vector. A response outlier has a discrepancy unusual under the proposed response model. A high-leverage row has an unusual predictor position in the design geometry. An influential row changes a specified fitted quantity substantially when its contribution changes or it is removed. A high-leverage observation lying close to the fitted surface may have a small residual. An unusual response near the center of the predictor cloud can have low leverage. Influence combines discrepancy with position. Calling all three simply an outlier makes a diagnostic cutoff look like a decision rule it cannot justify. Inspection can reveal a transcription mistake, a genuinely rare event, a different population, or an inadequate mean model. These imply different actions. A documented measurement error may be corrected. A rare but valid event may be exactly what a risk model must retain. A mixture of operating regimes may require separate models. Repeatedly deleting whichever row crosses a diagnostic threshold can progressively erase valid tail behavior and invalidate the analysis that motivated the deletion. Source distinction and domain-sensitive action: unusual and influential observations and retention, separate analysis, and deletion . The historical ozone anecdote is omitted because it is not needed to establish the diagnostic principles. 9.2 Leverage is a property of the design Define the hat matrix $H=X(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$. Its diagonal entry $h_{ii}=x_i^{\\mathsf T}(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}x_i$ is the leverage of row $i$. It depends on the predictors, not on the observed response. The projection result in Chapter 8 gives symmetry and idempotence. Proposition 9.1 — leverage bounds, proved here. Each leverage lies in $[0,1]$, and $\\sum_i h_{ii}=q$. If the design contains an intercept, $h_{ii}\\geq1/n$. The diagonal of $H^2=H$, together with symmetry, gives $h_{ii}=\\sum_j h_{ij}^2\\geq0$ and $h_{ii}\\geq h_{ii}^2$. Thus $h_{ii}(1-h_{ii})\\geq0$, giving the upper bound. Cyclic invariance of trace gives $\\operatorname{tr}(H)=\\operatorname{tr}[(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}X]=q$. With an intercept, the unit vector $n^{-1/2}\\mathbf1$ belongs to the fitted space. Projecting coordinate vector $\\delta_i$ onto that direction already contributes squared length $1/n$. Its projection onto the remaining orthogonal fitted directions adds a nonnegative amount, and $h_{ii}=\\|H\\delta_i\\|^2\\geq1/n$. ∎ The average leverage is $q/n$ for every full-rank design. No assumption that leverages are equally distributed is required. Rules such as $2q/n$ or $3q/n$ identify rows worth inspecting; they are not universal significance cutoffs. When $q$ is large relative to $n$, these screens also have little room to distinguish individual rows. One leverage equal to zero makes the corresponding row and column of $H$ zero. It does not imply the entire matrix is zero. One leverage equal to one makes that row's off-diagonal entries zero and its fitted value equal its observed value. It does not imply $H=I_n$ or perfect fits for all other observations. Moreover $h_{ii}=1$ makes deletion remove information needed for rank, so the ordinary deletion formulas below require $h_{ii}<1$. Under conditional error covariance $\\sigma^2I_n$, the residual covariance is $\\sigma^2(I_n-H)$. In particular,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(e_i\\mid X)=\\sigma^2(1-h_{ii}). \\tag{9.1}$$\nThis follows from matrix idempotence, not from an imagined scalar identity $h_{ii}^2=h_{ii}$. A diagonal entry of a projection is generally fractional, as the delivery example demonstrates. Source hat-matrix and leverage discussion: definition and bounds , trace and screening . The statements about individual extreme leverages are corrected here. 9.3 Standardization is not a single convention Let $s^2=\\mathrm{SSE}/(n-q)$ with $n>q$. The internally studentized residual , often called a standardized residual, is\n$$r_i=\\frac{e_i}{s\\sqrt{1-h_{ii}}}. \\tag{9.2}$$\nThe factor $\\sqrt{1-h_{ii}}$ adjusts for the different residual variances in (9.1). Dividing only by $s$ ignores this geometry. Package terminology varies, so a conversion must state the formula rather than rely on a label. The same observation contributes to both the numerator and the estimate $s$. Consequently $r_i$ is not generally a Student t variable with the residual degrees of freedom. In fact, the deletion identity below implies $r_i^2\\leq n-q$ when the required deleted fit exists. A variable bounded in magnitude cannot have the ordinary unbounded t distribution. Treating an internal residual as an exact t statistic would give an unjustified calibration. Let $s_{(i)}^2$ be the residual variance estimate after deleting row $i$ and refitting the same design columns. Provided that deleted design retains rank $q$ and $n>q+1$, define the externally studentized residual $$t_i=\\frac{e_i}{s_{(i)}\\sqrt{1-h_{ii}}}. \\tag{9.3}$$\nUnder the Gaussian equal-variance model, this statistic has the exact $t_{n-q-1}$ distribution for a prespecified observation. One way to see the independence is to write the deleted prediction error as the held-out observation error minus the deleted fitted-mean error; it is independent of the deleted model's orthogonal residual projection. The denominator uses that deleted projection's variance estimate. Screening every observation at a fixed single-observation level is a multiple-comparison procedure. A threshold of two may be convenient for a plot but is not a guarantee about the probability of any false flag across the dataset. Also, a small residual does not exclude influence when the row has high leverage and helps pull the fit toward itself. Source residual scaling and deleted variance: standardized and studentized diagnostics . This edition states the internal/external formulas and their different distributional status explicitly. 9.4 Deletion diagnostics can be derived without repeated inverses Write $G=X^{\\mathsf T}X$ and let $\\widehat\\beta_{(i)}$ denote the coefficient fitted after deleting observation $i$. Assume $h_{ii}<1$, so that the deleted design retains full column rank. The coefficient change has the exact form\n$$\\Delta_i=\\widehat\\beta-\\widehat\\beta_{(i)}\n=\\frac{G^{-1}x_i e_i}{1-h_{ii}}. \\tag{9.4}$$\nTo prove it, the deleted normal equations give $(G-x_ix_i^{\\mathsf T})\\Delta_i=x_i e_i$. Substituting the right side of (9.4) into the left multiplies $G^{-1}x_i$ by $G-x_ix_i^{\\mathsf T}$ and produces $x_i(1-h_{ii})$. The denominator cancels and yields $x_i e_i$, as required. Uniqueness of the deleted solution completes the proof. The condition $h_{ii}<1$ has a geometric interpretation. Whiten the deleted cross-product matrix as $G^{1/2}(I-uu^{\\mathsf T})G^{1/2}$ with $u=G^{-1/2}x_i$. Its only potentially reduced eigenvalue is $1-u^{\\mathsf T}u=1-h_{ii}$. It is positive precisely when the deletion has retained all coefficient directions. Multiplying (9.4) by $x_i^{\\mathsf T}$ gives the change in the fitted value at row $i$. Hence its held-out prediction error is\n$$y_i-x_i^{\\mathsf T}\\widehat\\beta_{(i)}\n=e_i+\\frac{h_{ii}e_i}{1-h_{ii}}\n=\\frac{e_i}{1-h_{ii}}. \\tag{9.5}$$\nA small training residual can therefore conceal a much larger leave-one-out discrepancy when leverage is close to one. The deleted residual sum of squares is\n$$\\mathrm{SSE}_{(i)}=\\mathrm{SSE}-\\frac{e_i^2}{1-h_{ii}},\\qquad\ns_{(i)}^2=\\frac{\\mathrm{SSE}-e_i^2/(1-h_{ii})}{n-q-1}. \\tag{9.6}$$\nFor the first identity, evaluate the full-data criterion at the deleted coefficient. Projection orthogonality makes it $\\mathrm{SSE}+\\Delta_i^{\\mathsf T}G\\Delta_i$. Remove the squared held-out residual from (9.5). Since $\\Delta_i^{\\mathsf T}G\\Delta_i=e_i^2h_{ii}/(1-h_{ii})^2$, the expression reduces to (9.6). Nonnegativity of the deleted sum of squares also proves the internal-residual bound discussed earlier. These identities avoid fitting $n$ models merely to compute the diagnostics. Nevertheless explicit refits make an excellent independent implementation check. The laboratory refits all deleted delivery datasets and checks their coefficient changes, held-out errors, and standardized changes against the formulas. 9.5 Cook distance, DFFITS, and coefficient-specific changes Cook distance measures the squared change across all fitted responses, scaled by the full-model residual variance:\n$$D_i=\\frac{\\|X\\widehat\\beta-X\\widehat\\beta_{(i)}\\|^2}{q s^2}\n=\\frac{e_i^2}{q s^2}\\frac{h_{ii}}{(1-h_{ii})^2}\n=\\frac{r_i^2}{q}\\frac{h_{ii}}{1-h_{ii}}. \\tag{9.7}$$\nThe second equality follows by substituting (9.4). This decomposition explains why discrepancy and design position interact. A diagnostic such as $4/n$ is a screening convention; Cook distance is not itself an ordinary probability. DFFITS uses the externally estimated variance and standardizes the change in the fitted value of the deleted row:\n$$\\mathrm{DFFITS}_i=\\frac{\\widehat y_i-x_i^{\\mathsf T}\\widehat\\beta_{(i)}}{s_{(i)}\\sqrt{h_{ii}}}\n=t_i\\sqrt{\\frac{h_{ii}}{1-h_{ii}}}. \\tag{9.8}$$\nThis expression assumes positive leverage and positive deleted residual variance. An intercept gives $h_{ii}\\geq1/n$, so positive leverage is automatic here. The sign records the direction of the fitted-value change. Let $c_{jj}$ be diagonal entry $j$ of $G^{-1}$. The standardized change in coefficient $j$ is\n$$\\mathrm{DFBETAS}_{ij}=\\frac{\\widehat\\beta_j-\\widehat\\beta_{(i),j}}{s_{(i)}\\sqrt{c_{jj}}}. \\tag{9.9}$$\nThis is a vector across coefficients. A row may have little effect on one coefficient and a substantial effect on another. The denominator uses the full design's coefficient scale together with the deleted variance estimate; comparing unstandardized coefficient changes across different units can be misleading. Common exploratory cutoffs include $2\\sqrt{q/n}$ for the magnitude of DFFITS and $2/\\sqrt n$ for DFBETAS. They prioritize inspection. They do not prove an observation is erroneous, nor do flags across several mathematically related diagnostics constitute independent replications of a hypothesis test. Report what changes after a sensitivity refit and decide how to handle a valid unusual row from the analysis objective. Source practical diagnostics: Cook distance and coefficient and fitted-value deletion changes . Implemented formulas were checked against statsmodels OLSInfluence . 9.6 Reproducing the delivery example in Python The source models service time using products stocked and distance walked. The dataset is delivery from R's robustbase package. Its primary package manual identifies the three variables and the original Montgomery–Peck source. The Python laboratory includes the factual observations in their original order, retrieved from a public data mirror, so it runs offline without R. The port fits the same intercept model, obtains leverage and residual diagnostics, and produces the source demonstration's standardized-residual, studentized-residual, leverage, DFFITS, Cook-distance bar and chart displays, and coefficient-specific DFBETAS panels. Numerical values are independently checked against the derived formulas, all explicit deleted refits, and a fresh base-R calculation on the identical delivery rows. The graphical styling is a Python presentation; it does not claim pixel-identical reproduction of the R plots. Executed delivery diagnostics, with source observation numbering preserved. Quantity Observation 9 Observation 22 Leverage $0.498292159$ $0.391575218$ Residual $7.419706203$ $-3.686527888$ Internal standardized residual $3.213762784$ $-1.449995414$ External studentized residual $4.310780119$ $-1.489624732$ Cook distance $3.419318411$ $0.451045451$ DFFITS $4.296080927$ $-1.195036104$ The coefficient vector for intercept, products, and distance is $(2.341231145,1.615907211,0.014384826)$. There are $25$ rows and $3$ coefficients, so the mean leverage is $0.12$ and the source Cook screening cutoff $4/n$ is $0.16$. Observations 9 and 22 exceed that cutoff, reproducing the lecture's qualitative comparison. Observation 22 has a moderate residual but substantial leverage, so its Cook flag answers a different question from a residual-only flag. Figure 9.1 makes that distinction visible across the full dataset. The dashed lines show the screening conventions stated above, and the source row numbers remain attached to the observations. Figure 9.2 separates changes to coefficients measured on different scales. 9.1. Diagnostic screens for the same fitted model can flag different aspects of a row. Dashed cutoffs prioritize investigation rather than automatic deletion. 9.2. DFBETAS preserves coefficient-specific information that a single Cook distance summarizes. Source R demonstration: delivery data and fit , diagnostic plots . Dataset description: robustbase delivery manual ; numeric data retrieval: Rdatasets delivery CSV . Exact Python code and executed stdout appear in the laboratory . 9.7 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — keep or remove? A valid delivery stop has high Cook distance. Does the diagnostic justify removing it? Solution No. Cook distance says the fit is sensitive to the row, not that the row is incorrect. Check its measurement, operational context, representativeness, and the prediction objective. Report a sensitivity refit. If unusual valid stops occur in deployment, removing them can make the fitted population less relevant. A different mean, variance model, or operating-regime analysis may be preferable. Conceptual — why two residual scalings? Explain why internal and external studentization differ even for the same residual. Solution The internal variance estimate includes the row being assessed. A large discrepancy can inflate that denominator and mask its own magnitude. The external version estimates variance after deleting the row. Under the Gaussian model, the deleted variance has the independence needed for an exact t calibration. The two formulas measure the same discrepancy against different estimates of its scale. Derivation — average leverage. Derive $\\sum_i h_{ii}=q$ and explain why an equally distributed leverage assumption is unnecessary. Solution Cyclic invariance of trace gives $\\operatorname{tr}[X(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}]=\\operatorname{tr}[(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}X]=\\operatorname{tr}(I_q)=q$. Dividing by $n$ gives the mean $q/n$. Individual entries can vary substantially while retaining this sum, so an equal-leverage assumption is neither used nor implied. Derivation — an internal residual cannot have an ordinary t law. Use the deleted sum of squares to bound $r_i^2$. Solution Nonnegativity in (9.6) gives $e_i^2/(1-h_{ii})\\leq\\mathrm{SSE}$. Divide by $s^2=\\mathrm{SSE}/(n-q)>0$ to obtain $r_i^2\\leq n-q$. Ordinary Student t variables have unbounded support, so this internally scaled statistic cannot have that exact distribution. A degenerate zero variance makes the diagnostic undefined rather than proving a distributional result. Computation — direct refits. Recompute observation 9's held-out error by deleting that row. Compare with $e_i/(1-h_{ii})$ and verify Cook distance by the norm of the full fitted-vector change. Solution The laboratory deletes each of the 25 rows in turn. It asserts equality between the direct held-out error and (9.5), and between direct coefficient changes and (9.4), at absolute tolerance $10^{-10}$. Substituting the recorded row-9 residual and leverage into (9.7) yields Cook distance $3.419318411$. This verifies the numerical method on these data; it is not a statistical test that the row is erroneous. Computation — compare the two flagged stops. Run the laboratory with --plots assets . Explain why observation 22 can cross the Cook screen while remaining inside a magnitude-two residual screen. Solution Its internal residual is $-1.449995414$, whose magnitude is below two. Its leverage is $0.391575218$, which magnifies a deletion effect through $h/(1-h)$. Equation (9.7) gives Cook distance $0.451045451$, exceeding the $0.16$ screen. The different flags follow the different definitions. The DFBETAS panels show which coefficients are most sensitive; they do not create a separate automatic-deletion instruction."}, {"number": 10, "title": "Categorical predictors and interpretable contrasts", "url": "chapter-10.html", "text": "10.1 A category is not a measurement scale A credit-balance model can include income and recorded student status. Income has a numerical unit; student status identifies groups. Replacing the labels No and Yes by numbers is a convenient representation, but it does not give the numbers an intrinsic quantitative meaning. The design matrix must describe which group comparisons the coefficients represent. The source uses the simulated Credit data distributed with R's ISLR package. Our downloaded file contains 400 rows and includes the numerical response Balance , measured in dollars, and the categorical predictors Student , Gender , and Ethnicity . A direct extraction of the primary package data was compared with the bundled file. These calculations concern that simulated dataset. They do not establish population facts about the recorded groups. Linear regression accommodates numerical responses and categorical predictors. A categorical response requires a suitable response model, such as the binary probability model developed in Chapter 17 . Predictor coding and response distribution are distinct choices. The presence of a category among the predictors does not require switching a numerical-response model to logistic regression. For a binary predictor, an indicator is sufficient. Define $d_i=1$ for one designated group and $d_i=0$ for the reference group. The conditional-mean model is $E(Y_i\\mid d_i)=\\beta_0+\\beta_1d_i$. Thus the reference mean is $\\beta_0$, the other group mean is $\\beta_0+\\beta_1$, and $\\beta_1$ is the modeled difference between them. The error term in the full response model remains present even when a table lists only the mean equations. Source discussion: Credit data and indicator coding . Data identity is determined by the actual package object and file, rather than the conflicting ten-thousand-observation description in the source/help text. 10.2 A binary-only fit estimates the two sample means Suppose each group has at least one observation. Let $n_0,n_1$ be the group sizes and $\\overline y_0,\\overline y_1$ their response means. The least-squares criterion separates as\n$$Q(b_0,b_1)=\\sum_{i:d_i=0}(y_i-b_0)^2+\n\\sum_{i:d_i=1}(y_i-b_0-b_1)^2. \\tag{10.1}$$\nPut $m_0=b_0$ and $m_1=b_0+b_1$. These are independent real coordinates: any pair of group means corresponds to one pair $(b_0,b_1)$. For either group, the identity $\\sum(y_i-m)^2=\\sum(y_i-\\overline y)^2+n_g(m-\\overline y)^2$ shows that its minimizing constant is its sample mean. Consequently,\n$$\\widehat\\beta_0=\\overline y_0,\\qquad\n\\widehat\\beta_1=\\overline y_1-\\overline y_0. \\tag{10.2}$$\nThis is a deterministic result. It does not assume Gaussian errors or equal group variances. For Student , the executed Python fit gives mean balances $480.369444$ for No and $876.825000$ for Yes. With No as the reference, the intercept is $480.369444$ and the coefficient of the Yes indicator is $396.455556$. It is a difference in fitted group averages, not a prediction that every student has exactly that additional balance. Individual deviations remain in the residuals. For the source Gender labels, explicitly taking Male as the reference gives means $509.803109$ and $529.536232$ for Male and Female. The Female indicator coefficient is $19.733123$. These are unadjusted comparisons. Adding income, credit limit, or another predictor changes the specified comparison, so the coefficient no longer equals a raw difference of marginal group means in general. With equal-variance independent errors, the variance of a binary-only difference is $\\sigma^2(1/n_0+1/n_1)$. This follows by writing it as the difference of two independent group means. Under unequal group variances, it becomes $\\sigma_0^2/n_0+\\sigma_1^2/n_1$ instead. The numerical group-mean fit is unchanged by this distinction; its uncertainty is not. A categorical encoding does not supply homoscedasticity. Source examples: Balance on Student and Balance on Gender . The independent group-mean derivation and variance distinction are developed here. 10.3 Different coefficients can represent the same fitted values Swap the binary indicator by defining $d_i^*=1-d_i$. Substitution gives\n$$\\beta_0+\\beta_1d_i=(\\beta_0+\\beta_1)-\\beta_1d_i^*. \\tag{10.3}$$\nThe new intercept is the other group mean and the new difference changes sign. The model space, fitted values, residuals, and sum of squared residuals remain unchanged. Changing the baseline changes the question named by an individual coefficient, even though all group comparisons can still be recovered. Another common coding uses $s_i=2d_i-1$, taking values $-1,+1$. Since $d_i=(s_i+1)/2$,\n$$\\beta_0+\\beta_1d_i=(\\beta_0+\\beta_1/2)+(\\beta_1/2)s_i. \\tag{10.4}$$\nThe intercept is now the unweighted average of the two fitted group means, and the coefficient is half their difference. This intercept need not equal the overall sample mean when group sizes differ. A grand mean weights observations; the midpoint of group means weights the groups equally. The executed Gender recodings produce $(509.803109,19.733123)$ for Female $=1$, $(529.536232,-19.733123)$ for Male $=1$, and $(519.669670,9.866562)$ for Female $=+1$, Male $=-1$. The laboratory asserts that all three fitted-response vectors agree. Coefficient equality is therefore the wrong criterion for checking two programs that intentionally use different bases. Proposition 10.1 — basis invariance, proved here. For a full-rank design $X$ and an invertible square matrix $B$, designs $X$ and $XB$ give identical least-squares fitted vectors. Their unique coefficients satisfy $\\widehat\\gamma=B^{-1}\\widehat\\beta$. Every vector $XB\\gamma$ equals $X\\beta$ for $\\beta=B\\gamma$, and the invertibility of $B$ makes this correspondence one-to-one. The two minimizations therefore search exactly the same set of fitted vectors. Substituting $\\gamma=B^{-1}\\widehat\\beta$ reproduces the minimizing vector, and full rank gives uniqueness. ∎ This argument explains equivalence of full-rank contrast systems. It does not justify replacing three categories by one arbitrary numerical score. A single score plus an intercept spans only a two-dimensional family of group means, whereas three unrestricted means require three independent coordinates. Not every recoding preserves the design-column space. Source recodings: swapping the reference and using minus/plus one . 10.4 Why an intercept uses one fewer indicator Let a categorical predictor have $K$ observed levels, each represented in at least one row. Write $I_{ig}=1$ if row $i$ belongs to group $g$, and zero otherwise. Each row belongs to exactly one group, so $\\sum_{g=1}^K I_{ig}=1$. Including all $K$ indicator columns and an intercept therefore creates the column relation\n$$\\mathbf1-\\sum_{g=1}^K I_{\\cdot g}=0. \\tag{10.5}$$\nThe resulting coefficient vector is not unique. Adding a constant to the intercept and subtracting it from every group coefficient preserves every fitted value. There are two convenient full-rank alternatives. Use all $K$ indicators without a separate intercept, so each coefficient is one group mean. Or use an intercept and $K-1$ indicators, taking the omitted group as the reference. The latter represents $\\mu_1=\\beta_0$ and $\\mu_g=\\beta_0+\\beta_g$ for $g>1$. If each group occurs, this representation is unique: the reference rows identify $\\beta_0$, and each other group's rows identify its offset. It spans the same group-constant fitted vectors as the no-intercept representation. The source's three Ethnicity labels are African American, Asian, and Caucasian. With African American as reference, the fitted intercept is $531.000000$, the Asian offset is $-18.686275$, and the Caucasian offset is $-12.502513$. The resulting fitted group means are $531.000000$, $512.313725$, and $518.497487$. The baseline is included through the intercept; it has not been dropped from the dataset. For $K>2$, replacing the category by the integers $1,\\ldots,K$ imposes an equal-step linear trend on these group means. It can be appropriate for a justified score, but it is a different model from unrestricted categorical effects. Likewise, an ordered category does not by itself establish equal numerical distances between its levels. Source multi-level example: three recorded categories and coefficient interpretation . 10.5 A faithful Python conversion preserves the design The laboratory converts all three source fits: Balance ~ Student , Balance ~ Gender , and Balance ~ Ethnicity . It uses pandas categorical types with explicitly ordered levels, then statsmodels formula least squares. Patsy's C(...) marks a predictor as categorical and generates treatment-coded columns. The coefficient vector is checked independently against the group-mean formula, rather than only comparing formatted summaries. The original file contains a leading space in the Male label. The program strips whitespace from this label and explicitly preserves the source reference order Male, Female. Without that order, trimming could change an alphabetically selected baseline. Student uses No, Yes; Ethnicity uses the three levels in the source order. These choices preserve the intended comparisons while cleaning a representation detail. A fresh R fit on identical category levels and observations agrees with the Python estimates at the recorded numerical tolerance. The Python program also checks reference reversal, minus/plus-one coding, and equivalence between the full-indicator and reference-indicator fits. In the three-category example, an intercept plus all indicators creates four columns with rank three. The redundant representation does not become identified merely because a solver returns some coefficient vector. With an additional numerical predictor $x$, a treatment-coded additive model has mean $\\beta_0+\\beta_xx+\\sum_{g>1}\\beta_g I_g$. Each group shares slope $\\beta_x$ and differs by an offset. The baseline intercept is the reference-group mean at $x=0$, not necessarily that group's observed average. If all values of $x$ are constant within each group, its column may already be in the indicator span, so checking group counts alone is insufficient to ensure the expanded design has full rank. At prediction time, preserve the fitted design's levels and contrast convention. A new category absent from training has no estimated group offset. Silently encoding it as the reference group assigns an unsupported mean. Either reject the unsupported input, define a justified pooling strategy before training, or collect the information required to estimate the additional level. The same rule applies when an apparently harmless data-cleaning step changes category spelling. Implementation references: Patsy categorical coding and ISLR Credit documentation . The bundled data were retrieved from the Credit CSV mirror and checked against the primary package object . All displayed values are computed in the chapter laboratory . 10.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — missing coefficient or missing group? A summary lists an intercept and only two coefficients for a three-level category. Has the reference group been omitted? Solution No. Its predicted mean is represented by the intercept. The other two coefficients are differences from that mean. Omitting a redundant indicator avoids the exact column relation in (10.5); it does not remove any reference-group rows from the fit. Conceptual — unseen level. A deployed model receives a category never observed in training. Why is coding it as all-zero indicators generally unsupported? Solution The all-zero vector encodes a specific known reference group. An unseen group has no estimated offset or evidence that its mean equals the reference mean. A deliberate pooling rule can define such behavior, but it must be part of the model and evaluation. An accidental encoding fallback is a new unsupported assumption. Derivation — group-mean minimizers. Derive (10.2) without differentiating a matrix criterion. Solution For each group, expand $\\sum(y_i-m)^2$ about its sample mean. The cross term vanishes because centered responses sum to zero, leaving a fixed within-group sum plus $n_g(m-\\overline y_g)^2$. With $n_g>0$, each term has its unique minimum at that group's sample mean. Solve $\\beta_0=m_0$ and $\\beta_1=m_1-m_0$. Derivation — a general binary code. A binary category is coded as two distinct real values $a,b$. Find the line whose fitted means are $\\mu_0,\\mu_1$ at those codes. Solution Solve $\\alpha+\\gamma a=\\mu_0$ and $\\alpha+\\gamma b=\\mu_1$. Subtraction gives $\\gamma=(\\mu_1-\\mu_0)/(b-a)$, and $\\alpha=\\mu_0-a\\gamma$. Distinct codes preserve the two-dimensional group-mean space. If $a=b$, group membership disappears and the difference is unidentified. This argument does not generalize to an arbitrary single score for more than two unrestricted group means. Computation — reproduce every source category fit. Run the laboratory and compare its coefficients with direct means for Student, Gender, and Ethnicity. Solution For each factor, the program computes the reference mean and subtracts it from every other group mean. It asserts equality with formula-model coefficients at absolute tolerance $10^{-10}$. Student's fitted difference is $396.455556$, Gender's is $19.733123$, and the two nonreference Ethnicity offsets are $-18.686275$ and $-12.502513$. These reproduce the source examples on the actual Credit observations rather than substitute data. Computation — compare bases rather than coefficient strings. Fit the three binary encodings and both full-rank three-category designs. What numerical invariants should agree? Solution The program compares fitted vectors at tolerance $10^{-10}$. Residuals and SSE then agree as well. The category-only group means also agree, although coefficients use different bases. For the redundant three-category design it reports four columns and rank three, exposing nonuniqueness. Comparing coefficient arrays without transforming their bases would falsely label equivalent fits as different. Indicator coding makes group offsets explicit. It also reveals what the additive model omits: different groups may have different slopes for a numerical predictor. Interactions and nonlinear features extend the design to represent that possibility."}, {"number": 11, "title": "Nonlinear features and interaction effects", "url": "chapter-11.html", "text": "11.1 A group difference can depend on income An additive credit-balance model gives students and nonstudents different intercepts but a common income slope. Its two fitted lines are parallel. If the association between income and balance differs by recorded student status, a constant offset cannot represent it. An interaction expands the mean model to allow the group difference to change with income. Let $x$ denote income measured in thousands of dollars and $d\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate student status, with $d=1$ for Yes. Define the conditional-mean model\n$$m(x,d)=\\beta_0+\\beta_xx+\\beta_dd+\\beta_{xd}xd. \\tag{11.1}$$\nAll four coefficients are real. The additive model is the special case $\\beta_{xd}=0$. Substituting the two indicator values gives\n$$m(x,0)=\\beta_0+\\beta_xx,\\qquad\nm(x,1)=(\\beta_0+\\beta_d)+(\\beta_x+\\beta_{xd})x. \\tag{11.2}$$\nThus $\\beta_{xd}$ is a difference of income slopes. It is not the correlation between the two predictors. Predictors can be independent in a balanced design while their product is necessary to describe the response mean. The modeled difference between student groups is\n$$m(x,1)-m(x,0)=\\beta_d+\\beta_{xd}x. \\tag{11.3}$$\nConsequently $\\beta_d$ is the group difference specifically at income zero. It is no longer a universal difference applying at every income. A nonzero interaction changes the interpretation of the lower-order coefficients because each is evaluated at a particular reference value of the other predictor. In R and Patsy formulas, Income * Student expands to the two main terms and their interaction. It is equivalent to Income + Student + Income:Student under the same category coding. Multiplication inside a data transformation and the star operator in a formula have related but different roles: the formula operator requests the lower-order terms as well. The laboratory checks that the two formula spellings produce identical fitted responses. Source discussion: Credit example and interaction formulas . Formula conventions are documented by Patsy . 11.2 Centering changes a reference, not a fitted surface A contrast evaluated at income zero may be far from the comparison of interest. Choose a reference income $c$ and define $z=x-c$. Substitute $x=z+c$ into (11.1):\n$$m(z+c,d)=(\\beta_0+c\\beta_x)+\\beta_xz+\n(\\beta_d+c\\beta_{xd})d+\\beta_{xd}zd. \\tag{11.4}$$\nThe group coefficient is now the group difference at $x=c$. The slope difference remains $\\beta_{xd}$, and every fitted value is unchanged. The laboratory centers at the observed mean income and verifies this identity numerically. Retaining the lower-order terms is essential to this invariance. A model containing only $xd$ imposes a zero effect for nonstudents and a line through the selected origin for students. Replacing $x$ by $x-c$ without adding the induced group and intercept terms generally changes its fitted space. The common practice of retaining main effects with an interaction therefore has a precise benefit: reference changes remain reparameterizations of the same family. For two numerical predictors, the model $\\beta_0+\\beta_1x+\\beta_2z+\\beta_3xz$ has partial derivative $\\partial m/\\partial x=\\beta_1+\\beta_3z$. An estimated slope depends on the value held fixed for the other predictor. The cross-partial derivative is $\\beta_3$. These are properties of the specified conditional mean; they do not convert observational associations into intervention effects. One can also express the interaction through a difference of differences. For two incomes $x_a,x_b$, subtract the group difference at $x_a$ from that at $x_b$. Equation (11.3) gives $\\beta_{xd}(x_b-x_a)$. This algebra is useful for interpreting the fitted surface. A causal difference-in-differences design requires additional assumptions and is not implied by the algebraic resemblance. 11.3 Nonlinear predictors can still give linear regression A mean $m(x)=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x+\\beta_2x^2$ is nonlinear as a function of the predictor but linear as a function of the unknown coefficients. The design has columns $1,x,x^2$, so the projection and least-squares formulas from Chapter 8 still apply. Linearity in coefficients is the relevant distinction for this calculation. At least three distinct predictor values are needed for these three columns to be linearly independent. If a linear combination $a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2$ vanished at three distinct values, that polynomial would have three distinct roots despite degree at most two; it must therefore have every coefficient zero. Conversely, only two distinct values cannot identify three arbitrary coefficients. Replication improves noise estimation but does not replace the missing predictor locations. The local slope and curvature are $m'(x)=\\beta_1+2\\beta_2x$ and $m''(x)=2\\beta_2$. The coefficient $\\beta_1$ is the derivative at zero, rather than the slope everywhere. A positive quadratic coefficient produces upward curvature; it does not imply that the mean increases throughout the observed range. The linear term can dominate its derivative over part of that range. Centering preserves the full quadratic model as well:\n$$\\beta_0+\\beta_1(z+c)+\\beta_2(z+c)^2\n=(\\beta_0+c\\beta_1+c^2\\beta_2)+(\\beta_1+2c\\beta_2)z+\\beta_2z^2. \\tag{11.5}$$\nThe laboratory checks the fitted-value equality. Centering can improve the numerical relation between lower-order columns, but it cannot create information absent from the predictor distribution. R's I(lstat^2) protects ordinary arithmetic from the formula parser. In Python's Patsy syntax, use I(lstat**2) . Writing a power directly in a formula without understanding the parser can request interactions or term expansion rather than a squared numerical feature. Orthogonal polynomial bases can span the same polynomial space while producing different coefficients; compare fitted values after confirming that their spaces and preprocessing match. Source polynomial example: Boston housing data and adding a squared predictor . 11.4 A richer fit needs a separate predictive assessment Adding a column to a least-squares design cannot increase its minimized training SSE: the larger design can reproduce the smaller fit by setting the new coefficient to zero. Therefore a lower training error alone is not evidence that the additional term improves prediction on new observations. An exploratory residual plot can motivate a term, but assessment must evaluate the resulting full procedure. The two Credit fits illustrate the distinction. The additive model gives an income coefficient $5.984335565$ and a student offset $382.670538843$. With an interaction, the nonstudent income slope is $6.218168737$, the student offset at income zero is $476.675843207$, and the slope difference is $-1.999150872$. At income values $20$ and $100$ on the thousands-of-dollars scale, the fitted student balance differences are $436.692825777$ and $276.760756056$ dollars, respectively. The adjusted $R^2$ changes from $0.273818883$ to $0.274428277$. The prespecified nested-model F calculation for the added interaction gives $F=1.333432372$ and $p=0.248891851$. At significance level $0.05$, it does not reject the zero-interaction restriction under the usual normal-theory assumptions. The nonparallel fitted lines in Figure 11.1 illustrate the model's permitted shape, not independently established predictive improvement or a reliably nonzero slope difference. 11.1. The interaction permits group-specific slopes. The plotted difference is a fitted association; the statistical comparison and predictive assessment remain separate questions. For the historical Boston data, the source uses lstat , a percentage-based socioeconomic score, to model medv , median owner-occupied value in thousands of dollars. The Python port uses the actual two source columns extracted from MASS, preserving all 506 rows. The linear coefficients are $(34.553840879,-0.950049354)$; the quadratic coefficients are $(42.862007328,-2.332821098,0.043546889)$. Training SSE decreases from $19472.381418326$ to $15347.243158382$. The scatter and residual pattern in Figure 11.2 motivate an expanded mean model. The quadratic's shape still extrapolates according to a particular polynomial, and the historical aggregate association is not a causal statement about a person or a contemporary housing valuation. The accompanying diagnostic panels reproduce the statistical roles of the source residual, Q-Q, scale-location, and leverage displays using Python. 11.2. A curved residual trend suggests that the single straight mean omits structure. The quadratic adds one feature; it does not guarantee adequate errors or validated extrapolation. The source also contrasts low-order models with interpolation. For $n$ distinct predictor values, a polynomial of degree at most $n-1$ can pass through all response values:\n$$P(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\\prod_{j\\ne i}\\frac{x-x_j}{x_i-x_j}. \\tag{11.6}$$\nAt $x=x_i$, its corresponding product is one and every other product is zero, proving interpolation. Distinct inputs make the denominators nonzero. Repeated inputs with conflicting responses cannot all be interpolated by any deterministic function. An exact training fit therefore depends on the design and is not a universal definition of a useful predictive model. 11.5 Python conversion and numerical witnesses The laboratory converts the additive and interactive Credit models, the alternative expanded interaction formula, the group-specific fitted-line displays, the Boston linear fit, the scatter and diagnostic displays, and the quadratic feature fit. The two source datasets are available offline. No source method in this recording requires an unavailable Python equivalent. Independent checks verify that the star and expanded formulas have the same fitted values, that income centering preserves the interaction fit, and that centering a full quadratic preserves its fitted values. A fresh R calculation on the same Credit and Boston rows provides an additional coefficient comparison. Reference-level and column-order transformations are made explicit before comparing arrays. The lowess trend in the residual display is a descriptive smoother; it is not part of either regression fit. Graphics defaults such as colors, selected point labels, and panel spacing differ between the two environments. The conversion preserves the numerical models and the diagnostic quantities rather than claiming a pixel-identical R interface. Exact Python source and saved output are included in the laboratory . Source comparisons: Credit fitted-line plots , Boston linear fit , diagnostic plots , and quadratic fit . Dataset definitions and historical source: MASS Boston documentation . 11.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — interacting or correlated? Does an income-by-student term assert that income and student status are correlated? Solution No. It specifies how their combination enters the conditional response mean. Correlation describes the predictor distribution. A balanced experiment can make its two predictors independent while the response contains their product. Conversely, correlated predictors do not necessarily require a product in a correctly specified additive mean. Conceptual — linear in what? Why can $\\beta_0+\\beta_1x+\\beta_2x^2$ be fitted by ordinary linear regression? Solution Once the observed inputs are fixed, the three design columns are known. The mean is their linear combination with unknown coefficients. Squared predictor values make the response curve nonlinear in $x$, not nonlinear in the estimated coefficients. Full rank, appropriate error assumptions, and numerical conditioning still need checking. Derivation — a contrast at a useful reference. Center income at $c$ in the binary interaction model and derive the student coefficient in the centered parameterization. Solution Substitute $x=z+c$. The terms involving $d$ become $(\\beta_d+c\\beta_{xd})d+\\beta_{xd}zd$. Thus the student coefficient at $z=0$ equals the original group difference at income $c$. The slope difference is unchanged. Retaining the main terms allows this to be a basis change preserving every fitted value. Derivation — the quadratic's reference slope. Show how centering a quadratic changes the intercept and linear coefficient while preserving curvature. Solution Expanding $(z+c)^2$ gives intercept $\\gamma_0=\\beta_0+c\\beta_1+c^2\\beta_2$, linear coefficient $\\gamma_1=\\beta_1+2c\\beta_2$, and quadratic coefficient $\\gamma_2=\\beta_2$. The centered linear coefficient is the derivative at the reference input $c$. Since $2\\gamma_2=2\\beta_2$, curvature is unchanged. The laboratory verifies the resulting fitted-vector equality. Computation — interpret the fitted Credit contrast. Run the source conversion and calculate the student difference at incomes 20 and 100. Does the line plot establish a statistically nonzero interaction at level 0.05? Solution Equation (11.3) with the executed coefficients gives $436.692825777$ and $276.760756056$. The nested test gives $p=0.248891851$, so it does not reject at level 0.05 under its reference assumptions. The plot illustrates different fitted slopes, not a separate significance test. Neither result alone measures held-out predictive utility. Computation — compare polynomial fits. Run the Boston conversion and confirm the SSE change. Explain why repeating this comparison over many degrees requires a different assessment protocol. Solution The linear and quadratic SSE values are $19472.381418326$ and $15347.243158382$. Adding the squared column enlarges the fitted space, so training SSE cannot increase. Trying many degrees and selecting the lowest training error uses the same responses repeatedly. A held-out or properly nested resampling assessment must repeat the feature-selection procedure inside training; evaluating only the finally selected fit on data already used to choose it does not estimate the intended new-data performance. Interactions and polynomial columns repair specific restrictions on the conditional mean. A remaining structured residual sequence can instead reflect dependence between observations. The next chapter develops that separate issue and shows why fitting a more flexible mean does not automatically repair uncertainty under correlated errors."}, {"number": 12, "title": "Autocorrelated errors and dependent observations", "url": "chapter-12.html", "text": "12.1 Correlation changes uncertainty before it changes a coefficient formula A regression fitted to observations in time order can leave long runs of positive or negative residuals. Those runs can indicate that adjacent observations carry overlapping information. Treating them as independent can produce an uncertainty estimate inconsistent with the actual sampling process, even when the mean relation is correctly specified. Let $y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon$ with fixed full-column-rank design $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$. Assume $E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=0$ and define $\\Omega=\\operatorname{Cov}(\\varepsilon\\mid X)$, a positive-definite covariance matrix. Ordinary least squares still uses $A=(X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$ and satisfies\n$$\\widehat\\beta_{\\mathrm{OLS}}=\\beta+A\\varepsilon,\\quad\nE(\\widehat\\beta_{\\mathrm{OLS}}\\mid X)=\\beta,\\quad\n\\operatorname{Cov}(\\widehat\\beta_{\\mathrm{OLS}}\\mid X)=A\\Omega A^{\\mathsf T}. \\tag{12.1}$$\nThe expectation follows from the conditional mean assumption; the covariance follows by linear transformation. Correlation between errors alone does not introduce bias into this fixed-design, mean-zero calculation. The distinction becomes important when predictors themselves depend on unobserved errors, as in some dynamic specifications. Then $E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=0$ may fail, and unbiasedness needs a new argument. It is that failure of exogeneity, rather than the mere presence of a nonzero covariance entry, that invalidates the expectation in (12.1). Nor must every variance be larger than a counterfactual independent-error formula. Dependence can increase or decrease uncertainty in particular linear combinations, depending on the design and covariance. A precise efficiency comparison holds between correctly specified known-covariance generalized least squares and other linear unbiased estimators under that same covariance; it is proved in §12.4 . Source discussion: correlated-error model and OLS moments . This edition preserves the conditional unbiasedness calculation and avoids treating correlation alone as proof of bias. 12.2 An AR(1) recursion does not stop correlation after one lag A parsimonious error model is the first-order autoregression\n$$\\varepsilon_t=\\rho\\varepsilon_{t-1}+u_t,\\qquad |\\rho|<1, \\tag{12.2}$$\nwhere the innovations $u_t$ have mean zero, variance $\\sigma_u^2>0$, and are uncorrelated across time and with earlier errors. The stationary solution has finite variance. The order one describes the number of directly included lagged errors in the recursion, not the number of nonzero autocovariances. Iterate the recursion $m$ times to obtain $\\varepsilon_t=\\rho^m\\varepsilon_{t-m}+\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\\rho^ju_{t-j}$. Under stationarity and $|\\rho|<1$, the first term tends to zero in mean square. The innovation series converges in mean square because the sum of innovation variances is finite. Thus\n$$\\varepsilon_t=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\rho^ju_{t-j},\\qquad\n\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2=\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_t)=\\frac{\\sigma_u^2}{1-\\rho^2}. \\tag{12.3}$$\nThe geometric-series denominator is essential; values $\\rho=\\pm1$ do not satisfy the stationary finite-variance argument. For nonnegative integer lag $h$, iterate forward from time $t-h$. The later innovations are uncorrelated with $\\varepsilon_{t-h}$, so\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(\\varepsilon_t,\\varepsilon_{t-h})\n=\\rho^h\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2. \\tag{12.4}$$\nPositive $\\rho$ gives positive correlations decaying with lag. Negative $\\rho$ alternates the sign. Unless $\\rho=0$, many lags are correlated. Higher autoregressive order similarly specifies a finite recursion, not a hard cutoff in marginal autocorrelation. For equally spaced observations, define $R_\\rho$ by $(R_\\rho)_{ij}=\\rho^{|i-j|}$. The error covariance is $\\Omega=\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2R_\\rho$. The innovation variance $\\sigma_u^2$ and marginal error variance $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2$ are different quantities. Confusing them changes both covariance entries and standard errors. Irregular time spacing requires a covariance model that uses the actual spacing rather than blindly treating every adjacent row as one equal lag. Source recursion and covariance discussions: AR(1) construction and lagged covariance . The stationarity condition and nonzero higher-lag correlations are explicit here. 12.3 What the Durbin-Watson statistic measures Let $e_t$ be the OLS residuals in their meaningful observation order, with $\\sum_t e_t^2>0$. The Durbin-Watson statistic is\n$$D=\\frac{\\sum_{t=2}^n(e_t-e_{t-1})^2}{\\sum_{t=1}^n e_t^2}. \\tag{12.5}$$\nConsecutive residuals with similar signs and magnitudes give small differences and a small numerator. Alternating signs increase those differences. Thus a small statistic suggests positive lag-one residual association; a large value suggests negative association. Values around two are a descriptive reference for little lag-one association, not a proof of the error model. Define $r_*=\\sum_{t=2}^n e_te_{t-1}/\\sum_{t=1}^n e_t^2$. Expanding the squared differences gives the exact identity\n$$D=2(1-r_*)-\\frac{e_1^2+e_n^2}{\\sum_{t=1}^n e_t^2}. \\tag{12.6}$$\nThe familiar approximation $D\\approx2(1-r_*)$ neglects the endpoint term. A lagged regression estimate using $\\sum_{t=2}^n e_{t-1}^2$ in its denominator is yet another convention; do not silently substitute it for $r_*$ in this identity. Each squared difference is nonnegative. Also $(a-b)^2\\leq2a^2+2b^2$, so summing bounds the numerator by four times the residual squared norm. Consequently $0\\leq D\\leq4$. This is an algebraic range, not a choice of significance cutoffs. The null calibration depends on the design and the sampling assumptions. Fitted residuals are a projection of the errors and are not an independent sample, so an ordinary sample-correlation t test with an asserted $n-1$ degrees of freedom is not a justified substitute. Lagged dependent variables, selection of the mean model, or an incorrect ordering require additional care. For a fixed, prespecified design under independent Gaussian errors, one can simulate the conditional null directly. Draw independent standard-normal errors, project each vector with $M=I-H$, and compute the same statistic. The unknown mean disappears because $MX=0$, and error scale cancels in the ratio. A lower-tail comparison screens positive serial correlation. This model-specific simulation does not repair an incorrect mean or an unmodeled sampling mechanism. Source statistic and direction: Durbin-Watson algebra . The source caption sequence contains conflicting verbal directions; the squared-difference definition fixes the interpretation. The Python function is documented by statsmodels . 12.4 Known covariance gives a precise efficiency result If $\\Omega$ is known and positive definite, generalized least squares minimizes $(y-Xb)^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}(y-Xb)$. Differentiation and full rank give\n$$\\widehat\\beta_{\\mathrm{GLS}}=(X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}y. \\tag{12.7}$$\nIts conditional expectation is $\\beta$, and its covariance is $(X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}$. These claims follow by substituting $y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon$ and multiplying the covariance factors. Gaussianity is not needed for these moment statements. Proposition 12.1 — known-covariance efficiency, proved here. Among estimators linear in $y$ and unbiased for every coefficient vector under this fixed design and covariance, GLS has a covariance no larger in positive-semidefinite order. Let $A_G=(X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}$. Any linear unbiased estimator $Ay$ satisfies $AX=I_q$. Write $A=A_G+B$, so $BX=0$. Since $A_G\\Omega=(X^{\\mathsf T}\\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}$, the cross covariance $A_G\\Omega B^{\\mathsf T}$ vanishes. Therefore\n$$A\\Omega A^{\\mathsf T}-A_G\\Omega A_G^{\\mathsf T}=B\\Omega B^{\\mathsf T}.$$\nFor any coefficient direction $v$, $v^{\\mathsf T}B\\Omega B^{\\mathsf T}v\\geq0$ because $\\Omega$ is positive definite. This is precisely the positive-semidefinite covariance comparison. Equality can occur, so the improvement is not necessarily strict. ∎ A whitening matrix $P$ satisfying $P\\Omega P^{\\mathsf T}=cI_n$ for a positive scalar $c$ converts GLS into ordinary least squares on $Py$ and $PX$. For stationary AR(1) errors, define\n$$y_1^*=\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}\\,y_1,\\quad x_1^*=\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}\\,x_1,$$\n$$y_t^*=y_t-\\rho y_{t-1},\\quad x_t^*=x_t-\\rho x_{t-1}\\quad(t\\geq2). \\tag{12.8}$$\nThe transformed errors are $\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}\\varepsilon_1,u_2,\\ldots,u_n$, which are uncorrelated and all have variance $\\sigma_u^2$. This proves the covariance identity and the equivalence of the transformed fit to GLS. The first-row scaling is the Prais-Winsten stationary transformation. Dropping that row instead gives the conditional Cochrane-Orcutt transformation and discards its information. An intercept must be transformed too: its later-row entries become $1-\\rho$, and its first entry is $\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}$. Transforming only the response or adding a fresh untransformed intercept fits a different mean model. Source transformations: Cochrane-Orcutt and Prais-Winsten matrices . Implementation reference: statsmodels GLS . 12.5 Estimating the dependence is an additional procedure Real data rarely provide the true $\\rho$. A feasible procedure starts with OLS residuals, estimates a lag-one coefficient, transforms the data using that estimate, refits, and repeats. One lag-regression estimate is $\\widehat\\rho=\\sum_{t=2}^n e_te_{t-1}/\\sum_{t=2}^n e_{t-1}^2$, provided the denominator is positive. It is not the same normalization as $r_*$ in the exact Durbin-Watson identity. The laboratory implements a transparent iterative Prais-Winsten example: it restricts the estimated coefficient to $[-0.98,0.98]$ to remain inside the stationary region, allows at most 100 iterations, and stops when coefficient changes are below $10^{-10}$. The restriction is part of this teaching implementation, not an estimate supplied by the source. Other packages use different convergence rules and treatments of nonstationary estimates; reproducing their output requires matching those conventions. The source also sketches Durbin's two-step approach. Combining a static mean with an AR(1) error recursion yields\n$$y_t=\\rho y_{t-1}+\\beta_0(1-\\rho)+\\beta_xx_t-\\rho\\beta_xx_{t-1}+u_t. \\tag{12.9}$$\nAn initial regression on the lagged response and current and lagged covariates can estimate $\\rho$ before a transformed fit. This requires an identifiable augmented design and appropriate innovation assumptions. The finite-sample augmented coefficients need not satisfy the nonlinear relation between the last coefficient and the product $-\\rho\\beta_x$. The laboratory labels its resulting fit as Durbin-initialized whitening rather than an exact known-covariance estimator. Once the covariance estimate depends on the responses, the GLS matrix is random. Proposition 12.1's known-covariance comparison no longer directly establishes an exact finite-sample distribution or efficiency claim for the feasible estimator. Uncertainty can require justified asymptotics or a resampling procedure that repeats covariance estimation. Choosing an arbitrary correlation value solely to narrow an interval does not supply that justification. Source initialization discussion: Durbin's augmented regression . An example of package-specific iteration rules is documented by the prais package ; the explicit teaching implementation is not claimed to reproduce every package default. 12.6 A reproducible ordered-data construction The assigned recordings develop theory and transformations; no R demonstration is identified in their complete caption tracks. The Python laboratory therefore supplies an explicitly original construction. It creates 80 ordered observations, a fixed varying predictor, coefficients $(2,3)$, stationary Gaussian AR(1) errors with $\\rho=0.7$, and innovation standard deviation $0.5$. Its seeded random realization is retained in a reference CSV for independent checks. Executed construction, distinguishing known-covariance and feasible procedures. Fit or diagnostic Result OLS intercept; slope $1.809371477;\\ 3.010666645$ Known-covariance GLS intercept; slope $1.872134462;\\ 3.046010793$ True OLS standard deviations $0.183708631;\\ 0.166056904$ True GLS standard deviations $0.181568046;\\ 0.157276753$ Durbin-Watson statistic $0.647275361$ Exact endpoint correction in (12.6) $0.076456569$ Iterated feasible correlation estimate $0.663376083$ The whitening fit and the library GLS coefficients agree numerically. The known theoretical covariance matrices satisfy the positive-semidefinite comparison, and the exact Durbin-Watson identity agrees with the library statistic. These are finite checks of the formulas on the construction. One realization's coefficient error is not a measurement of estimator bias, and a better covariance does not imply a coefficient must be closer to truth in every realization. A fixed-design Gaussian null simulation uses 9,999 independent draws and obtains no simulated statistic as small as the observed value. The reported plus-one Monte Carlo tail value is $0.0001$, not zero. Its interpretation is conditional on the prespecified mean design, independent Gaussian null errors, and the stated lower-tail alternative. It is not evidence that every aspect of the fitted response model is correct. Figure 12.1 preserves the ordered rows and exposes the relation between successive residuals. Randomly rearranging complete observation pairs leaves the OLS criterion unchanged but can change this diagnostic dramatically, because adjacency is part of its definition. A time-based diagnostic cannot infer a meaningful order from an arbitrarily sorted table. 12.1. Positive residual persistence in the constructed ordered sample gives a small Durbin-Watson statistic. The plot is a simulated teaching example, not a recovered course dataset. Run the standalone Python laboratory without R. The code states the first-observation convention, transformed intercept, lag-estimation denominator, stationarity restriction, and stopping rule. These details are part of the algorithm, not cosmetic implementation choices. 12.7 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual — what does order one mean? Does AR(1) imply that errors two observations apart are uncorrelated? Solution No. Under the stationary recursion their lag-two covariance is $\\sigma_\\varepsilon^2\\rho^2$, generally nonzero. The recursion directly conditions on one lag, but earlier innovations propagate through repeated multiplication by $\\rho$. Order describes the conditional recurrence, not a cutoff of the marginal correlation function. Conceptual — dependence and bias. Identify the assumption needed to preserve OLS unbiasedness when the errors are correlated. Solution For fixed full-rank $X$, require $E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=0$. Equation (12.1) then gives $E(\\widehat\\beta\\mid X)=\\beta$ for any finite covariance. Nonzero off-diagonal covariance entries do not alter that expectation. A predictor related to the errors can violate the conditional mean condition, but that is an additional issue. Derivation — endpoint correction. Expand the Durbin-Watson numerator and recover the exact boundary term. Solution The two sums of residual squares over $t=2,\\ldots,n$ and $t-1=1,\\ldots,n-1$ add to $2\\sum_t e_t^2-e_1^2-e_n^2$. Subtract twice the adjacent-product sum and divide by the full squared norm. The result is (12.6). Dropping the endpoint term is an approximation whose accuracy depends on its actual size. Derivation — transform the intercept. Show why the first observation receives a different scale in stationary Prais-Winsten, and state the transformed intercept column. Solution The stationary first error has variance $\\sigma_u^2/(1-\\rho^2)$. Multiplication by $\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}$ makes its variance $\\sigma_u^2$, matching later innovations. The first intercept entry receives that same factor. Every later difference of intercept entries is $1-\\rho$. Transforming the full design as well as the response keeps the coefficient vector attached to the original mean relation. Computation — known covariance versus estimated covariance. Run the lab and compare its GLS, whitening, and feasible fits. Which equality is an algebraic requirement? Solution Known-covariance GLS and the stationary whitening transformation must agree because they minimize equivalent quadratic criteria; the program asserts agreement at tolerance $10^{-11}$. The feasible fit estimates a correlation $0.663376083$ rather than using the generating value $0.7$, so its coefficients need not equal the known-covariance fit. Nor must conditional Cochrane-Orcutt agree, since it omits the first observation's criterion contribution. Computational design — calibrate the actual statistic. Why does the lab project every simulated null error vector, and why does it report a plus-one value after zero lower-tail hits? Solution Projection reproduces the dependence and constraints induced by fitting the fixed mean design. Computing DW on raw independent errors would simulate a different diagnostic. Under the stated null, the unknown linear mean vanishes after projection and scale cancels. The plus-one calculation is $(0+1)/(9999+1)=0.0001$, retaining a finite Monte Carlo tail resolution rather than claiming an impossible exact zero probability. If the original design had been selected from the responses, that selection would also need to enter the simulation procedure. Dependence concerns how different observations share unexplained variation. Normality concerns the conditional distributional shape. Neither can be diagnosed simply from the other. Chapter 13 next separates pooled response distributions, model errors, and the projected residuals to which a test is actually applied."}, {"number": 13, "title": "Normality checks and the assumptions behind inference", "url": "chapter-13.html", "text": "13.1 Which distribution must be normal? A regression can predict a response whose pooled histogram is strongly skewed. It can also fail when the pooled response looks normal. The distinction matters when a diagnostic determines whether we trust an interval for a coefficient or a future observation. A distributional assumption must identify the random quantity being modeled and what is held fixed. Let $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^n$ contain the responses, and let $\\mathbf X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$ be a fixed design matrix of full column rank. Its $q$ columns include an intercept when specified. The linear model is $\\mathbf y=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$, where $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q$ is the coefficient vector and $\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$ is the unobserved error vector. The Gaussian homoscedastic assumption is Definition 13.1 (conditional Gaussian model). Conditional on the design, $\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon\\mid\\mathbf X\\sim N_n(\\mathbf0,\\sigma^2\\mathbf I)$ with $\\sigma^2>0$. Consequently, $\\mathbf y\\mid\\mathbf X\\sim N_n(\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta,\\sigma^2\\mathbf I)$. Each response has its own conditional mean $\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta$. This does not assert that the entries of the response vector are identically distributed. A pooled histogram mixes observations with different means. If predictors are random, the marginal distribution integrates over their distribution and can be nonnormal even when every conditional error is Gaussian. For example, a binary predictor separates two Gaussian response distributions with different means; their mixture need not be Gaussian. The appropriate question is whether the conditional model and its errors describe the data adequately. Ordinary least squares does not require Gaussian errors to minimize squared residuals. Its conditional unbiasedness requires $E(\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\mathbf0$, not a normal distribution. Gaussianity supplies additional finite-sample distributional results, including the usual exact $t$ and $F$ reference distributions when the other assumptions also hold. A histogram cannot check exogeneity, and recentering an observed residual vector does not repair omitted-variable bias. In particular, zero residual mean follows algebraically from an intercept; it does not establish zero conditional error mean. The fitted residual vector is $\\mathbf e=(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)\\mathbf y$, where $\\mathbf H=\\mathbf X(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T$ is the orthogonal projection onto the design-column space. Residuals are observable, but they are not the original errors. Proposition 13.2 (proved here). Under the conditional Gaussian model, $\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf X\\sim N_n(\\mathbf0,\\sigma^2(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H))$. The covariance is singular whenever the model estimates at least one coefficient. Because $(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)\\mathbf X=\\mathbf0$, we have $\\mathbf e=(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. A linear transformation of a Gaussian vector is Gaussian. Its mean is zero and its covariance is $\\sigma^2(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)^T$. Symmetry and idempotence of an orthogonal projection reduce this to $\\sigma^2(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)$. This matrix has rank $n-q$, so it is singular if $q>0$. ∎ Thus $\\operatorname{Var}(e_i\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\sigma^2(1-h_{ii})$ and $\\operatorname{Cov}(e_i,e_j\\mid\\mathbf X)=-\\sigma^2h_{ij}$ for different observations. An off-the-shelf normality test calibrated for independent, identically distributed observations is not automatically an exact test of regression errors when applied to residuals. Dividing by a leverage adjustment helps compare marginal scales; it does not generally remove dependence. A model-based simulation can account for refitting and the design, provided the simulation reproduces the entire diagnostic procedure. Related source: Tests for Normality 1, 00:17 . The distinctions between conditional responses, pooled responses, and projected residuals are explicit clarifications of the model assumptions. 13.2 Quantile comparisons and their uncertainty A quantile-quantile plot compares the ordered observations with quantiles of a proposed reference distribution. It exposes where a mismatch occurs: the middle, one tail, both tails, or isolated observations. A histogram can hide these features through its choice of bin edges. Neither graph proves a distributional hypothesis. For a real sample $v_1,\\ldots,v_n$, write its order statistics as $v_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le v_{(n)}$. Choose plotting probabilities $p_i$ and theoretical standard-normal quantiles $z_i=\\Phi^{-1}(p_i)$, where $\\Phi$ is the standard-normal cumulative distribution function. Plot $(z_i,v_{(i)})$. If the sample is approximately $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$, the points should approximately follow $v=\\mu+\\sigma z$. The expected line is generally not the bisector $v=z$: response location and units determine its intercept and slope. The source R example uses qqnorm and qqline . Their conventions can be preserved in Python rather than replaced with a visually similar plot using different plotting positions or a least-squares reference line. R's normal plotting positions use $p_i=(i-a)/(n+1-2a)$, with $a=3/8$ for $n\\le10$ and $a=1/2$ otherwise. The default reference line passes through the sample first and third quartiles. For sample quartiles $Q_1,Q_3$ and normal quartiles $z_{.25},z_{.75}$, its slope and intercept are $$b=\\frac{Q_3-Q_1}{z_{.75}-z_{.25}},\\qquad a_0=Q_1-bz_{.25}.\\tag{13.1}$$ Here $a_0$ denotes the line intercept, not the plotting-position offset. NumPy's quantile(..., method=\"linear\") implements the interpolation convention used by R's default type-7 sample quantiles. The line follows from solving the two equations $Q_1=a_0+bz_{.25}$ and $Q_3=a_0+bz_{.75}$; it is not a regression fit to every plotted point. The lecture also uses car::qqPlot on a numeric vector. Its default normal envelope has a direct Python translation. To understand its scale, an empirical proportion near a probability $p$ fluctuates on the order of $\\sqrt{p(1-p)/n}$. The derivative of the normal quantile is $1/\\phi(z)$, where $\\phi$ is the standard-normal density. A first-order transformation gives the approximate standard error $b\\sqrt{p_i(1-p_i)/n}/\\phi(z_i)$. The package's default envelope is therefore $$a_0+bz_i\\;\\pm\\;\\Phi^{-1}(0.975)\\,\\frac{b}{\\phi(z_i)}\\sqrt{\\frac{p_i(1-p_i)}n}.\\tag{13.2}$$ This is an approximate pointwise envelope based on the fitted quartile line. It is not a simultaneous 95% confidence region for the entire quantile curve and does not incorporate every effect of estimating location and scale. The first-order quantile argument is least reliable at extremes and in small samples. The broader tails express lower precision of extreme sample quantiles; they do not make any observation acceptable by construction. An observation outside a band is a reason to investigate the model, its data quality, and its calibration. A heavy-tailed distribution often produces points below the reference in the left tail and above it in the right tail. A skewed distribution can bend asymmetrically. Several populations mixed together can produce a different systematic pattern. The diagnostic should guide a model revision tied to the data-generating mechanism. Applying transformations solely until a test stops rejecting changes the procedure and can undermine a claimed confirmatory interpretation. Related source: Tests for Normality 1, 05:35 . Implementation conventions were checked against R's quantile-plot documentation and the car package source . 13.3 Empirical distributions and the Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance The empirical cumulative distribution function records the fraction of observations at or below a value. For independent observations $v_1,\\ldots,v_n$, define $F_n(t)=n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\mathbf1\\{v_i\\le t\\}$. It jumps by the observed proportion at each distinct value. The one-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic compares it with a specified continuous cumulative distribution function $F_0$: $$D_n=\\sup_{t\\in\\mathbb R}|F_n(t)-F_0(t)|.\\tag{13.3}$$ The supremum is needed because the empirical curve jumps. Checking only the right edge of every jump can miss the largest discrepancy. For sorted observations and a continuous reference, an equivalent computation is $$D_n=\\max_{1\\le i\\le n}\\left\\{\\frac{i}{n}-F_0(v_{(i)}),\\;F_0(v_{(i)})-\\frac{i-1}{n}\\right\\}.\\tag{13.4}$$ To see why, between successive observations the empirical curve is constant while the reference increases monotonically. The greatest absolute discrepancy on that interval occurs at one of its endpoints. The two terms examine the right and left sides of the jump. In the laboratory, the constructed sample consisting of $-1,0,1$, tested against a fully specified standard normal, has distance $0.174678079$. The direct formula agrees with SciPy's routine. For independent observations from a completely specified continuous null, transforming each observation through $F_0$ gives independent uniform variables. This explains why the null distribution of the statistic does not depend on the particular continuous $F_0$. It does not mean that a normality test is valid without choosing or estimating distributional parameters. If the normal mean and variance were fitted from these same observations, the transformed values are no longer the independent uniforms used in that argument. Standard KS p-values then have the wrong calibration. A test specialized for a fitted normal family, such as a Lilliefors test, or a simulation that repeats parameter estimation can address this different null. Neither choice automatically solves the additional dependence introduced by regression residuals. Discrete observations and ties also change the continuous-null setting. These distinctions are more important than choosing whichever named routine returns the largest p-value. Related source: Tests for Normality 1, 11:40 . See SciPy's KS implementation and statsmodels' fitted-family Lilliefors test . 13.4 What the Shapiro-Wilk statistic measures The Shapiro-Wilk test compares the ordered sample with the shape expected from normal order statistics, while removing arbitrary location and scale. Its null is that the observations are an independent sample from some nondegenerate normal distribution. The statistic is not a probability, and a large value does not certify normality. Let $\\mathbf z_{()}$ be the ordered version of a sample of $n$ independent standard-normal variables. Define its expectation vector $\\mathbf m=E\\mathbf z_{()}$ and covariance matrix $\\mathbf V=\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf z_{()})$. Sorting introduces dependence: $\\mathbf V$ is not the identity matrix. Under a normal location-scale model, the ordered observed sample satisfies $E\\mathbf v_{()}=\\mu\\mathbf1+\\sigma\\mathbf m$ and has covariance $\\sigma^2\\mathbf V$. The source motivates a generalized least-squares fit of the ordered observations to their expected normal order statistics. Minimizing $(\\mathbf v_{()}-\\mu\\mathbf1-\\sigma\\mathbf m)^T\\mathbf V^{-1}(\\mathbf v_{()}-\\mu\\mathbf1-\\sigma\\mathbf m)$ leads to linear normal equations. Normal symmetry gives $\\mathbf1^T\\mathbf V^{-1}\\mathbf m=0$: reversing the order changes $\\mathbf m$ to $-\\mathbf m$, preserves $\\mathbf V$, and preserves $\\mathbf1$, so this scalar equals its own negative. The fitted scale therefore follows the direction $\\mathbf V^{-1}\\mathbf m$. Normalize that direction as $\\mathbf a=\\mathbf V^{-1}\\mathbf m/\\sqrt{\\mathbf m^T\\mathbf V^{-2}\\mathbf m}$. Then $\\|\\mathbf a\\|=1$ and $\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf1=0$. The idealized Shapiro-Wilk form is $$W=\\frac{(\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf v_{()})^2}{\\sum_{i=1}^n(v_i-\\bar v)^2}.\\tag{13.5}$$ Because the weight vector has zero sum, its numerator is unchanged by adding a constant to every observation. Multiplying every observation by a nonzero constant multiplies numerator and denominator by its square; reversing the order for a negative constant only changes the sign of the weighted contrast. Thus the statistic is invariant to location and nonzero scale. Cauchy-Schwarz applied to the centered ordered sample shows $0\\le W\\le1$ for a nonconstant sample. A lower value indicates a stronger departure from the normal-order pattern. The test rejects in the lower tail. The entries of $\\mathbf m$ and $\\mathbf V$ and the statistic's sampling distribution are nontrivial numerical objects. The laboratory uses the tested library implementation rather than estimating them by a small simulation and presenting that approximation as the standard test. SciPy documents its algorithm and the limitations of its p-value approximation; for samples larger than 5,000 it cautions that the statistic can remain accurate while the p-value need not. Our source dataset contains 60 observations, so that particular size limitation is not triggered. A p-value exceeding a chosen significance level means that this test did not reject at that level. It does not mean the null has been accepted as true, and it does not measure the probability that normality is true. A small sample can miss consequential deviations; a sufficiently large sample can detect departures too small to matter for a particular prediction. Distributional diagnostics must be interpreted with the purpose of the model, the sampling design, and the uncertainty calculation. Related source: Tests for Normality 2, 01:37 . The numerical contract is documented by SciPy's Shapiro-Wilk routine . 13.5 The ToothGrowth example in Python The source demonstration examines the len column of ToothGrowth . R's dataset documentation identifies 60 guinea pigs receiving vitamin C at different doses and by different delivery methods. The response measures odontoblast length; it is not a clinical trial of human dental treatment. The dataset belongs to R's datasets package. Loading dplyr is not required to obtain it. The companion Python laboratory reads an offline copy of the same public dataset. It converts the source sequence: histogram, normal Q-Q coordinates, quartile reference line, car::qqPlot 's default numeric-vector envelope, and shapiro.test . The histogram uses the Sturges bin-count rule; exact pretty-bin endpoints chosen by R are a plotting convention rather than a different statistical model. The Q-Q coordinates and quartile-line computations preserve the explicit R conventions. The envelope implements the inspected package formula, rather than substituting a bootstrap band with a different coverage meaning. Fig. 13.1. The actual ToothGrowth response has all 60 plotted values inside this approximate pointwise normal envelope. This is a descriptive comparison, not proof of Gaussian regression errors. The executed Python result is $W=0.967428644$ with $p=0.109100493$. At a significance level of $0.05$, the test does not reject normality for the pooled response sample. The quartile line has intercept $19.175000$ and slope $9.043874$. These values come from the stored laboratory output. Selected source frames at 16:44 and 17:25 confirm the car::qqPlot call and the displayed R Shapiro result W=0.96743, p=0.1091. A fresh R 4.5.1 reference run also matches the statistic and p-value within the displayed precision. As an explicitly authored extension, the laboratory fits a conditional model with an intercept, the indicator of delivery method VC , and numeric dose. The fitted coefficients are $9.272500$, $-3.700000$, and $9.763571$, respectively. Shapiro-Wilk applied to the residual vector gives $W=0.955382810$ and a nominal independent-sample p-value of $0.028091034$. This different result illustrates why a response test and a model diagnostic answer different questions. The residual p-value is labeled nominal because the residuals have the projection covariance derived in §13.1 . It is not presented as an exact regression-error test. All methods in this source demonstration have Python implementations. The conversion does not reproduce R's interactive plot identification or its precise graphics styling, neither of which changes the computed normality statistic. The remaining evidence boundary is that no full visual transcription of every on-screen command was performed. Named methods and data identified in the spoken demonstration are converted; any source settings visible only on screen require a separate visual check. Source demonstration: Tests for Normality 2, 12:35 . Data description: R's ToothGrowth documentation . The offline CSV was obtained from the Rdatasets distribution of the R dataset . 13.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: a normal-looking response. A researcher tests the pooled response for normality, obtains a large p-value, and concludes that a multiple-regression model satisfies its assumptions. Identify the unsupported steps. Solution The test concerns the response's marginal distribution, whereas the regression assumption concerns errors conditional on the specified design. It does not check the conditional mean, constant variance, independence, or omission of relevant predictors. A large p-value is failure to reject the stated null, not a probability that the complete regression model is correct. Examine conditional residual patterns and the sampling design instead. Conceptual: all points inside a band. Does containment of every Q-Q point in a nominal 95% pointwise envelope establish a 95% probability that the population is Gaussian? Solution No. Pointwise coverage concerns an individual quantile under a specified construction; simultaneous coverage concerns the whole curve. Neither is a posterior probability for a model. This particular envelope is approximate and uses a fitted line. Containment is a diagnostic observation whose meaning depends on that construction and the sampling assumptions. Derivation: residual covariance. Derive the covariance of residuals when the true errors have general covariance $\\boldsymbol\\Omega$ and zero conditional mean. Solution With $\\mathbf M=\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H$, the residual is $\\mathbf e=\\mathbf M\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. Therefore $E(\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf X)=0$ and $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf e\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\mathbf M\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf M^T$. If $\\boldsymbol\\Omega=\\sigma^2\\mathbf I$, symmetry and idempotence give $\\sigma^2\\mathbf M$. Error dependence or unequal variance cannot be removed merely by subtracting the residual mean. Derivation: bounds and invariance of $W$. Given a unit vector $\\mathbf a$ with zero sum, prove that the statistic in eq. (13.5) lies between zero and one and is unchanged under a positive affine transformation. Solution Set $\\mathbf v_c=\\mathbf v_{()}-\\bar v\\mathbf1$. Since $\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf1=0$, the numerator is $(\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf v_c)^2$. Cauchy-Schwarz bounds this by $\\|\\mathbf a\\|^2\\|\\mathbf v_c\\|^2=\\|\\mathbf v_c\\|^2$, the denominator. Both are nonnegative. Replacing observations by $u+sv_i$ with $s>0$ preserves ordering and multiplies numerator and denominator by $s^2$, while the added constant vanishes in both centered expressions. The laboratory verifies the transformation $3+2v_i$ numerically. Computational: both sides of an empirical jump. Compute the KS distance for the sample $-1,0,1$ against the standard normal, and explain why checking one side is insufficient in general. Solution Evaluate both $i/3-\\Phi(v_{(i)})$ and $\\Phi(v_{(i)})-(i-1)/3$ for each ordered point, then take their maximum. The laboratory obtains $0.174678079$ and agrees with stats.kstest . The empirical CDF has different left and right limits at an observation. A reference can be farther from the left limit than the right, so one-sided sampling of its jumps can miss the supremum. Computational design: calibrating a residual diagnostic. Describe a simulation calibration for a Shapiro statistic after fitting a fixed-design Gaussian regression. What must be repeated? Solution Hold the design fixed, simulate independent Gaussian error vectors, form simulated responses from a fitted null mean, refit the same regression, compute residuals using the same scaling rule, and recompute the statistic. Compare the observed statistic with the lower tail of these refitted statistics. Refitting reproduces the projection dependence. This is a model-based Monte Carlo calculation, not a remedy for an incorrect design or an unmodeled dependence structure. If variable selection was part of the original procedure, repeat selection too; otherwise the simulated procedure differs from the observed one. Normality diagnostics describe distributional shape under a specified sampling model. They do not determine whether coefficients can be estimated stably. The next chapter examines a separate difficulty: predictors whose columns contain almost the same information."}, {"number": 14, "title": "Multicollinearity, identifiability, and conditioning", "url": "chapter-14.html", "text": "14.1 When several coefficients describe the same fit A model can fit historical observations accurately while assigning unstable coefficients to its predictors. Household size and income may move together; macroeconomic measures may share a trend. If the data rarely vary one predictor while holding the others fixed, a fitted partial effect can depend strongly on small changes in the observations. This is a question about the geometry of the design, not the normality of the response. Let $\\mathbf X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$ be the regression design, $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q$ its coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf y=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. An intercept counts as one of the $q$ columns. The full-column-rank condition is a statement of linear algebra. It does not require the random predictor variables to be statistically independent. Correlated predictors are allowed. Exact linear dependence destroys identification; near dependence can make identification imprecise. Proposition 14.1 (proved here). If $\\mathbf X\\mathbf a=\\mathbf0$ for a nonzero vector $\\mathbf a$, then $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ and $\\boldsymbol\\beta+t\\mathbf a$ give the same fitted mean for every real $t$. The individual coefficients are not uniquely determined by that mean. Multiplication gives $\\mathbf X(\\boldsymbol\\beta+t\\mathbf a)=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+t\\mathbf X\\mathbf a=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta$. Therefore the squared-error criterion has the same value at every point on this line. No amount of arithmetic can distinguish those coefficient vectors from the observed design alone. ∎ The source's constructed columns are $\\mathbf x_1=(2,5,10,12)^T$ and $\\mathbf x_2=2\\mathbf x_1$. Without an intercept, only the combination $\\beta_1+2\\beta_2$ enters the mean. Their two-column matrix has rank one. Replacing the second column by $(4,9,19,25)^T$ gives rank two, as the laboratory checks. Its coefficients now have a unique least-squares solution, but uniqueness alone says nothing about their precision. At exact dependence, writing an inverse formula with a zero denominator does not produce a valid estimator with an infinite variance. The inverse does not exist. Software can drop a column or select one solution through a generalized inverse; that convention does not restore identification of the original coefficients. A particular linear combination is estimable only when it has the same value for every coefficient vector producing the same mean. A pairwise correlation matrix is useful but incomplete. One predictor can be nearly a linear combination of several others without having a near-perfect correlation with any single one. For example, three centered columns can satisfy $\\mathbf x_3=\\mathbf x_1+\\mathbf x_2$ even when the first two columns are orthogonal. Testing only pairs misses the dependence among the three-column set. Source: Multicollinearity 1, 07:59 . The notes distinguish stochastic independence, full rank, and the nonexistence of an inverse at exact dependence. 14.2 Singular values and variance inflation For a full-rank design, write its thin singular-value decomposition as $\\mathbf X=\\mathbf U\\mathbf D\\mathbf V^T$. The columns of $\\mathbf U$ and $\\mathbf V$ are orthonormal, and $\\mathbf D$ contains positive singular values $d_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge d_q$. Least squares gives $\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}=\\mathbf V\\mathbf D^{-1}\\mathbf U^T\\mathbf y$. A component of the response along the last left singular vector is divided by $d_q$. A small singular value therefore amplifies that component's contribution to the coefficient vector. Under conditional mean-zero errors with covariance $\\sigma^2\\mathbf I$, the coefficient covariance is $\\sigma^2\\mathbf V\\mathbf D^{-2}\\mathbf V^T$. Small singular values create large uncertainty in particular coefficient directions. The fitted vector is $\\mathbf H\\mathbf y=\\mathbf U\\mathbf U^T\\mathbf y$, which does not contain this inverse scaling. Thus large coefficient changes can coexist with much smaller changes in fitted responses on the observed design. Prediction away from the observed predictor relationships can still be unstable. The two-norm condition number is $\\kappa_2(\\mathbf X)=d_1/d_q$. For full rank, $\\kappa_2(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)=\\kappa_2(\\mathbf X)^2$. A threshold stated for one matrix cannot be transferred unchanged to the other. Column units and centering also affect this diagnostic. The determinant of $\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X$ is a poor scale-free diagnostic: changing measurement units changes its magnitude. Condition-number and VIF cutoffs are conventions, not distribution-free hypothesis tests. A more coefficient-specific measure follows from partial regression. For slope $j$, regress its predictor column $\\mathbf x_j$ on all the other design columns, including the intercept. Let $\\mathbf r_j$ be the resulting residual. It is the part of predictor $j$ that the remaining predictors cannot explain. For a full-rank design, $$\\widehat\\beta_j=\\frac{\\mathbf r_j^T\\mathbf y}{\\mathbf r_j^T\\mathbf r_j},\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\beta_j\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\mathbf r_j^T\\mathbf r_j}.\\tag{14.1}$$ To derive the coefficient formula, first project both the response and column $j$ off the remaining columns. The remaining least-squares problem has one predictor, $\\mathbf r_j$; its normal equation is $\\mathbf r_j^T(\\mathbf y-\\mathbf r_j\\widehat\\beta_j)=0$, because $\\mathbf r_j$ is orthogonal to every removed column. Substitution of $\\mathbf y=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$ gives $\\widehat\\beta_j-\\beta_j=\\mathbf r_j^T\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon/(\\mathbf r_j^T\\mathbf r_j)$, from which the variance follows. Write $S_{xx,j}=\\sum_i(x_{ij}-\\bar x_j)^2$ and let $R_j^2$ be the coefficient of determination from that auxiliary regression. Then $\\mathbf r_j^T\\mathbf r_j=S_{xx,j}(1-R_j^2)$, so $$\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\beta_j\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx,j}}\\underbrace{\\frac1{1-R_j^2}}_{\\mathrm{VIF}_j}.\\tag{14.2}$$ This defines the variance inflation factor relative to a predictor with the same centered sum of squares and no linear association with the remaining predictors. It measures information lost for a partial coefficient, not the causal importance of the variable. Dropping predictors solely until every VIF is small can change the scientific question and introduce omitted-variable bias. Source: Multicollinearity 3, 06:05 . Python's VIF routine is documented by statsmodels . 14.3 Changing the available information or changing the estimator A remedy must correspond to the purpose of the analysis. Additional observations help separate effects if they explore predictor combinations absent from the original data. Repeating nearly the same predictor relationship many times can reduce noise without resolving an exact structural dependence. Dropping a redundant column removes a rank defect, but dropping a scientifically relevant predictor can change the coefficient being estimated. Regularization changes the estimator rather than creating new observations. Principal-component regression first centers the numerical predictor matrix and writes $\\mathbf Z=\\mathbf U\\mathbf D\\mathbf V^T$. For $k$ retained positive singular values, regress the centered response on the first $k$ score columns $\\mathbf Z\\mathbf V_k=\\mathbf U_k\\mathbf D_k$. Transforming the coefficient back to the original predictor basis gives $$\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{\\rm PCR}=\\mathbf V_k\\mathbf D_k^{-1}\\mathbf U_k^T\\mathbf y_c.\\tag{14.3}$$ The score columns are orthogonal in the training sample. Their coefficient calculation therefore separates into scalar least-squares problems. Discarding small-singular-value directions removes the strongest inverse amplification, but can discard predictive information too. PCA orders directions by predictor variance without looking at the response. If the response depends strongly on a low-variance direction, retaining components solely to explain most predictor variance can miss the signal. Choose the retained dimension by an evaluation procedure tied to the response, fitting scaling and PCA within each training split. Ridge regression penalizes squared slope magnitude. After centering the predictors and response, minimize $\\|\\mathbf y_c-\\mathbf Z\\boldsymbol\\beta\\|^2+\\kappa\\|\\boldsymbol\\beta\\|^2$ for a chosen $\\kappa>0$. The intercept is recovered from means and is not penalized. Differentiation gives $(\\mathbf Z^T\\mathbf Z+\\kappa\\mathbf I)\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{\\kappa}=\\mathbf Z^T\\mathbf y_c$. The added positive diagonal makes this matrix invertible even when the centered predictor columns are dependent. Using the singular-value decomposition, $$\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{\\kappa}=\\mathbf V\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\\frac{d_j}{d_j^2+\\kappa}\\right)\\mathbf U^T\\mathbf y_c.\\tag{14.4}$$ Relative to the least-squares factor $1/d_j$, ridge multiplies direction $j$ by $d_j^2/(d_j^2+\\kappa)$. It shrinks weakly determined directions most strongly instead of abruptly deleting them. Under a correctly specified conditional mean and homoscedastic errors, its conditional expectation in each singular direction is the same shrinkage factor times the corresponding true coefficient. Its covariance is $\\sigma^2\\mathbf V\\operatorname{diag}(d_j^2/(d_j^2+\\kappa)^2)\\mathbf V^T$. The difference between the expectation and the true coefficient is bias introduced by ridge, not bias caused by multicollinearity in full-rank OLS. A coefficient's mean squared error combines variance and squared bias. Reducing one does not guarantee that their sum decreases for every true coefficient vector. As $\\kappa$ approaches zero, a full-rank fit approaches OLS. As it grows without bound, the centered slopes approach zero. Predictor scale matters because the penalty treats equally sized numerical coefficients equally; changing units changes what is penalized. State the scaling convention and how the penalty is chosen before interpreting a ridge output. Source: Multicollinearity 2, 02:09 for PCR and 06:54 for ridge. 14.4 Converting the longley and freeny demonstrations The source uses the longley dataset to predict GNP.deflator from the six remaining variables. This differs from the commonly demonstrated Longley model whose response is Employed . Choosing the familiar response would reproduce a different example. The offline Python data retain the R names and all 16 rows; only the CSV row-name field is removed. The laboratory converts the correlation and OLS/VIF workflow, calculates every VIF independently through an auxiliary regression, and verifies agreement with statsmodels. For the six predictors in the order GNP, Unemployed, Armed.Forces, Population, Year, Employed , the executed VIFs are approximately $1214.572149$, $83.958646$, $12.156386$, $230.912215$, $2065.733939$, and $220.419682$. For centered unit-norm columns, the design condition number is $120.625320$, while the normal-equation condition number is $14550.467756$. These are different conventions applied to the same data. The source then drops GNP , Population , and Year . Recomputing on the three remaining predictors gives VIFs $1.859355$, $1.757381$, and $2.277019$. The training $R^2$ decreases from $0.992647396$ to $0.981444579$. This numerical comparison describes the two fitted models; it does not establish that deleting variables improves out-of-sample prediction or produces a causal model. The source's ridge package uses a different default from calling a Python ridge estimator with its default penalty. Its inspected linearRidge implementation centers the data, scales predictor columns to unit Euclidean norm under corrForm , creates candidate penalties from principal-component fits, and selects among them through its effective-degrees-of-freedom criterion. The Python laboratory translates that path explicitly. For the full Longley example it selects a penalty of $0.0104691196008$ and a two-component candidate. Using that same scaled matrix and fixed penalty, an independent scikit-learn SVD fit agrees with the translated coefficient calculation. The caption's additional revenue-data example is consistent with R's freeny dataset. The companion program supplies the documented Freeny model, y on its four explanatory variables, as a transparent counterpart. It contains lagged quarterly revenue and therefore also raises dependence issues beyond multicollinearity. Its exact on-screen formula was not transcribed, so that entry is labeled a dataset-based reconstruction rather than a visually certified source-output match. A fresh R 4.5.1 run of the inspected package implementation also matches the translated automatic penalties and coefficients. All statistical operations used here can be expressed in Python. Remaining reproduction uncertainty concerns source settings and the exact additional formula, not an inability of Python to fit the models. Using different penalty selection, predictor scaling, a penalized intercept, or an arbitrary coefficient-sign convention would silently change the example. The conversion ledger records which conventions were recovered and which source details remain unverified. Demonstration: Multicollinearity 3, 09:07 . Dataset documentation: longley and freeny . Algorithm source: ridge::linearRidge ; independent fixed-penalty implementation: scikit-learn Ridge . 14.5 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: independence. Must two correlated predictors be excluded from an ordinary regression? Distinguish full rank from statistical independence. Solution No. Full rank requires that no design column be an exact linear combination of the others. It permits correlated random predictors. Near dependence can inflate partial-coefficient uncertainty, while exact dependence prevents unique identification. Whether to omit a variable depends on the estimand, data support, and prediction task; correlation alone is insufficient. Conceptual: pairwise screening. If three predictors satisfy $\\mathbf x_3=\\mathbf x_1+\\mathbf x_2$, why can pairwise correlations miss an exact rank defect? Solution If the first two centered columns are orthogonal and have equal norm, their correlations with the sum are $1/\\sqrt2$, not one. Yet the third column is exactly their sum. The null vector $(1,1,-1)^T$ proves rank deficiency. A multivariate rank or singular-value check examines combinations that a pairwise screen does not. Derivation: an estimable combination. In the source's exact toy example, which linear combination of two coefficients determines the mean, and why can it remain identified? Solution Since $\\mathbf x_2=2\\mathbf x_1$, the mean is $\\mathbf x_1(\\beta_1+2\\beta_2)$. The coefficient combination $\\theta=\\beta_1+2\\beta_2$ determines the mean uniquely because the nonzero column $\\mathbf x_1$ identifies its scalar multiplier. Adding $t(-2,1)^T$ to the original coefficient vector leaves $\\theta$ unchanged, even though the individual coefficients vary. Derivation: ridge bias. Derive the bias of a ridge estimator for a centered fixed design and mean-zero errors. Solution Let $\\mathbf A=\\mathbf Z^T\\mathbf Z+\\kappa\\mathbf I$. Then $E\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_\\kappa=\\mathbf A^{-1}\\mathbf Z^T\\mathbf Z\\boldsymbol\\beta=\\boldsymbol\\beta-\\kappa\\mathbf A^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\beta$. The bias is $-\\kappa\\mathbf A^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\beta$. It vanishes at zero penalty when the unpenalized problem is identified, or in particular zero-signal directions. Mean-zero errors do not make a positive-penalty ridge estimator generally unbiased. Conceptual: matching the source. If the Longley example were instead run with Employed as response, could its VIF list be compared directly with the source's list? Solution No. Changing the response also changes which column is excluded from the predictor matrix. VIF is a property of that predictor matrix, so it describes a different set of auxiliary regressions. Preserve GNP.deflator as the response for the source comparison. The supplied program lists its predictor order and verifies each VIF by direct auxiliary fitting. Computational design: PCR failure. Construct a response aligned with a small-singular-value predictor direction. Explain what a variance-threshold PCR fit can lose and how to assess the retained dimension. Solution For a centered design $\\mathbf Z=\\mathbf U\\mathbf D\\mathbf V^T$, choose a noiseless response proportional to a left singular vector that would be discarded. Its projections on all retained left singular vectors are zero, so truncated PCR predicts the centered response as zero even though full-rank least squares can recover it. Assess component count on held-out response loss, with scaling and PCA refitted within each training split. High explained predictor variance alone is not the required criterion. Collinearity concerns which mean effects the design can separate. A separate question is whether observations have the same error variance. The next chapter changes the covariance model and derives the corresponding weighting and uncertainty calculations."}, {"number": 15, "title": "Heteroscedasticity and variance-aware regression", "url": "chapter-15.html", "text": "15.1 A fitted mean does not specify the noise A house-price model may make larger absolute errors on expensive houses than on inexpensive houses. This need not imply that its conditional mean is biased. It means that a single error variance may fail to describe the observations. The distinction determines whether to change the mean model, the coefficient estimator, the standard errors, or more than one of them. For a fixed full-column-rank design $\\mathbf X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$, let $\\mathbf y=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$ and assume $E(\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\mathbf0$. Write the conditional error covariance as $\\boldsymbol\\Omega$. Heteroscedasticity means that its diagonal entries are not all equal. In the independent-error case, $\\boldsymbol\\Omega=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1^2,\\ldots,\\sigma_n^2)$. Nonzero off-diagonal entries additionally describe dependence, which is a distinct issue. Define $\\mathbf A=(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T$. Ordinary least squares has $\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{O}=\\mathbf A\\mathbf y=\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\mathbf A\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. Taking conditional expectation and covariance gives $$E(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{O}\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\boldsymbol\\beta,\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{O}\\mid\\mathbf X)=\\mathbf A\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf A^T.\\tag{15.1}$$ Mean-zero errors preserve unbiasedness. Unequal variances change its covariance and generally invalidate the conventional formula $\\widehat\\sigma^2(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)^{-1}$. They do not necessarily increase every coefficient's variance relative to every imagined constant-variance model: a comparison requires a specified reference covariance. The relevant efficiency comparison below holds for the same actual covariance and different estimators. Residual-versus-fitted plots can suggest a changing spread. Curvature can instead indicate a wrong conditional mean, and groups with different means can produce apparent fanning when pooled. Establish the scientific units and inspect plausible mean misspecification before fitting a variance model. Even with a correct mean, squared residuals are not direct observations of the individual error variances. Writing $\\mathbf M=\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H$, we have $E(e_i^2\\mid\\mathbf X)=(\\mathbf M\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf M^T)_{ii}$. Under homoscedasticity this reduces to $\\sigma^2(1-h_{ii})$; under heteroscedasticity the discrepancy from $\\sigma_i^2$ can have either sign. Interpret a residual pattern in the context of the fitted mean, leverage, and covariance model. Source: Heteroscedasticity 1, 05:29 , and the model discussion at 15:18 . 15.2 Whitening and generalized least squares Suppose $\\boldsymbol\\Omega$ is known and positive definite. This is stronger than being a covariance matrix: a covariance can be merely positive semidefinite and singular. Positive definiteness permits an inverse and a square-root transformation. Choose an invertible matrix $\\mathbf L$ satisfying $\\mathbf L\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf L^T=\\mathbf I$. Premultiplying the model gives transformed errors with identity covariance. Least squares on $\\mathbf L\\mathbf y$ and $\\mathbf L\\mathbf X$ minimizes $(\\mathbf y-\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta)^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}(\\mathbf y-\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta)$. Differentiating this quadratic and solving its normal equation gives the generalized least-squares estimator $$\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{G}=(\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\mathbf y,\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{G}\\mid\\mathbf X)=(\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\mathbf X)^{-1}.\\tag{15.2}$$ The covariance expression follows by inserting $\\boldsymbol\\Omega$ between the coefficient map and its transpose and canceling $\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\boldsymbol\\Omega$. It does not require normality. Normality supplies exact distributional inference when the covariance is known or known up to a common estimated scale. Proposition 15.1 (proved here). For the same design and positive-definite error covariance, GLS has covariance no larger than OLS in the positive-semidefinite matrix order. Let $\\mathbf B=(\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}$ and $\\mathbf C=\\mathbf A-\\mathbf B$. Both $\\mathbf A\\mathbf X$ and $\\mathbf B\\mathbf X$ equal the identity, so $\\mathbf C\\mathbf X=0$. Also $\\mathbf B\\boldsymbol\\Omega=(\\mathbf X^T\\boldsymbol\\Omega^{-1}\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T$, giving $\\mathbf B\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf C^T=0$. Expanding $(\\mathbf B+\\mathbf C)\\boldsymbol\\Omega(\\mathbf B+\\mathbf C)^T$ leaves $\\mathbf B\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf B^T+\\mathbf C\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf C^T$. For every vector $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf u^T\\mathbf C\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\mathbf C^T\\mathbf u\\ge0$. Thus the covariance difference is positive semidefinite. Equality is possible; strict improvement in every direction is not guaranteed. ∎ For independent errors, whitening divides row $i$ of both response and design by the standard deviation $\\sigma_i$, including its intercept entry. Equivalently, weighted least squares uses weights $w_i=1/\\sigma_i^2$ in $\\sum_iw_i(y_i-\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta)^2$. Dividing by the variance instead would apply weights $1/\\sigma_i^4$ and solve another problem. Multiplying every weight by one positive common constant preserves the coefficient estimates. In practice, variances are usually estimated. Feasible GLS replaces the true covariance by a fitted one. It does not automatically inherit every finite-sample property of known-covariance GLS, especially when weights depend on the same responses. A variance fit must remain positive. Modeling log variance is one way to enforce that; clipping a negative predicted variance needs an explicit rule. Diagnostics of a weighted fit should distinguish raw residuals from whitened residuals $\\sqrt{w_i}e_i$. Correctly specified heteroscedastic errors need not produce equal raw residual spread. Source: Heteroscedasticity 2, 05:13 . Weight semantics: statsmodels WLS . 15.3 Goldfeld-Quandt: a test with a specified ordering The Goldfeld-Quandt comparison is designed for a possible change in variance along a chosen ordering variable. Sort the observations by that variable, omit a chosen central block if desired, and fit the same regression specification separately to the lower and upper retained blocks. Let their sizes be $n_1,n_2$, their common full design rank be $q$, and their residual sums of squares be $S_1,S_2$. Assume $n_1>q$ and $n_2>q$ so both residual variance estimates have positive degrees of freedom. Under independent Gaussian errors with common variance and a correct mean model in both blocks, $S_j/\\sigma^2$ has a chi-square distribution with $n_j-q$ degrees of freedom. The blocks involve disjoint independent errors, so these quadratic forms are independent. Therefore $$F=\\frac{S_2/(n_2-q)}{S_1/(n_1-q)}\\sim F_{n_2-q,n_1-q}.\\tag{15.3}$$ This derives the reference ratio from two independent variance estimates. For an increasing-variance alternative, use the upper tail. A decreasing alternative reverses the relevant tail; a two-sided test needs its own p-value rule. Equal block sizes are convenient but not required. Choosing an ordering, central deletion, or alternative after examining the results changes the procedure and its nominal interpretation. The test does not detect every possible nonconstant variance pattern. The laboratory specifies an increasing alternative, 120 observations ordered by a constructed predictor, two retained blocks of 50, and 20 omitted middle observations. Each block estimates two coefficients, giving degrees of freedom $(48,48)$. The direct residual-variance ratio equals statsmodels' statistic: $F=10.129821645$, with p-value approximately $1.55730689\\times10^{-13}$. This is a seeded synthetic check of the translated algorithm, not a claimed reproduction of the lecturer's petroleum CSV. A heteroscedasticity-consistent covariance estimate offers another response to unequal variances when the mean model remains useful. The basic HC0 estimator substitutes squared fitted residuals into the diagonal covariance: $$\\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}_{HC0}(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}_{O})=(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T\\operatorname{diag}(e_i^2)\\mathbf X(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf X)^{-1}.\\tag{15.4}$$ This is an estimator, not an exact finite-sample identity. Its usual justification is large-sample behavior under independent observations, a correctly specified conditional mean, adequate moment and design regularity, and leverage that does not concentrate on a few rows. HC1, HC2, and HC3 alter finite-sample or leverage corrections. Such standard errors leave the OLS coefficients unchanged and do not themselves address serial dependence or an incorrect conditional mean. They should be named when reporting results rather than called simply “robust.” Source test: Heteroscedasticity 2, 00:16 ; implementation: statsmodels Goldfeld-Quandt . The HC discussion is an authored extension of the covariance identity. 15.4 Transforming units changes the question The source considers logarithmic responses and per-capita quantities as remedies. They can be useful when their scale matches the scientific mechanism. A multiplicative disturbance can become additive after a logarithm. A state total can share an obvious scale dependence with population. However, a smaller numerical residual after changing units is not evidence by itself that prediction improved. If a response is rescaled from dollars to thousands of dollars, its residuals shrink by the conversion factor with no improvement in the fitted information. A log residual and a raw monetary residual have different meanings. Likewise, a petroleum-total residual cannot be directly compared with a per-person residual by its numerical range. Compare predictions on the same target scale with a justified loss and an appropriate held-out sample. Changing a response to $\\log Y$ also changes the conditional quantity being fitted. Exponentiating an estimate of $E(\\log Y\\mid\\mathbf x)$ does not generally estimate $E(Y\\mid\\mathbf x)$, because exponentiation is nonlinear. Under a conditionally lognormal model with known log-error variance $s^2$, the conditional mean is $\\exp(\\mathbf x^T\\boldsymbol\\beta+s^2/2)$; this follows from the normal moment-generating function. The median is $\\exp(\\mathbf x^T\\boldsymbol\\beta)$. Retransformation needs a stated target and distributional or empirical correction. The source's per-capita petroleum model regresses consumption divided by population on price and vehicles divided by population. Dividing every term of a total-consumption regression by population would also divide its original intercept and its price term. Thus the source's model is a new specification, not an algebraically identical rewriting of the old one. Its interpretation and its residual variance must be assessed as such. Source transformations: Heteroscedasticity 3, 01:10 , petroleum models, 11:13 . 15.5 Python conversions and source-data limits The Python laboratory implements the four demonstrated workflows: home-price raw/log models and estimated weights; expenditure raw/log models; petroleum total/per-capita models and variance testing; and a market-share model with promotion indicators and group-based weights. It also checks known-variance GLS against explicit whitening and checks the covariance-dominance result numerically. The home-price data were recovered from an independently published Monash teaching dataset with 522 rows. The first source rows, original dollar and square-foot units, residual degrees of freedom, and fitted log-model coefficients agree with the inspected lecture frame. The log-price OLS coefficients are $(1.963614,1.219758,0.110340)$ for intercept, log home area, and log lot area. A superficially similar Penn State file contains 521 rows and different units; it is retained in the data provenance but is not substituted for the matched source example. Estimating standard deviations from absolute residuals regressed on fitted log price and applying reciprocal-squared weights gives $(2.376709,1.201356,0.083076)$. Fresh R and Python fits agree for the same recovered inputs and specified weighting procedure. The market-share data contain 36 monthly rows. The first source rows match the recovered public file after encoding the source's three promotion labels. The inspected R formula includes both a discount indicator and promotion factors, even though discount is determined by promotion category. Its five-column matrix has rank four and R aliases one coefficient. Python uses the equivalent full-rank basis intercept, Price, P1, P2 , preserving fitted values rather than claiming a unique value for the aliased coefficient. Its OLS coefficients are $(3.195916,-0.333584,0.308076,0.484311)$. The source's weights are reciprocal residual variances separately estimated within discounted and nondiscounted groups. Python implements that exact rule; the WLS fit also matches a fresh R run. Fig. 15.1. Diagnostics on the attributed public counterparts. The right panel uses whitened residuals; its vertical scale has a different meaning from an unweighted raw residual. The petroleum example was recovered from the textbook publisher's GAS10 table, with the 50 observations and columns PCON, PRICE, REG, POP matching the source table. The original-order total-consumption regression produces Goldfeld-Quandt statistic $4.537989153$ and p-value $0.000389445259$, agreeing with a fresh R lmtest::gqtest run and the source's rounded display. The total and per-capita regression coefficients are both reproduced in the stored output. The test's row ordering is preserved; a different ordering would define a different test. The original 20-row consumption-expenditure CSV remains unavailable in complete form. The Python raw/log-response workflow is implemented and exercised with a labeled synthetic fixture, but exact source coefficients and plots cannot be reproduced from an incomplete table. This is a missing-input limitation, not an unavailable Python statistical method. No fabricated replacement is presented as the original data. Demonstrations: Heteroscedasticity 3 . Data recovery sources: Monash real-estate modeling example , Penn State market-share data , and the Pearson chapter-10 data archive containing GAS10 . 15.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: a smaller residual. A log-response regression has numerically smaller residuals than a raw-response regression. Has predictive accuracy improved? Solution The units and loss have changed. Convert predictions to the same stated target scale, account for the conditional quantity being predicted, and compare an appropriate held-out loss. A reduction in numerical residual magnitude across incompatible units does not establish improvement. Conceptual: zero correlations. Does a diagonal covariance matrix imply independent errors? Solution It implies pairwise uncorrelated errors. Independence is stronger. Joint Gaussian errors with diagonal covariance are independent; without that distributional condition, zero covariances alone do not establish independence. The GQ derivation explicitly uses independent Gaussian blocks. Derivation: the correct weight. Show the equivalence between dividing each row by $\\sigma_i$ and weighted least squares with $w_i=1/\\sigma_i^2$. Solution The transformed residual is $(y_i-\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta)/\\sigma_i$. Its square equals $w_i(y_i-\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta)^2$. Summing gives the same objective. Every design entry, including the intercept, is transformed. A row transformation by $1/\\sigma_i^2$ would square again and use a different weight. Derivation: a common weight factor. Prove that multiplying all positive weights by a constant leaves the WLS coefficients unchanged. Solution Replacing the weight matrix $\\mathbf W$ by $c\\mathbf W$ changes the coefficient expression to $(c\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf X)^{-1}c\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf y$. The scalar cancels. Estimated uncertainty still requires an explicit convention for whether weights represent known absolute precision or precision up to a fitted scale. Computational: verify GQ. Recalculate the laboratory's GQ statistic from two separate regressions and explain its degrees of freedom. Solution Fit the first 50 and last 50 observations after omitting the central 20. Each two-column fit has residual degrees of freedom 48. Divide the upper-block residual mean square by the lower-block value. The executed ratio $10.129821645$ matches the package statistic and the upper-tail $F_{48,48}$ calculation. Computational: efficiency without simulation. Check GLS's covariance advantage using the known variances in the laboratory. What does it establish? Solution Compute both covariance matrices from eqs. (15.1) and (15.2), subtract, and check that the eigenvalues are nonnegative within floating-point tolerance. The true slope variances are $0.050811693$ for OLS and $0.022485608$ for GLS. This verifies the finite constructed case and the algebraic implementation. It is not evidence that estimated weights always improve a real dataset."}, {"number": 16, "title": "Model selection, evaluation, and an end-to-end case study", "url": "chapter-16.html", "text": "16.1 The fitted procedure is what must be evaluated Adding a predictor cannot increase the minimum training residual sum of squares in a nested least-squares model. That fact alone cannot decide whether the added predictor improves prediction. It describes an optimization on observations already used to fit the model. The object deployed on new data includes the choice of variables, transformations, scaling, component count, and any tuning. An evaluation that treats those choices as fixed after looking at the test responses evaluates a different procedure. Let a full-rank fixed design $\\mathbf X$ have $n$ rows and $q$ columns. Suppose the conditional mean belongs to its column space and the error covariance is $\\sigma^2\\mathbf I$. With projection $\\mathbf H$ onto that space, the training residual is $(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. The expected training squared-error sum is $\\sigma^2\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf I-\\mathbf H)=(n-q)\\sigma^2$. This follows from $E(\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon^T\\mathbf M\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon)=\\sigma^2\\operatorname{tr}\\mathbf M$ for a deterministic matrix $\\mathbf M$ and zero-mean errors. Now obtain independent new responses at the same design points, $\\mathbf y_{new}=\\mathbf X\\boldsymbol\\beta+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon_{new}$. Their prediction error using the original fitted mean is $\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon_{new}-\\mathbf H\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon$. Independence gives covariance $\\sigma^2(\\mathbf I+\\mathbf H)$, so its expected squared-error sum is $(n+q)\\sigma^2$. The difference is $2q\\sigma^2$. This is a proved optimism calculation for a fixed, correctly specified, homoscedastic mean model. It is not an exact correction for every variable-selection algorithm or an arbitrary misspecified model. Its mechanism is useful: a fitted model uses random noise in its training data to reduce training loss. The same noise-fitting does not predict a new disturbance. A held-out sample can assess a complete fitted procedure. If preprocessing estimates a mean, scale, rotation, or missing-value rule, estimate it on the training subset. If a model is chosen by comparing candidate losses, perform that comparison inside the training portion or an inner validation loop. Preserve an outer test set for the final assessment. Repeatedly choosing methods by their test results makes those results part of selection. A new independent assessment would then be required. The source introduces information-criterion selection at Variable Selection and Model Evaluation, 00:16 . The fixed-design optimism proof and explicit held-out workflow are authored extensions. 16.2 AIC, BIC, adjusted fit, and nested comparisons For independent Gaussian errors with unknown variance and a full-rank mean model, write $S$ for the fitted residual sum of squares. Assume $n>q$ and $S>0$, so the fitted variance is positive and the maximized likelihood is finite. The variance maximum-likelihood estimator is $S/n$, while the unbiased residual variance estimator is $S/(n-q)$. Substituting the maximum-likelihood estimator into the Gaussian log likelihood gives $$-2\\widehat\\ell=n\\{\\log(2\\pi)+1+\\log(S/n)\\}.\\tag{16.1}$$ There are $K=q+1$ freely fitted likelihood parameters if the variance is counted alongside the $q$ regression coefficients. The usual likelihood-based criteria are $\\mathrm{AIC}=-2\\widehat\\ell+2K$ and $\\mathrm{BIC}=-2\\widehat\\ell+K\\log n$. Their broader statistical motivations involve assumptions and approximations beyond this substitution; neither formula by itself proves that its selected model is the true model. For comparing unweighted Gaussian mean models on the same response rows, R's extractAIC.lm uses $n\\log(S/n)+2q$. It omits common likelihood constants and does not count the estimated scale in its degrees-of-freedom term. Consequently, its printed number differs from AIC(lm(...)) by a constant common to these candidate models. A Python conversion must match the intended convention before comparing displayed scores. Rankings remain the same when the difference really is constant across candidates. The logarithm in $n\\log(S/n)$ cannot be replaced by $S/n$ when the variance is unknown. BIC's per-parameter penalty exceeds AIC's when $\\log n>2$. This inequality has the opposite direction from saying that the logarithm becomes smaller as the sample grows. A heavier penalty can favor a simpler model, but the selected answer still depends on likelihood changes and the candidate search. With an intercept, define $S_{tot}=\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2$ and assume $S_{tot}>0$. Then $R^2=1-S/S_{tot}$ and, for $n>q$, adjusted $R^2=1-[S/(n-q)]/[S_{tot}/(n-1)]$. Its degrees-of-freedom correction can decrease when a predictor is added. It remains a statistic computed on fitted data, rather than an untouched estimate of generalization error. For prespecified nested Gaussian homoscedastic models with ranks $q_R<q_F<n$ and residual sums $S_R,S_F$, the classical comparison is $$F=\\frac{(S_R-S_F)/(q_F-q_R)}{S_F/(n-q_F)}.\\tag{16.2}$$ Under the reduced model, the extra fitted component and full residual component are independent orthogonal Gaussian projections. Their scaled squared lengths have chi-square distributions with the numerator and denominator degrees of freedom, yielding an exact $F_{q_F-q_R,n-q_F}$ reference. If the reduced model was chosen by first inspecting these same outcomes, this prespecified-test calibration no longer describes the entire selection procedure. A large p-value does not prove excluded coefficients are zero. Implementation conventions: R extractAIC ; R step . The exact score derivation above corrects caption ambiguities while retaining the source selection problem. 16.3 A greedy search and a reproducible source example Backward stepwise selection begins from a specified upper model. At each step it compares deleting one permitted term with retaining the current model, chooses the best score, and stops when no permitted deletion improves that score. Forward selection begins from a lower model, compares allowed additions, and stops by the analogous rule. A search involving interactions must respect the chosen hierarchy of terms. The supplied Python implementation handles the single-column main-effect scope used in the source example. This is a greedy local search. It does not generally examine every subset or guarantee the globally smallest score. Forward and backward paths can terminate at different models. All candidate fits must use the same observation rows; otherwise a score change can reflect a changed response sample instead of a changed model. Missing-value filtering therefore precedes the candidate search and uses one documented rule. The source's air-pollution table was recovered through the pollution data in SMPracticals . It has 60 metropolitan observations and 15 predictors, with mortality as the response. Matching the source's full-model score and both complete selection paths provides a stronger identification than a similar dataset title. The original field order maps to source labels $X_1,\\ldots,X_{15}$ as precipitation, January temperature, July temperature, older-population percentage, household size, education, housing, density, non-white population percentage, white-collar employment, poverty, hydrocarbon pollution, nitric-oxide pollution, sulphur-dioxide pollution, and humidity. The executed full-model extractAIC score is $439.787096634$, matching the source's displayed $439.79$. Backward deletion ends with predictors numbered $1,2,3,4,5,6,9,12,13$, score $429.633968121$, and adjusted $R^2=0.713883873$. Forward addition follows $9,6,2,14,1,3,5$, ending with score $428.828918239$ and adjusted $R^2=0.709841820$. A fresh R reference run reproduces the same retained terms and scores. The complete paths are in the laboratory and its stored output. These historical aggregate variables describe associations across metropolitan areas. Stepwise inclusion does not establish individual-level effects or causal impacts of the selected socioeconomic variables. Their coding and historical measurement context remain part of their interpretation. The program's additional held-out example is explicitly an authored evaluation demonstration; it is not a source claim that the historical data establish a useful forecasting system. Source: selection demonstration, 16:12 . Data description and original study attribution: SMPracticals pollution documentation . 16.4 A case study connects fitting to diagnostics The second recording starts with R's women dataset. Its 15 rows contain average weights associated with heights, rather than 15 independently sampled individual patients. The Python conversion fits the same raw linear, quadratic, and cubic powers. Adjusted $R^2$ increases from $0.990318281$ to $0.999385928$ and $0.999722156$. The linear slope is $3.45$; adding a quadratic term changes the linear coefficient to approximately $-7.348319$ because that coefficient now describes the slope contribution at a different algebraic reference. It does not imply that the fitted curve decreases over the observed heights. Its derivative includes the quadratic term. The state case uses state.x77 with murder rate as response and Population, Income, Illiteracy, and Frost as predictors. The conversion preserves those columns, computes confidence intervals, VIF, leverage, Cook distance, externally studentized residuals, and the diagnostic plots. A reduced model retains Population and Illiteracy. Comparing it with the full model gives $F=0.006108462$ with p-value $0.993910981$. Backward AIC selection also retains that pair. These are the actual reproduced calculations, not proof that omitted variables have no association under every model. Fig. 16.1. The reproduced state-model diagnostics separate mean pattern, distributional shape, spread and influence. Marker size in the lower-right panel reflects Cook distance. Nevada's externally studentized residual is $3.542928636$; a two-sided individual residual test with a Bonferroni adjustment across 50 states gives p-value $0.047544180$. This is an investigative diagnostic, not a reason to remove a valid state automatically. Deletion changes the population represented by the analysis. Examine data quality and scientific model fit before choosing a revised analysis. The source also reports a Durbin-Watson procedure. The preserved state ordering gives statistic $2.317691198$. A residual-resampling implementation with an explicit new seed produces a simulated two-sided p-value; it is not expected to match the source's unreported random seed exactly. More fundamentally, alphabetic state order is not a time axis. A serial-correlation interpretation based on that arbitrary order is unsupported. Spatial or regional dependence would need an appropriate structure. The laboratory includes component-plus-residual plots, a conditional Box-Cox profile and a small exhaustive subset comparison using Mallows' $C_p$. The Box-Cox family for positive responses is $(y^\\lambda-1)/\\lambda$, with logarithm as the continuous limit at zero. Profiling its Gaussian regression likelihood requires the transformation Jacobian; fitting transformed responses and comparing only residual sums on incompatible scales omits that term. The fitted conditional profile power is $0.865322$. Such a fitted power and a hypothesis that power one is adequate are different reported quantities. For predictor $j$, a component-plus-residual plot places $e_i+\\widehat\\beta_j x_{ij}$ against $x_{ij}$. Adding back its fitted component lets departures from a straight partial relation appear. For a candidate with $q_C$ coefficients including its intercept, the subset comparison uses $C_p=S_C/s_F^2-n+2q_C$, where $S_C$ is its residual sum of squares and $s_F^2$ is the full model's residual variance estimate. The independent, homoscedastic, correctly specified full mean model makes $s_F^2$ a suitable variance reference. $C_p$ is a selection criterion, not a causal test. The graphical counterparts retain the diagnostic quantities. The Q-Q implementation follows the inspected car linear-model method: Student reference quantiles for externally studentized residuals, a robust reference line, Gaussian response simulation with refitting, and pointwise quantiles across simulated ordered residuals. Its explicit new random seed cannot reproduce an unknown source seed point for point. Interactive label placement and exact robust-fitting tolerances are not asserted to be identical. Unreported source settings are not silently represented as exact output agreement. Source: women example, 00:44 and state case, 05:20 . The R dataset documentation clarifies the aggregated women measurements. 16.5 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: a global optimum. Forward and backward AIC selection return different subsets. Must one implementation be wrong? Solution No. They search different neighboring models from different starting points. Each may stop at a local optimum under its allowed moves. The recovered pollution example reproduces two distinct source paths and final subsets. An exhaustive search over the same candidate scope is a different algorithm. Conceptual: after selection. Why do ordinary coefficient p-values from the selected model not automatically have their prespecified-model interpretation? Solution The responses helped select the model whose coefficients are then tested. The sampling distribution of the complete procedure includes that search, while the conventional table conditions on a fixed specification. Use an inferential method designed for selection, an independent confirmation sample, or report the table as descriptive rather than treating it as untouched confirmatory evidence. Derivation: optimism. Derive the expected difference between new-response and training squared-error sums in the fixed-design setting of §16.1 . Solution The residual projection has rank $n-q$, giving expected training error $(n-q)\\sigma^2$. The new response error combines independent new noise and projected training noise, with covariance $\\sigma^2(\\mathbf I+\\mathbf H)$ and expected squared norm $(n+q)\\sigma^2$. Subtraction gives $2q\\sigma^2$. A data-dependent selected projection is not the same fixed matrix, so that proof cannot be transferred unchanged. Derivation: a score constant. Why can R's extractAIC.lm and a likelihood-based Gaussian AIC print different values but rank the same fixed-row models? Solution The maximized negative twice log likelihood is $n\\log(S/n)+n\\{1+\\log(2\\pi)\\}$. Counting the fitted variance adds another constant penalty across these models. If all models use the same unweighted responses and sample size, those omitted terms are common. Subtraction preserves score differences. Changing rows, likelihood families, or weights can invalidate the common-constant argument. Computational: source reproduction. Recover the first backward deletion and explain why the displayed two-decimal tie is insufficient to choose among later candidates. Solution The complete Python path first removes predictor 11, matching the source. Compare full-precision scores for every candidate deletion; the rounded printed values can conceal a strict difference. Preserve the documented candidate order for genuine ties. The code verifies both final source subsets, and a fresh R run agrees with their scores. Computational design: a valid comparison. Specify a held-out assessment of a pipeline that includes variable selection and PCA. Where should each fitted operation occur? Solution Reserve an outer test set before comparing choices. In the training set, fit any imputation, centering, scaling and PCA within each inner training split, choose the component count and variables using inner validation, then refit the chosen complete pipeline on the outer training data. Apply that fitted pipeline once to the outer test features. Evaluate the specified response loss without selecting another pipeline from that test result. Match the split to grouping or time structure when applicable."}, {"number": 17, "title": "Odds ratios and logistic regression", "url": "chapter-17.html", "text": "17.1 Odds describe a different ratio from risk A binary prediction task asks for the probability of a specified outcome, such as default or an upward market move. Encoding that outcome as one and its complement as zero makes the positive event explicit. The encoding is part of the model contract: reversing it changes the sign of log-odds coefficients and reverses the interpretation of a reported odds ratio. For an event with probability $p\\in(0,1)$, its odds are $p/(1-p)$. For two groups with outcome probabilities $p_1,p_0$, the risk ratio is $p_1/p_0$, while the odds ratio is $[p_1/(1-p_1)]/[p_0/(1-p_0)]$. A threefold odds ratio is not generally a threefold probability ratio. The two can be close when both outcome probabilities are small, but that is an approximation with conditions. For a table with rows $X=1,0$ and columns $Y=1,0$, write cell counts as $\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$. The within-row estimated outcome odds are $a/b$ and $c/d$, so the cross-product estimate is $\\widehat{OR}=ad/(bc)$. The source's illustrative counts correspond to $(a,b,c,d)=(688,650,21,59)$ and give $\\widehat{OR}=2.973772894$. These are reproduced source arithmetic, not newly verified epidemiological evidence or a treatment recommendation. Under an appropriate large-sample count model with positive cell counts, a delta-method approximation gives $\\operatorname{SE}(\\log\\widehat{OR})\\approx\\sqrt{1/a+1/b+1/c+1/d}$. One way to see its origin is to approximate independent Poisson cell-count variances by their means. The derivative of $\\log a-\\log b-\\log c+\\log d$ with respect to each count is its reciprocal with the corresponding sign; multiplying squared derivatives by the count variances and summing gives the expression. The laboratory obtains $0.259923354$. This is an asymptotic standard error; zero or sparse cells require a different treatment rather than dividing by zero. The odds ratio has a useful case-control sampling property under explicit conditions. Suppose inclusion depends on the outcome alone, with positive inclusion probabilities $s_1,s_0$ for $Y=1,0$. Every population joint probability in the first outcome column is multiplied by $s_1$, and every probability in the other column by $s_0$, followed by a common normalization. The multipliers cancel in the cross-product ratio. This explains invariance to that particular outcome-dependent sampling scheme. Arbitrary selection depending jointly on exposure and outcome need not preserve the ratio, and population risks cannot generally be read directly from a case-control table. Pearson correlation is mathematically defined for nonconstant binary indicators; it is often called the phi coefficient. The source's motivation should not be read as a theorem that binary correlation or a least-squares fit is impossible. For binary outcomes, a linear-probability model can be fitted, but it may predict outside $[0,1]$ and has conditional variance $p(1-p)$. Logistic regression supplies a probability-valued mean model with a different likelihood. Multicategory responses require an appropriate extension, not merely declaring a binary model to have more classes. Source: Odds Ratio, 10:26 , and log-odds inference, 22:07 . The sampling and interpretation conditions are made explicit here. 17.2 A conditional probability model and its likelihood Let $Y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$ be independent conditional on predictor vectors $\\mathbf x_i\\in\\mathbb R^q$, including an intercept. Define $\\pi_i=P(Y_i=1\\mid\\mathbf x_i)$. The binary logistic model sets $\\log[\\pi_i/(1-\\pi_i)]=\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta=\\eta_i$. Solving for the probability gives $\\pi_i=\\exp(\\eta_i)/(1+\\exp(\\eta_i))$. This function maps every finite real predictor to a probability strictly between zero and one. A coefficient is a change in log odds per unit change in its design coordinate. Holding all other design coordinates fixed, increasing coordinate $j$ by one multiplies the odds by $\\exp(\\beta_j)$. The corresponding probability change depends on the original probability. Differentiating the logistic mean gives $\\partial\\pi/\\partial x_j=\\pi(1-\\pi)\\beta_j$ when that coordinate enters additively without an interaction. With interactions or transformed coordinates, apply the chain rule to the full linear predictor. The Bernoulli likelihood is $\\prod_i\\pi_i^{y_i}(1-\\pi_i)^{1-y_i}$. Taking logarithms and substituting the logistic relation gives $$\\ell(\\boldsymbol\\beta)=\\sum_i\\{y_i\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta-\\log(1+e^{\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\beta})\\}.\\tag{17.1}$$ For numerical evaluation, use logaddexp(0, eta) for the final logarithm; directly exponentiating a large positive predictor can overflow even when the final probability is well behaved. Differentiating once and twice yields $$\\nabla\\ell=\\mathbf X^T(\\mathbf y-\\boldsymbol\\pi),\\qquad \\nabla^2\\ell=-\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf X,\\quad W_{ii}=\\pi_i(1-\\pi_i).\\tag{17.2}$$ The derivative $d\\pi_i/d\\eta_i=\\pi_i(1-\\pi_i)$ supplies the weights in the Hessian. Because $\\mathbf W$ is positive for finite predictors, the Hessian is negative definite when the design has full column rank. Thus a finite stationary point is a unique maximizer. This conditional statement does not guarantee that a finite maximizer exists. Newton iteration solves an information-weighted normal equation and updates $\\boldsymbol\\beta_{new}=\\boldsymbol\\beta+(\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf X)^{-1}\\mathbf X^T(\\mathbf y-\\boldsymbol\\pi)$. It is also expressible as iteratively reweighted least squares using working response $z_i=\\eta_i+(y_i-\\pi_i)/[\\pi_i(1-\\pi_i)]$. The laboratory implements a direct Newton fit with a likelihood-increasing line search and verifies it against the unpenalized binomial GLM. Finite-difference checks verify the score independently. Under complete separation , a coefficient direction can place all positive outcomes on one side and all negative outcomes on the other. Moving indefinitely along that direction drives the likelihood toward its supremum without reaching a finite coefficient vector. Extremely large coefficients and standard errors may then indicate a structural estimation problem rather than strong evidence for a precise effect. Penalization or specialized bias-reduction methods can yield finite estimates, but they change the estimation procedure and must be identified. Source model: Logistic Regression 1, 03:50 . Python uses statsmodels GLM with an explicitly added intercept, binomial family and logit link, avoiding unintended classification penalties. 17.3 Deviance, coefficient tests, and what a p-value does not say The estimated information matrix $\\mathbf X^T\\widehat{\\mathbf W}\\mathbf X$ gives the usual inverse-information covariance approximation at a finite fitted maximum. Under regular large-sample conditions, a prespecified coefficient's standardized estimate is approximately normal. The Wald statistic for $\\beta_j=0$ is $\\widehat\\beta_j/\\operatorname{SE}(\\widehat\\beta_j)$. This is not an exact finite-sample normal result, and it can perform poorly near separation or with sparse information. The likelihood-ratio statistic for nested models is $2(\\widehat\\ell_F-\\widehat\\ell_R)$, where the subscripts identify full and reduced maximized likelihoods on the same observations. Its chi-square reference with the difference in parameter counts is asymptotic under regularity, including an identified interior null and finite fits. A small improvement or a large p-value does not prove the coefficients are zero. It measures the incompatibility with the tested null through that chosen statistic. Deviance compares a fitted model with a saturated model, which can reproduce each observed response: $D=2(\\ell_{sat}-\\widehat\\ell)$. For independent individual Bernoulli observations, the saturated log likelihood is zero under the convention $0\\log0=0$, so deviance reduces to $-2\\widehat\\ell$. Grouped binomial counts contain a different saturated constant; the general definition should be retained. Null deviance uses the intercept-only fit, while residual deviance uses the specified fitted model. Their difference is the likelihood-ratio improvement, not a new form of training accuracy. A binary regression still has residuals, for example $y_i-\\widehat\\pi_i$, as well as Pearson and deviance residuals. Their existence does not require a Gaussian response. Ordinary linear-model $R^2$ is not the standard likelihood summary here, although several distinct pseudo-$R^2$ quantities can be defined. A model can improve log likelihood while making the same thresholded classifications. Probability estimation and discrete decisions are different evaluation tasks. Source: Logistic Regression 2, 06:05 for output interpretation and 09:25 for deviance. 17.4 The Default example: marginal and conditional comparisons The source uses ISLR's Default dataset. Its package documentation explicitly describes it as simulated, with 10,000 customer records. It is a teaching dataset rather than newly verified operational credit information. The Python conversion preserves all rows and encodes default=Yes as the positive outcome and student=Yes as one, matching R's stated factor references. The balance-only fit has intercept $-10.65133062$ and slope $0.005498916935$. Its probabilities at balances 1,000 and 2,000 are $0.005752145$ and $0.585769370$. These are probabilities from the nonlinear inverse link, not values obtained by multiplying a balance by the slope. A change of one balance unit changes log odds by the slope; the same balance increase produces different absolute probability changes at different starting balances. The student-only coefficient is $0.404887081$, with fitted default probabilities $0.029195011$ for nonstudents and $0.043138587$ for students. After adjusting for balance and income, the student coefficient becomes $-0.646775808$. Those two comparisons condition on different information. A reversal does not by itself prove that becoming a student causally reduces risk. Different covariate distributions, model structure and the noncollapsibility of odds ratios affect marginal and conditional comparisons. Noncollapsibility means that a conditional odds ratio can differ from its marginal counterpart even without confounding. Fig. 17.1. The simulated Default example. The right panel holds income fixed at 30,000 and compares conditional fitted probability curves. It does not show marginal default rates or causal effects. The adjusted model's null deviance is $2920.649711346$, residual deviance $1571.544827579$, and AIC $1579.544827579$. These values reproduce the source models. A fresh R run matches the Python coefficients, probabilities and likelihood summaries to numerical tolerance. The independent Newton fit and likelihood derivative checks verify the mathematical implementation; they do not certify the substantive model for a real lending population. Source: Default implementation, 08:52 , and adjusted model, 22:19 . Data status: ISLR Default documentation . 17.5 Smarket: probability fitting is not a trading result The source's Smarket example uses 1,250 historical observations and models upward direction with five lagged returns and trading volume. The conversion excludes the current response-defining return Today and preserves the specified six predictors. In a real prediction task, every feature's availability time must also be established. A retrospective column is not automatically known when a decision would have been made. The reproduced null deviance is $1731.174769116$, residual deviance $1727.584094203$, and likelihood-ratio improvement $3.590674913$. The asymptotic chi-square p-value for six added coefficients is $0.731869315$. The model provides little evidence of improvement by that particular in-sample test; this is not a proof that every possible forecast is useless. At threshold $0.5$, define Up as positive. A true positive is an actual Up predicted Up; a false positive is an actual Down predicted Up. A false negative is an actual Up predicted Down. The source's spoken false-positive and false-negative descriptions are reversed, so the conversion makes row and column meanings explicit. Table 17.1. Reproduced training confusion matrix. Actual outcome Predicted Down Predicted Up Down 145 457 Up 141 507 Accuracy is $(145+507)/1250=0.5216$ and error rate is $0.4784$. The latter is not accuracy. Always predicting Up gives training accuracy $0.5184$. A training confusion matrix is not evidence of profitable future trading: it excludes execution costs, timing, selection, and changing distributions. The laboratory's separate authored example trains a lag-only model before 2005 and evaluates on 2005; its accuracy is $0.559523810$. This is one historical split, not a current market recommendation. For calibrated outcome probability $p$ and fixed false-positive and false-negative costs $C_{FP},C_{FN}>0$, predicting positive has expected cost $C_{FP}(1-p)$ and negative has expected cost $C_{FN}p$. Choose positive when $p>C_{FP}/(C_{FP}+C_{FN})$. A universal threshold of one-half therefore encodes a particular symmetric-cost decision rule, not a property of logistic regression. Source: Smarket implementation, 01:45 and confusion matrix, 19:46 . Historical dataset description: ISLR Smarket documentation . 17.6 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: odds versus probability. Does multiplying outcome odds by a fixed factor multiply outcome probability by the same factor? Solution No. If original probability is $p$ and odds multiply by $r>0$, the new probability is $rp/(1-p+rp)$. The probability ratio is $r/(1-p+rp)$, which depends on $p$. It approaches $r$ when the relevant probabilities are sufficiently small, but is not generally equal to it. Conceptual: a fitted probability. Why is a least-squares binary fit possible even though logistic regression is often preferable? Solution Least squares can minimize squared error for numerical zero-one responses. Its fitted values need not remain in the probability interval, and Bernoulli variance changes with the conditional mean. Logistic regression directly models a bounded probability and fits a Bernoulli likelihood. The difference concerns model assumptions and behavior, not the mathematical impossibility of drawing or fitting a line. Derivation: the information matrix. Derive the Hessian of the logistic log likelihood and state the condition for strict concavity at finite coefficients. Solution The score is $\\mathbf X^T(\\mathbf y-\\boldsymbol\\pi)$. Differentiating $\\pi_i$ gives $\\pi_i(1-\\pi_i)\\mathbf x_i$, so the Hessian is $-\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf X$. At finite coefficients every diagonal weight is positive. For full column rank and nonzero $\\mathbf u$, $\\mathbf u^T\\mathbf X^T\\mathbf W\\mathbf X\\mathbf u>0$, proving strict concavity. This does not ensure that the likelihood attains a finite maximum under separation. Derivation: the intercept score. What in-sample identity holds at an unpenalized finite maximum when the model includes an intercept? Solution The intercept component of the score is $\\sum_i(y_i-\\widehat\\pi_i)$. Setting it to zero gives equal mean fitted probability and observed positive fraction. The laboratory asserts that identity for Default. It is an in-sample likelihood equation and does not guarantee calibration on new data or inside every subgroup. Computational: the source confusion matrix. Identify false positives and false negatives in Table 17.1 and calculate accuracy and error rate. Solution With Up positive, false positives are the 457 actual Down observations predicted Up; false negatives are the 141 actual Up observations predicted Down. Correct predictions total 652, giving $652/1250=0.5216$. The remaining 598 give error $0.4784$. The Python output and a fresh R table match these counts exactly. Computational: a cross-language conversion. Which settings must remain fixed when translating the Default adjusted model? Solution Preserve all rows, the No/Yes response orientation, the No reference for student, the intercept, raw balance/income units, the binomial likelihood and logit link, and the absence of regularization. If numerical scaling is used internally, transform coefficients back before comparison. Verify likelihood, fitted probabilities and coefficient tolerances, not just classification accuracy. The laboratory does this and independently checks its Newton implementation."}, {"number": 18, "title": "Regression discontinuity and local causal identification", "url": "chapter-18.html", "text": "18.1 A threshold is a design feature, not a causal conclusion A scholarship may become available at a score cutoff, an eligibility rule may begin at a specified age, or a pricing algorithm may change its action when a continuous index crosses a boundary. These settings create an opportunity to compare observations close to the threshold. A visible break in a fitted curve is not enough to establish what caused that break. A running variable is the score or measurement that determines assignment. Denote it by $r\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $c$ be a known cutoff. In a sharp regression-discontinuity design, the treatment indicator is $D=\\mathbf1\\{r\\ge c\\}$. The treatment is the intervention that changes at the boundary; it is not the running variable itself. A causal question must identify the treatment, the outcome, and the population near the boundary. A segmented regression can describe different mean curves on two sides of any chosen point. Regression discontinuity as a causal design additionally requires that the assignment rule and counterfactual comparison be credible. A data analyst cannot create this design merely by searching for the cutoff with the largest fitted jump. The treatment rule must exist, and other determinants of the outcome must satisfy the identifying conditions. The source discusses a minimum-age alcohol-access example and historical ride-pricing thresholds. A primary published study by Carpenter and Dobkin analyzes the age threshold in a specific historical population. Its empirical result is not a claim that every single-day birthday spike measures the same permanent treatment effect. The notes do not infer current driving-license rules or present a historical pricing multiplier as a current platform policy. Source: Regression Discontinuity Design, 03:01 . Historical study: Carpenter and Dobkin, The Effect of Alcohol Consumption on Mortality (2009) . 18.2 The local effect identified by continuity Let $Y(0)$ and $Y(1)$ be a unit's potential outcomes without and with treatment. Consistency means that the observed outcome is $Y=(1-D)Y(0)+DY(1)$. Assume that treatment status follows the sharp cutoff rule and that there are observations arbitrarily close to $c$ on both sides. Define $m_d(r)=E[Y(d)\\mid r]$ for $d=0,1$. Proposition 18.1 (proved here). If both conditional potential-outcome means have continuous limits at $c$, then the right-minus-left limit of the observed conditional mean equals the local treatment effect $\\tau(c)=m_1(c)-m_0(c)$. For $r<c$, sharp assignment gives $D=0$, so $E(Y\\mid r)=m_0(r)$. For $r\\ge c$, it gives $D=1$, so $E(Y\\mid r)=m_1(r)$. Continuity extends the untreated mean from the left to $m_0(c)$ and the treated mean from the right to $m_1(c)$. Subtracting these limits gives $$\\lim_{r\\downarrow c}E(Y\\mid r)-\\lim_{r\\uparrow c}E(Y\\mid r)=m_1(c)-m_0(c)=\\tau(c).\\tag{18.1}$$ Support on both sides makes the observed limits meaningful. Consistency connects observed outcomes with their potential-outcome means. Neither step requires a globally linear response curve. ∎ The effect is local to the cutoff. Extending it to people far from that boundary requires additional assumptions or evidence. The proof also shows its failure mode. If the untreated potential-outcome mean itself jumps at the same cutoff, the observed discontinuity contains that jump as well as the treatment effect. A simultaneous policy change or systematic sorting of different units can therefore invalidate the intended causal interpretation. Whether potential-outcome means would be smooth at the cutoff is not directly testable from the observed outcomes: each side reveals only one treatment state. Substantive knowledge of assignment and measurement is indispensable. Examining score density, predetermined covariate balance, placebo cutoffs and alternative specifications can detect problems, but passing these checks does not prove the unobserved counterfactual condition. Some scores are discrete or heavily rounded, limiting how closely observations approach the boundary and changing the appropriate interpretation of continuity-based approximations. If crossing the cutoff changes treatment probability rather than treatment status deterministically, the sharp proof no longer applies. A treatment-probability discontinuity can support another design under additional assumptions, but one should not interpret the observed outcome jump as the sharp treatment effect automatically. The distinction is part of identifying the intervention before choosing a regression formula. The lecture introduces the cutoff jump at 07:11 . A detailed primary methodological treatment is Cattaneo, Idrobo and Titiunik, A Practical Introduction to Regression Discontinuity Designs: Foundations , sections 2 and 4. 18.3 Estimating the boundary with separate slopes Center the score at the cutoff by writing $x_i=r_i-c$. A piecewise-linear mean specification is $$E(Y_i\\mid r_i)\\approx\\alpha+\\tau D_i+\\gamma x_i+\\delta D_i x_i.\\tag{18.2}$$ On the left, the fitted intercept at the boundary is $\\alpha$ and slope is $\\gamma$. On the right, they are $\\alpha+\\tau$ and $\\gamma+\\delta$. Thus $\\tau$ is the fitted intercept difference at the cutoff. The interaction permits different slopes. Omitting it imposes a common-slope restriction; that is a modeling assumption rather than a consequence of the design. Centering is essential for interpreting the treatment coefficient. If an uncentered score is used with a treatment-score interaction, the coefficient of $D$ is the difference at score zero, which may lie far from the cutoff. At the actual cutoff the fitted difference combines that coefficient with the interaction. A correct transformation of the design leaves the fitted curves unchanged but changes which coefficient directly reports the desired boundary contrast. A local fit uses observations within a bandwidth $h>0$ of the cutoff. A triangular kernel assigns weight $w_i=1-|x_i|/h$ for $|x_i|<h$ and zero otherwise. Weighted least squares on the design columns $(1,D_i,x_i,D_i x_i)$ estimates both boundary intercepts. The kernel weights describe locality; they are not a claim that the observation error variance is the reciprocal of that kernel. A covariance calculation must respect the estimator's actual purpose. The bandwidth trades approximation error against sampling uncertainty. Under smooth conditional means, a Taylor expansion near the boundary contains a constant, a linear term and a remainder of order $x^2$. Local-linear weights reproduce constants and linear terms, leaving a leading bias of order $h^2$ under appropriate nondegenerate design conditions. With score density positive near the cutoff, there are order $nh$ local observations; weights of order $1/(nh)$ give a variance of order $1/(nh)$ under bounded independent error variances. This rate argument suggests an approximate mean squared error $C_bh^4+C_v/(nh)$ with positive constants $C_b,C_v$. Differentiating gives $h^5=C_v/(4C_bn)$, the familiar $n^{-1/5}$ rate. The argument identifies the mechanism; it does not supply a practical optimal bandwidth or a complete asymptotic theorem for arbitrary designs. Vanishing curvature, discrete scores, dependence and other boundary conditions can change the details. A conventional heteroscedasticity-consistent standard error addresses sampling variability of the fitted local regression. It does not by itself correct the local-polynomial bias. At bandwidths chosen for point-estimation accuracy, that bias can be material for confidence intervals. Specialized bias-corrected methods also account for uncertainty introduced by estimating the bias. The laboratory reports conventional HC3 standard errors only as an implementation demonstration, without claiming formal robust bias-corrected regression-discontinuity inference. Source model and different curve shapes: 09:13 and 11:12 . Local-polynomial inference distinctions: the methodological reference above, section 4.3. 18.4 An executable design and a deliberate identification failure The Python laboratory generates 1,200 independent synthetic observations with a known sharp assignment rule at zero. The untreated conditional mean is $1+0.6r+0.4r^2$, the treatment effect is $2+0.3r$, and the disturbance has independent Gaussian standard deviation $0.35$. Both potential-outcome means are continuous, and the true effect at the cutoff is two outcome units. These data are an original teaching construction, not a reconstruction of the source's mortality or pricing records. The triangular local-linear fit with bandwidth $0.5$ uses 279 observations on the left and 305 on the right. It estimates a jump of $1.998572802$, with conventional HC3 standard error $0.067265349$. Bandwidths $0.25$, $0.75$ and $1$ give estimates $2.022091069$, $2.014904482$ and $2.019623690$. Agreement with the known target in this run verifies the computational example; it is not a universal coverage result. Each fit is independently checked against explicit square-root-weighted least squares. Fig. 18.1. Left: a constructed sharp design with effect two at zero. Right: a continuous nonlinear mean. Separate lines fit over an overly broad range can manufacture an apparent boundary difference from curvature. The second construction has conditional mean $1+\\tanh(4r)$, which is continuous at zero. A full-range segmented linear approximation nevertheless reports a jump of $0.714900377$ in this sample; narrowing the bandwidth to $0.25$ gives $0.084937885$. The difference arises from approximation and noise, not a genuine discontinuity in that data-generating mean. This implements the source's warning that curved patterns can be mistaken for a jump. The third construction adds a separate $0.8$ discontinuity to the untreated conditional mean at the same boundary while keeping the treatment effect function unchanged. The fitted jump increases by exactly $0.8$, as asserted by the program. A researcher seeing only observed outcomes could wrongly assign the additional change to treatment. The failure is in identification; choosing a more powerful optimizer cannot repair it. No R code is demonstrated in the complete caption track assigned to this final lecture. Its graphs and empirical stories are used to motivate the design, while the laboratory supplies transparent synthetic inputs. Full source microdata and an independent numerical reproduction of the historical empirical studies are not claimed. Reproducing a graph's appearance would not substitute for recovering its data, assignment rule and uncertainty calculation. 18.5 Exercises with worked solutions Conceptual: a piecewise fit. A researcher searches many cutoffs and finds one with a large fitted response jump. What is still missing for a causal regression-discontinuity interpretation? Solution There must be an actual treatment assignment rule at a known cutoff, a defined intervention, consistency, adequate score support, and credible continuity of the counterfactual outcome means. Searching on the outcome does not create these conditions. The search also changes the statistical inference problem. A fitted discontinuity can describe the sample without identifying a treatment effect. Conceptual: the target population. Does the effect in eq. (18.1) describe everyone who receives treatment? Solution No. It is the effect at the cutoff, defined through conditional potential-outcome means there. People far above the cutoff can have different treatment effects. Extrapolation needs additional structure or evidence. This local estimand can still be relevant for a policy that changes eligibility near the existing boundary. Derivation: centering and the jump. An uncentered model is $a+bD+gr+uDr$. Express the boundary jump at $r=c$ and the equivalent centered parameterization. Solution The right-minus-left fitted difference at $c$ is $b+uc$. Substitute $r=x+c$ to obtain $(a+gc)+(b+uc)D+gx+uDx$. Thus the centered intercept is $a+gc$, the centered treatment coefficient is $b+uc$, and the two slope terms retain $g,u$. Fitted values are unchanged, while the centered treatment coefficient now directly reports the boundary contrast. Derivation: a violated continuity condition. Suppose the untreated mean has a boundary jump $\\zeta$, while the treatment effect is continuous with value $\\tau(c)$. What does the observed jump identify? Solution On the right, the observed mean is the right-limit untreated mean plus $\\tau(c)$. On the left it is the left-limit untreated mean. Subtraction gives $\\zeta+\\tau(c)$. Without information separating the two terms, the observed jump does not identify the treatment effect alone. The laboratory sets $\\zeta=0.8$ and verifies the induced estimator difference. Computational: two representations of a local fit. Verify that the triangular weighted fit agrees with an ordinary least-squares fit to transformed observations. Solution For every retained row, multiply its outcome and all four design entries by $\\sqrt{w_i}$. Ordinary least squares then minimizes the same sum $\\sum_iw_i(y_i-\\mathbf x_i^T\\boldsymbol\\theta)^2$. The laboratory compares both coefficient vectors at every bandwidth and asserts agreement to floating-point tolerance. This checks the computation, not the causal assumptions. Computational design: a bandwidth audit. Explain what to report when estimates change substantially across plausible local bandwidths, and why conventional robust standard errors are not a complete solution. Solution Report bandwidths, retained sample sizes on both sides, polynomial order, kernel, estimates, uncertainty method and relevant diagnostics. Investigate curvature, sparse support, sorting and covariate discontinuities rather than selecting the most favorable result. Conventional heteroscedasticity-robust standard errors address sampling variation around the local approximation; they do not remove approximation bias or establish counterfactual continuity. Use an inference method designed for the RD estimator when making a formal causal claim. The course's methods now serve distinct purposes. Covariance and latent representations describe variation; clusters summarize similarity; regression specifies conditional means; diagnostics examine failure modes; validation evaluates a fitted prediction procedure. A causal design adds assumptions that identify what an intervention changes. The same software output cannot answer all of these questions without those distinctions."}];
